import type { V205BookData } from '../types';

export const DATA: V205BookData = {
  book: "Joshua",
  display: "Joshua",
  chapters: {
    "1": {"chapter":1,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 01"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, the book of Joshua takes it's name from the main protaginist in this particular book \"Joshua.\" In Deuteronomy, while Moses was recounting what God had done for the children of Israel, he was also promoting and encouraging a young man to take his place once Moses passes away. Moses didn't get the chance to enter the promised land with the rest of his people, and so it would be Joshua leading the next generation into this land, and Joshua knows how it will happen, how to split the land up, and what kind of trials and battles he will have to face to do it. Joshua knows, in the end, it will all be worth it."},{"kind":"para","text":"This book of the Bible really fits together with Deuteronomy, and it's worth going back and reading that account before heading into this one. But I will make the assumption that whoever is reading this has not gone back to study through Deuteronomy, and so I will try my best to pull from the various sources and help complete the picture of why Joshua does certain things, and why certain things happen because the Isrealites mess up."},{"kind":"para","text":"Deuteronomy (though labelled the second law) is a book on God's love displayed through God's law. It was not the law that saves us. In fact, God's law condemns us. It was God's grace and mercy that saves us, and His law leads us to His grace. Joshua is a man full of God's grace and mercy, and we will see his character painted right here in this first chapter of Joshua."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":9,"title":"God Encourages Joshua","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Now\" states that we are leading off from the previous statement in the book of Deuteronomy. This indicates that it was Joshua who more than likely wrote the final chapter of Deuteronomy. Joshua was the son of \"Nun.\" Nun was not a man that was talked about very often, other than recognizing that he was a father to many fascinating biblical characters. Joshua was certainly one of them. In numbers, it talks about Nun being the father of Oshea or \"Hosea\" as well."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Of the tribe of Ephraim, Oshea the son of Nun.","ref":"Numbers 13:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"I'm under the impression that Joshua and Oshea were the same person. Joshua's name seems to constantly be followed by \"son of Nun.\" Trying to trace the history of Nun is not an easy task either, considering that we are limited by scriptural references."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Rephah was his son, also Resheph, and Telah his son, and Tahan his son, Laadan his son, Ammihud his son, Elishama his son, Non his son, Jehoshua his son.","ref":"1 Chronicles 7:25-27"},{"kind":"para","text":"Now we have another name \"Elishama.\" Elishama is mentioned in Numbers."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Of the children of Joseph: of Ephraim; Elishama the son of Ammihud: of Manasseh; Gamaliel the son of Pedahzur.","ref":"Numbers 1:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"It seems (then) that Joshua had a direct lineage from Joseph's branch in Israel. I find this interesting, seeing as Joseph was the man that was sold into slavery in Egypt, and then made Egypt a more prosperous land during a great and terrible famine. This new Pharaoh of Egypt didn't know Joseph, and so he became oppressive to the Israelites in the land, which is what ultimately led them out of the land."},{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua coming from this line was no accident. It says in this verse that Joshua was \"Moses' minister...\" but I don't think Moses really chose Joshua. I think Moses took a survey of the land and found plenty of capable men, and Joshua was not one of them. However, GOD chose Joshua, and told Moses to take him under his wing, teaching him what to do next. Joshua probably didn't think too highly of himself, either (as we will see in a moment), but God knew what he was capable of doing and what he would eventually do for his people. Listen to what God tells Joshua to do next now that Moses has passed and it's time for the next steps."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The land was given to them by the Lord. They had the capability and the power to take all of it. There was supposedly about 300,000 miles of land for them to have for themselves and take possession of. Joshua would lead them to take about 30,000 miles of it, and sadly that is where it stops. No one really takes the mantle after Joshua. Perhaps the Lord wanted Israel to be well off enough to make the decision on their own without a leader guiding them (other than God Himself leading them). Or maybe God just wanted them to see what their full potential could be if they would just look to Him and trust in His providence. Whatever the case may be, the book of Judges displays that God's people always find a way to hesitate about what the Lord is providing them."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are all guilty of it. We try and make excuses not to do it, or we try and find another way around that might be easier for us to take (and if it's not easy, we will write it off as if God didn't really mean to put us into that position to begin with). People who are wholly yielded to the Lord are a very valuable commodity today. It was valuable back then as well, for there weren't many who were willing to do whatever the Lord asked of them. But God is reminding Joshua here about what Moses had promised (through God's Word, mind you), and what his overarching leadership goal would be."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that God was encouraging Joshua. All the great men of faith throughout history were encouraged by the Lord. This is a key thought to remember for every person reading this today... There are far more discouragers in the world than there are encouragers. In fact, the church is FILLED with discouragers. There are many people who are quick to write off one man's ideas for the Lord (usually because it's something old school, or something different). Sometimes you will find ONE who will encourage you in your work for God."},{"kind":"para","text":"When I was at Lancaster Baptist, I wanted to learn about how to open-air preach. I went to my teacher at the time and asked him if we could start something like this in our class. \"That kind of thing doesn't work today,\" he said. “Well, how do you know?\" I asked. \"Have you tried it for yourselves?\" \"No,\" he said, \"I just know people don't take kindly to others yelling about people going to hell.\" I think he had the wrong idea about what I was conveying. I said, \"So if I started open-air preaching today, could I invite some people from class to do it?\" \"No,\" he said, \"you would be wasting their time.\" I ended the conversation with that. Later on, I met a man in the class who was going to the train station and open-air preaching. I met up with him and started going to the train station every Saturday, handing out tracts and taking the opportunity to talk one-on-one with people about Scripture. There were several times when people would start to gather around because the conversation was interesting to them, and everyone would hear the gospel. I guarantee this man was discouraged from his attempts to get help from the church hundreds of times over, and yet he kept at it. I might have been the ONLY encourager to him, and he was reaching more with the gospel than most of the church was door-knocking."},{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua was being encouraged directly from the Lord here, and it is sad that he had to be encouraged at all. The reason is because most of Israel probably didn't believe in him. God knew what he would do though, and so God raised him up."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, being encouraged from other believers is one thing, and it's a welcomed ministry (a lacking one at that). But being encouraged from the Lord's mouth directly is far greater than an encouragement you can get from others. What a great symbol of God's power and comfort."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For God hath not given us the spirit of fear; but of power, and of love, and of a sound mind.","ref":"2 Timothy 1:7"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I will lift up mine eyes unto the hills, from whence cometh my help.","ref":"Psalm 121:1"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Cast thy burden upon the LORD, and he shall sustain thee: he shall never suffer the righteous to be moved.","ref":"Psalm 55:22"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"These things I have spoken unto you, that in me ye might have peace. In the world ye shall have tribulation: but be of good cheer; I have overcome the world.","ref":"John 16:33"},{"kind":"para","text":"God should be the source of a Christians encouragement. When you are feeling discouraged about a matter, go to God's Word to see if He might encourage you in it."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua had the Pentateuch for encouragement. He could remember God's law and love for His people. He had a written account of God's promises. It wasn't just word of mouth about what Moses said, or this person might have heard from so-and-so. No, there was written and signed documentation that God was going to be with His children through this journey, and that God had made promises He was GOING to keep for His children if they would just let Him."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God is not just suggesting to Joshua to be encouraged. God is COMMANDING Joshua to be encouraged. He is saying, \"TRUST me, I have never let you or your ancestors down. You MUST take me at my Word.\" Joshua has a choice to make here. Trust that God will keep his promises, or let his own mind sway him into scientific logic and theory that will keep him from being used in mightier ways. Of course, science and faith walk strongly together, but there are some things that cannot be explained. Some miracles that just take place. Some people who are just simply used more. Some situations that have no logical structure to get around. Joshua was all of these faith practices, and he would have to trust what God had done, so that he could rest assured that God can do more."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":11,"title":"The Son of Fish","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There was some part in God's encouragement to Joshua that worked because he is seen taking command now. He was uncertain of himself, and maybe a little undone with the leading of Moses, but he is confident that God will keep him if he just obeys. He saw it happen to Moses, and he knows that Moses hadn't lied to him about any of it before. He surely saw miracles happen even to his own family (whoever this Nun character may have been was probably well known, whether for his strength and valor or for his weakness and shame). \"Nun\" means “fish.\" By the fact that fish is a seemingly little thing to the world (and the fact that naming your child \"fish\" is a bit strange), my guess is that Nun was not a man that seemed like much. He probably seemed like a frail man. Yet it's with a fish that God gets His men to cooperate;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now the LORD had prepared a great fish to swallow up Jonah. And Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights.","ref":"Jonah 1:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"It's with fish that God feeds the multitude;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he commanded the multitude to sit down on the grass, and took the five loaves, and the two fishes, and looking up to heaven, he blessed, and brake, and gave the loaves to his disciples, and the disciples to the multitude.","ref":"Matthew 14:19"},{"kind":"para","text":"Twice!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he took the seven loaves and the fishes, and gave thanks, and brake them, and gave to his disciples, and the disciples to the multitude.","ref":"Matthew 15:36"},{"kind":"para","text":"It was what He used to get His disciples attention;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now when he had left speaking, he said unto Simon, Launch out into the deep, and let down your nets for a draught. And Simon answering said unto him, Master, we have toiled all the night, and have taken nothing: nevertheless at thy word I will let down the net. And when they had this done, they inclosed a great multitude of fishes: and their net brake. And they beckoned unto their partners, which were in the other ship, that they should come and help them. And they came, and filled both the ships, so that they began to sink. When Simon Peter saw it, he fell down at Jesus' knees, saying, Depart from me; for I am a sinful man, O Lord. For he was astonished, and all that were with him, at the draught of the fishes which they had taken: And so was also James, and John, the sons of Zebedee, which were partners with Simon.","ref":"Luke 5:4-10a"},{"kind":"para","text":"It was with fish that He commenced the Great Commission;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he saith unto them, Follow me, and I will make you fishers of men.","ref":"Matthew 4:19"},{"kind":"para","text":"Fish is a small thing to the world, but a big thing to the Lord, and God uses these insignificant creatures to display His providence and strength."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Notwithstanding, lest we should offend them, go thou to the sea, and cast an hook, and take up the fish that first cometh up; and when thou hast opened his mouth, thou shalt find a piece of money: that take, and give unto them for me and thee.","ref":"Matthew 17:27"}]}]},{"start":12,"end":18,"title":"No Rest Until We All Rest","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These two and a half tribes had already made a deal with Moses about what they were going to do instead of entering the promised land with Joshua and the rest of Israel. They didn't want to go over into the land because there was land they had already found that was good for their cattle (even though God is promising them something better). My assumption is that they didn't want to chance losing their current wealth of cattle in case what Moses and Joshua were getting at were not really going to work out. They asked Moses if they could hold onto this land over here and \"...bring us not over Jordan.\" (Numbers 32:1-5) They have no idea what they are doing..."},{"kind":"para","text":"This resembles us all too well today. We think that if we follow God, we will lose everything we have because there is no security. The truth is, you might end up losing it all, but God will always replace what you give up for Him with something far better. Even as I'm writing this, I have to be reminded of this... God has always given me something better to do with my time when I stop wasting it on things I think are worthwhile. He always gives me something better if I would just choose to wait on Him and follow His lead. When I give up what I think is best, God always places HIS best into my hands. What is better than God's best?"}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Just because they already had land for themselves does not give them the right to neglect their brothers and sisters taking land for themselves. They still had to help and put in the work if they wanted favor from the Lord for that land. Honestly, it would have just been better for them to have gone into the land with the rest of Israel, seeing as they have to put in the effort anyway. They could simply rest right where they left off, instead of having to travel back once the fighting is over (if it ever ends). Still, today, the fight for land has not ended..."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They agreed to Joshua, and they agreed to him on one occasion, and that was \"...only the Lord thy God be with thee, as he was with Moses...\" They didn't want to go with Joshua if God was not with him. Of course, they knew that God was with Joshua because he was following the Lord's command and he was holding Moses' law in high esteem. When we get to the book of Judges, we find that these two and a half tribes should not have stayed on that side of the Jordan. There is a lot of spiritual significance to crossing that river (which I will get into more in the next chapter). These tribes were hoping for rest without being in God's promises, and the truth is, we will never find it anywhere else than in the Lord's guidance and leadership."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we accept His hope and salvation, then (and only then) do we enter into His rest. It requires us to cross the Jordan, however, and that can't happen unless we look to the cross of Christ."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":18}]},
    "2": {"chapter":2,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 02"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our first chapter, we see that Joshua is being encouraged by the Lord to take the leadership of the Israelites so they are prepared to take the land that God promised them. Joshua doesn't feel like God chose the right person, and God makes it clear that He did not make a mistake by calling Joshua out. It seems to work because the people of Israel are 100% behind Joshua (at least thats what they say for now)."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Joshua sends two spies to see about this land. The spies are met with an unlikely ally, and they are protected (By God's soveriegn plan) so that they can live to see the land prosper!"}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"Spies Sent","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When the sentence starts with an “and,\" that means it’s continuing off from the previous thought. This story is a progression from our last chapter, so this is all kind of happening at once."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And they answered Joshua, saying, All that thou commandest us we will do, and whithersoever thou sendest us, we will go. According as we hearkened unto Moses in all things, so will we hearken unto thee: only the LORD thy God be with thee, as he was with Moses. Whosoever he be that doth rebel against thy commandment, and will not hearken unto thy words in all that thou commandest him, he shall be put to death: only be strong and of a good courage.","ref":"Joshua 1:16-18"},{"kind":"para","text":"So the Israelites have made it a priority to follow what Joshua was telling them to do. So here's Joshua's first order of service: he wants to send spies to the land to see what they are dealing with before they enter in. Much like the angels during the days of Lot, they go into the city to see what the fuss is, and they are accepted into a house from a resident there. Rahab (though she's a harlot) seems to think that these men are not going to be very welcomed in. \"Harlot\" means \"to commit adultery\" or \"commit fornication\" or \"to be unfaithful (to God).\" In this sense, she was probably a prostitute (more than likely), and it was probably the safest job for her in this city she was living in. The people that she was around were rapists, murderers, and idolaters of the worst kinds. She likely didn't enjoy her job as a harlot, but it was the only way she could live in the land without being abused. She welcomes the Israelite spies in and gives them a place to lay low."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems they didn't do a very good job at hiding themselves. The locals probably saw them and said, \"Have we seen these men before? I don't recognize their faces. They don't look like any of us.\" It seemed pretty obvious to the people around them that they didn't belong. That is how it is with those who are chosen by the Lord. We recognizably don't belong in many areas. The crazy thing is, it was THESE people who didn't belong in the land they were in."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, we have established that one of the laws of God was \"thou shalt not bear false witness against thy neighbour.\" Rahab wasn't an Israelite, but we all know that breaking God's law is wrong (no matter what land you might come from). She knew that lying (specifically) would be wrong. Does that mean what Rahab was doing here was a sin? We have a lot of people who would probably tell you it is. In some fashion, it could be looked at this way, but I don't believe what she was doing here was a sin."},{"kind":"para","text":"Take it further into the days of Nazi Germany, and there were Christian Germans holding Jewish people in secret places in their homes. Nazis knock on their door and ask, \"Are you harboring any Jews in your home?\" If the Christian Germans say, \"No, we don't have any here,\" and the Nazis search the home to find nothing, they are not going against the desire of God because they are saving LIVES. That is what God is all about! He loves to SAVE people. Now, let’s flip it here and say they come to their door and ask, and the Christian Germans say, \"Yes, they are under the floorboards\" and lead them straight to the protected Jews, we would be disgusted by those so-called \"Christians\" who just gave up lives without a second thought."},{"kind":"para","text":"Bring it back to Rahab. Imagine if Rahab said, \"Oh yes, I have them hiding in this secret area, let me show you where it is!\" We would be looking at a very different story coming up here. Rahab, for some reason or another, wanted to protect the Israelites. Perhaps she knew what was going to take place. Maybe she just wanted to give these strangers a chance. Whatever the case is, she made the right decision here as we will see coming up..."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This doesn't seem like Rahab’s first rodeo. She might have hidden men up on her roof before. She may have cared for people who came into town and desired to give them a chance to survive. I don't know, but it was a smart spot to hide them because the men of Jericho didn't even think of looking in there. By doing this, Rahab was putting her own life on the line, and she will answer why she has done this in the next verses..."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":14,"title":"Rahab's Deal","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Think about this for a second. The Israelites were wandering in the desert for forty years. The reason for that was because they saw the people of the land, and they were afraid to take it due to the size of the people (they were giants). Because they didn't listen to God, they forced themselves into the desert life. When the Israelites came to survey the land the first time, the GIANTS during that time were already scared of what they would do. They heard about what God had done for the Israelites and they knew that they would be wiped out. I don't think they would have even put up much of a fight because they knew that they would lose based off of ONLY the stories that they heard. The Israelites literally SAW what God can do and they didn't believe. The people of Jericho had more faith than the Israelites about God's strength. It seems they have been waiting for this moment for a while, and they knew it was going to happen either way. The men that were searching for the spies just a minute ago were gearing up for war. They did NOT want the Israelites to come in. Rahab (however) knew that the Israelites owned this land, they deserved it! She, likely, wanted to see change come, and she wanted to see their God prosper in this land. She was, perhaps, hoping to get out of the prostitution business."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Rahab didn't trust these men to swear by their own names. She wanted them to swear by their God's name!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For when God made promise to Abraham, because he could swear by no greater, he sware by himself,","ref":"Hebrews 6:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"It would be extremely unwise to swear by another name, and it would be even more unwise to swear by the Lord's name and not mean it. She trusted in their God. She knew that He COULD be trusted just based off of the stories that she had heard. It was not by accident that they ended up in her home. I think she knew that, and so she uses it as an opportunity to rescue her family as well. The Israelites owed her that much for putting her own life on the line for them just now."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It wouldn't take much for her to call out the door and say, \"The men are right here! Come get them!\" if they were not willing to make this offer. They were ALSO wise to accept this offer and give her freedom in this land. This goes to further prove that God wants all people everywhere to be saved. God knew that this lady and her family needed saving from their sins and from their environment. The spies had no idea that they would be walking about this lady's house, but it was not lost on God. He guided their footsteps and provided a means."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":21,"title":"A Scarlet Thread","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Better than anything yet, her house was built into the outer wall of the city! They had even EASIER access to the city and to escape. Of course, we know that the wall has to come down, and that would include the houses that were built into that wall as well."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They didn't want to forget which house was hers. Scarlet was an interesting color choice."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Ye daughters of Israel, weep over Saul, who clothed you in scarlet, with other delights, who put on ornaments of gold upon your apparel.","ref":"2 Samuel 1:24"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And they stripped him, and put on him a scarlet robe.","ref":"Matthew 27:28"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Come now, and let us reason together, saith the LORD: though your sins be as scarlet, they shall be as white as snow; though they be red like crimson, they shall be as wool.","ref":"Isaiah 1:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"Aside from the fact that scarlet was used in their priestly garbs, scarlet symbolizes quite a bit to the saved. To the Israelites, it symbolized protection (as the blood of Christ also symbolizes for us). To Rahab, it symbolized salvation (as the blood of Christ also symbolizes for us). To those that knew Rahab, it symbolized separation (as it does to the saved today who place their trust in Christ). To the outside world, it symbolized nothing (as does our faith to those that choose to reject the Lord today). That scarlet thread is the same one that connects all of Scripture together until the day the beast uses it to symbolize himself. At that point, it will be too late."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That was the safe spot. It is quite likely that Rahab’s house would be the only one standing when the Israelites come to take the land. That will be another symbol of God's provident protection, seeing as it will not be the Israelites that will take down the wall themselves (we will look at that later)."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They had to do some trusting on her part as well. They had to trust that she wouldn't tell the men where they were (at least for three days). They would have to sit and wonder if she was keeping her end of the deal. She (immediately) puts the thread in the window as a symbol of assurance to the Israelites who were about to walk away. That (likely) made them feel better about her word when they saw that scarlet thread."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":24,"title":"It's Time!","entries":[{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The first time they went in, they should have been confident that God was giving them the land. This second time, the faith of a NON-Israelite increased their faith. They were certain (based on the testimony of Rahab) that God was going to provide for them. They just had to go and take it. It leads us to question if Joshua was right in sending spies into the land to begin with."},{"kind":"para","text":"God has made it clear over and over again (even through the book of Deuteronomy) that the Israelites were to get the land. They just had to TAKE it. Yet they are waiting again for the spies, and they want to make sure the land was right for them. I believe Joshua did this, not because he was worried about whether or not they were strong enough to take the land. The spies seem to assume this is the reason. No, I think Joshua did this to get a battle plan together. These people in this city were not just going to give their land and homes over to Israel. They would need to fight for it!"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":24}]},
    "3": {"chapter":3,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 03"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see spies sent out from Israel to go and scope out this land. They are taken in by a kind harlot named Rahab and are given a method of escape before they are caught by the people and possibly killed for tresspassing in this land. Because of this, Rahab was given salvation from the coming war when Israel takes over. She just had to put up a \"scarlet\" thread in her window so they knew which house to avoid taking over."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see the spies come back to talk to the people of Israel and prepare them for the new land. They are ready to follow the Lord by faith into the unknown to receive the promise that God has made for them. This chapter has been anticipated since the beginning of scripture, and it's one that Moses wrote about in the first five books of the Bible. We finally get to see the vision that Moses was given by the Lord come to fruition, and it's a great journey onto the shores of this Promised Land."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":6,"title":"Prepare For The Land","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here they are! There has been so much waiting and build-up from Exodus to now, and they are finally on the verge of fulfilling the promise of God. They are about to move by faith into the land that God told them they would possess. This is quite a big deal for world history, as well as Israel history. This move will impact their entire future even still today. They are finally moving by faith into what God has called them to move into by faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"God still moves His children this way today. When we don't follow through with following God where He has called us, we always experience great loss. Most of Israel experienced great loss when they didn't listen to the Lord to follow Him. Forty years of wandering. The death of an entire generation. The inability for their leader to carry them into the land. Now we are here. Last three nights out in the wilderness, and they are ready to go where they were told."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is an incredible image. God's Ark was a symbol of God's presence. They were not just going into the Promised Land by force or by command. They were following the Lord there. That is what they had been doing all this time, whether they realized (wanted to believe it) or not, God was their focal point, and where He goes, we should all want to follow. Sometimes that looks scary and uncertain. I could argue to say that MOST times where the Lord leads is scary and uncertain. I don't know many times when we can follow the Lord into something easy and simple. God wants to take us through some difficulty, not because He loves watching us struggle, but rather He loves our faith to be full."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Better than just following God where He leads, God creates the path in which we must go. We must trust His path, because He knows it best. We have never gone there, but God knows exactly what He is doing. We should give God room to lead us; otherwise, we might turn back and stop following after Him, or we might get too far ahead, and God will not have a way to help."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here's a reminder to God's children. \"Sanctify\" yourself! \"Sanctify\" means \"to be set apart.\" Joshua wants the people to separate themselves for the Lord. Keep themselves from worldly things, or things that would distract them from the real cause. We should be looking forward to what God will do tomorrow."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sometimes we are so caught up on today that we don't take the time to \"sanctify\" ourselves. We don't anticipate what the Lord can do for us tomorrow. It’s usually tomorrow that we get to look forward to, and yet it's something we rarely think about. I know we have heard it said, \"Tomorrow is the day that never comes.\" or \"Why wait for tomorrow when it's not promised?\"vYou are right, but it's also something that God wants us to prepare for. We should be concerned with our current events, not because we should \"live in the moment\" alone, but rather we should be concerned with current events so we can prepare for what God wants to do with us next. God often uses our moments today to do what He can tomorrow. If we have robbed Him of glory today, tomorrow will not look very exciting..."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here's the moment! God is leading them in now."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":13,"title":"The Gameplan To Cross","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua will be magnified, not so he can receive glory. When God uses a man, it is not so that he can be greater than the rest. We know there are a lot of people in the church today who think they are the \"set apart\" ones. Joshua just told the people (all of them) to be set apart. He told them ALL to be \"sanctified.\" It was not just Joshua today who would be \"set apart.\" It would be the whole nation. The difference here, however, is that Joshua was their leader. God positioned Joshua to have influence on these people so that they had direction. Not direction from Joshua, but direction from the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"What is the leader’s job then? Is it to command the people and force them to follow your rule? Absolutely not. Every great leader of the Lord treated himself as equal with the people. Great leaders are not hard to reach, yet they are never swayed by society. They are only led by Godly influence. The role of the leader is not to parade their power over people. The role of the leader is to point his people back to where the power comes from. We should only do things (as leaders) that point people back to the Lord. That was Joshua’s role here. We could argue that it was the role of the Levites to do this specific job, but God knew that even the Levites would not maintain this image. They needed a leader, and they needed someone to point them back to God. This is proven after Joshua dies and they have no leader. They fall into the traps of the world and they have no vision."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Where there is no vision, the people perish: but he that keepeth the law, happy is he.","ref":"Proverbs 29:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"People need leaders who can constantly point them back to God because people will constantly forget what they are supposed to be doing."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Be not thou therefore ashamed of the testimony of our Lord, nor of me his prisoner: but be thou partaker of the afflictions of the gospel according to the power of God;","ref":"2 Timothy 1:8"}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua was prepared to listen to the Lord no matter what the Lord says, or how the Lord leads. He is ready and willing, as a good, obedient child to a father. God wants all of His children to meet up on the shores of the promised land together. Revelation tells us there will be a \"new\" Heaven in eternity."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And I saw a new heaven and a new earth: for the first heaven and the first earth were passed away; and there was no more sea.","ref":"Revelation 21:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"There are a lot of interpretations of what that could mean, but whatever you might think, we know that God's children will meet up on the shore of this new Heaven to all witness and go in together. We will not be separated in eternity from our brothers and sisters in the Lord. We will be experiencing all of eternity in unity as a collective group. We will follow the Lord into this old, dusty world, to see the final battle that He has prepared, and we will all wait and anticipate (together) the new land."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That blessed number 7 shows up here. Seven is God's perfect number. There are seven tribes here (and I don't want to play any emphasis on stereotypical rumors or conspiracies about God's Word, and so I won't). Seven tribes against this young tribe of Israel. There is no way Israel could win on its own. There's no way they could fight against seven different people groups and win. It would be downright impossible for them to do something like this. But, here they are. They are ready and willing. And God will drive these people out on their behalf. They have one plan, and one plan only. Trust that the Lord will provide the miracle for them, and simply obey His leading."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Just as Moses split the water, Joshua is anticipating the same amazing experience as they go across the water to stand on the shores of this new land. Here's the amazing part though: The faith that drives them across the waters will have to lead them through this entire new land. They cannot turn back once they go across unless they want to chance swimming. There was nowhere for them to turn to. Going across this way means they will have to stay on the other side or be separated from their own people, and certain death to follow because they are not under the guidance of the Lord."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":17,"title":"The New Land","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The people on the other side of the Jordan probably thought that they had a lot of time before the Israelites got to where they were going. They didn't think they would have to prepare too early on. The rains at this time were probably abundant at the Jordan River, and it seems as though the waters were rushing quite a lot. Enough for them to have to have a break in the water. Yet, again, here they are. They have seen quite the miracle take place here."},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe this miracle was even greater than the miracle in which Moses saw in Exodus when crossing the Red Sea. Some people don't believe Moses would have too much trouble crossing the sea. They would say, “Well, Moses crossed the sea because that sea doesn't go very deep. Even today, you could probably walk across it without much of an issue.\" There are several things I could say about that. But how could they explain away this scene here with Joshua? It gives a time frame, it gives weather conditions, and it gives a miracle. There is no argument as to what is being talked about here in scripture as being anything less than a miracle."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Not a single person got wet on this trek. They ALL passed over the Jordan, and the Lord made sure they were all clean and ready to take on their next task. If this miracle didn't build their faith for their next task, nothing ever will. It seems (however) that all of Israel was ready to follow the Lord into their move. They were excited. How could they not be, simply based on everything that Moses promised them? Now they are experiencing the moving of the Lord themselves. They, perhaps, didn't see what God had done for their fathers in the wilderness. Maybe they only heard stories of the glory of God. It's been forty years and they were not fully aware of what God could do. But now they have seen it, and now they have no excuse for the next part of their moving."},{"kind":"para","text":"I find it fascinating that it says the waters stopped flowing all the way back to the city of \"Adam.\" I can't seem to locate where \"Adam\" is, but it's a great representation of our faith walks today. We come from the city of Adam before Christ. Christ gives us access to the promised land, and He is standing halfway through the rushing waters of sin, so that we can come through it without being swallowed up by it. He leads us through our sin into a new land. A new covenant. A new life. And we don't have to wander any longer. Sin can stay in the city of Adam for all I care. I want to go to the new land."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":17}]},
    "4": {"chapter":4,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 04"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, the Israelites were setting up a gameplan to come over to this promised land. They have received the direction of the Lord to cross the Jordan and to take their first steps on this new land. They are preparing the people, as well as the way, and are getting ready to follow through with what the Lord has commanded."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, they start their trek across the water and leave an important lesson as they go. We finally get to see the Israelites enter into the land they have been anticipating for generations now."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"Leave a Testimony","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If you don't remember in Genesis, Abraham did this quite a bit wherever he was going. He would take stones and stack them on top of each other as a \"memorial.\" Think of it as a \"testimony.\" Abraham left testimonies of remembering what God did in that place, and anyone that passed by that \"testimony\" would know that God did a work there. That is what God is doing here with his children as they step foot on their new land. He is telling them to take twelve stones (representing the twelve tribes, of course) and stack them as a testimony. Anyone that walked by would know it was not the Israelites that did this amazing thing. It was the Lord that did it."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These stones were a testimony that God got them safely across the water where He needed them to be. When we attended Lancaster Baptist Church, they had a stone signing event. At first, I found it foreign and strange. They gave each person at the event a small stone to write their names (and possibly a quick message) on. They took these stones and put them in the walls of a new building they were putting together for the children. I think some of them are still out for display, but the majority of them are a testament inside the walls. If the walls are ever broken open in the distant future, they will probably be confused as to all the messages that are written on them."},{"kind":"para","text":"We might not be using stones today to leave a testimony, but we should leave a testimony in some way wherever we go. When we visit our unsaved friend’s house, leave a testimony. If we see them come to know the Lord, leave a testimony. If we visit a park, leave a testimony. If we go out to eat with friends, leave a testimony. However that may look, it is wise to leave something behind stating that the Lord has done something there for you. We should be walking testimonies if we belong to the Lord, for we are built as a spiritual house."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"To whom coming, as unto a living stone, disallowed indeed of men, but chosen of God, and precious, Ye also, as lively stones, are built up a spiritual house, an holy priesthood, to offer up spiritual sacrifices, acceptable to God by Jesus Christ.","ref":"1 Peter 2:4-5"},{"kind":"para","text":"These twelve stones that were chosen by these men were precious for the sake of the future testimony."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":14,"title":"Israel Arrives","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The men may not have quite understood the purpose of this, and I don't know if there ever is going to be a time in the future when anyone will ever ask what those stones are there for, but it was something that these people remembered, and it would be something that they would likely tell their children about. These stones were probably decent-sized, and they probably put in quite a bit of work to fish them out and bring them back. But it was worth it for the memorial and for the sake of obeying the Lord's commands. Sadly (as we will see in the book of Judges), the children of Israel will forget this testimony…"}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He places them at the feet of the ark, because that is where the Lord proved his presence. They will also place stones on the shoreline because the Lord provided to get them there, but they leave it in the river because that was the proof of God's safety and salvation for them. They didn't get wet at all. They weren't consumed by the water. They made it \"clean\" over onto dry land the whole way through."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There is great significance to this in our lives today. The stones in the water would likely never be seen unless the Jordan dried up. Those stones symbolized God's miraculous safety across the river where they were leaving their old life behind and starting a new one. The stones on the promised land shore were a symbol of their new lives starting. The testimony of a born-again man begins in the middle of transformation. The conception of the new birth happens the moment we realize we are sinners in need of a Savior. That is when the testimony for the Lord begins. That is when He does His first works in us."},{"kind":"para","text":"God had been doing a work for the Israelites for a very long time, but they were consistently rejecting His work every step of the way. In this account, they are fully obedient and prepared to follow the Lord where they needed to be. They understood that God was guiding, and the proof was right before their eyes. There would be no reason to question or doubt the Lord's obvious providence."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They had 40,000 armed and trained men ready for battle if the time calls for it. They probably didn't even think they would get this far without some sort of conflict, but here they are. They are in enemy territory. But the land doesn't belong to the enemy, and the enemy knew it..."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God greatly used Joshua. They weren't \"afraid\" of Joshua, they were in awe of his leadership and the fact that it was obviously evident that God was with him. There are just some people that you might meet who have the evidential power of God in them. They are respected people. Some have the power of God in them and they are far too high for the humble man to reach. I feel as if that is not the way in which God's power is to be displayed. God's power is to be reached by all, and if there is a minister in his office who is too powerful for the lessers to reach and know, then they are abusing their God-given powers. A preacher, or pastor, should be reachable. Joshua was feared, but he was reachable. He might have been powerful, but he was WITH his people. He wasn't out of arm’s reach."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":18,"title":"They Finish Their Crossing","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So the picture is, the ark and the priests go into the center of the water, and the rest of Israel follow. The waters are ceased for a time so that they can cross over without getting wet. When the ark is about halfway through, the priests stop, the men set up stones in the middle of the river and on the shoreline, and the Israelites pass all the way through to the other side of the water. The priests and the ark finish passing through once all of Israel has passed, and as soon as the ark gets onto the shore of the new land, the waters start back up again and cover the areas that they scientifically should be covering (gravity and pressure and what have you). They are all on this new land and in anticipation for battle, and they all respect and honor Joshua for his great leadership thus far (and apparently until his death)."}]}]},{"start":19,"end":24,"title":"The Gospel Proof","entries":[{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They are finishing their testimony of this miracle by placing these twelve stones on the shoreline. The only conclusion we can draw from this testimony is obviously the good news that the Lord provides salvation for His children. That is the gospel message throughout all of Scripture. It was the testimony that was being placed on the shore during the days of Joshua, and it is the testimony placed on the shores of our hearts today."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The testimony was there as PROOF that God can do the impossible. It was PROOF that God was a provider. It was PROOF that God loved His creation and desired to be with them and lead them into His promises. It was PROOF that God would do whatever it took to save those that would be willing. It's PROOF that God's love is boundless, and digs deeper than most men are willing to allow. It's PROOF that God has a burden for His people and that He wants to protect and rescue them out of where they are, to where they need to be. It's PROOF that God wants to change our lives for something far greater."},{"kind":"para","text":"God wants to use our transformation from the old land to the new as a means of testimony for the impossible, and He wants to do it through US of all people. He wants US to know Him and to make Him known. That is what Joshua proves here in this portion of scripture, and it's what all the prophets (major and minor), and Jesus Christ will make obvious when they speak about the Lord. It will be what will be obvious in the lives of the disciples and in the last days as well. God has geared the world for this cause, and He hasn't let it down since the beginning of time and it will be all that matters when we come to the end of ourselves…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":24}]},
    "5": {"chapter":5,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 05"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see the children of Israel leaving a testimony where the Lord was leading by placing stones as reminders of what God had done for them in those places. One pile was placed in the middle of the river. The other pile is placed on the shoreline. They take great comfort and solace tha they had arrived on the otherside of the river and set up camp. The set an example of proof that God rescued them, gave them a promise, kept (and is still keeping) His promise, and has given them all new life in this new land."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see that they are found out quite quickly to have arrived, and they take some time of rest before pursuing forward with the Lord's gift of providence. They also meet an unexpected guest, leaving us on an undesirable cliffhanger..."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":1,"title":"News Spreads Quickly","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So it's assumed that these people knew this day was coming. The Canaanites specifically (more than likely) understood what was going to eventually happen (I assume Noah's children passed this information down to future generations). Even if the Canaanites didn't speak much of it, the other tribes were sure to know the story and pass that information on. Either way, they knew that the Israelites had made it over. Someone went and told them the news, and now they are all faint from the obvious God-given provision."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is a good reminder to the reader, by the way. When God does something, news about His doing will spread quickly. Did this stop the Canaanites from trying to keep the Israelites out of the land? Of course not! Did this turn the hearts of the Canaanites to the Lord so there wouldn't be a battle? No, as we will soon see, these people fought for their territory even though they knew it wasn’t theirs. They didn't redeem it. They weren't promised it. They weren't the ones walking in the lineage of Abraham. It was promised to the Israelites and it will come to fruition in such a case."}]}]},{"start":2,"end":9,"title":"A Time of Rest","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We speak about the importance of circumcision in Genesis with Abraham and his clan getting circumcised. This is an image of the fact that they are separated from the rest of the world. They are set apart to the LORD. Those that were not set apart wouldn't even dare to circumcise themselves."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So it seems those that were in Egypt took the right of circumcision quite seriously. But those that were born in the wilderness after they had left Egypt did not have the chance or motive to be circumcised. This image is displayed later on in scripture as well (used as an example of being cut off from sin). This seemed like a very strange time to do this, but the Lord had requested it here and now."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I'm sorry if this is not meant to be humorous (I don't know if it's intended to be funny or not), but I can't help but laugh at this idea. The circumcision was a serious symbol to the children of Israel, displaying the right for God to lead them into this promise and this new land. It was a covenant between God and the lineage of Abraham. This covenant couldn't be completed if they were not willing to keep their end of the deal, and that was the requirement of circumcision. Perhaps they didn't have time to do it in the forty years of wandering. But this is certainly an odd time, and the part that makes me laugh a little is the fact that they were all in pain and in need of healing for a little while on the shores of the Jordan before they could move forward. They had a time of healing, and if you don't remember, the tribes in the land already knew that the Israelites had crossed."},{"kind":"para","text":"I suppose they had some watchmen that saw them cross and camp. This would have been the perfect time to be attacked. Maybe the people were far enough away from the Israelites campsite that it would have taken them too long to attack before they healed. Maybe they were afraid to leave their stations anticipating the Israelites coming to take them over. I don't know, but the Lord provided a time of rest on the banks of Jordan before they proceeded forward with their calling."},{"kind":"para","text":"There's a great connection here to our necessity of rest as well. A lot of pastors and ministers are in a mode of constant hurry. I've heard pastors say, \"You cannot organize ministry, you have to ALWAYS be moving.” That is not true. God provides times of rest, and we reject those times. It is the reason for the wilderness journey, after all. They were given rest after crossing the Red Sea, and they rejected it by making a false god. This time they cross the river, and they don't reject the rest that God gives them. I think it's arguable to say that if you choose to reject God's rest which is gifted to His ministers, then you will open yourself up to a higher probability of entering into sin."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he said, My presence shall go with thee, and I will give thee rest.","ref":"Exodus 33:14"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he said unto them, Come ye yourselves apart into a desert place, and rest a while: for there were many coming and going, and they had no leisure so much as to eat.","ref":"Mark 6:31"}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Gilgal\" means \"a wheel, rolling.” The Lord rolled away the reproach of Egypt this day. They had the ability and gift of leaving Egypt and entering the promise! We should all have our place of Gilgal where we have left behind our old life to enter into the new one."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":12,"title":"The Manna Ceases","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Not only did they get a time of rest, they got a time of sacrifice and offering to the Lord! They get a feast after a success. I think that's a good reason to feast if anything. But they were feasting to commemorate the fact that God has kept His promise every step of the way so far. They had to be in awe and wonder at the things that have taken place recently. It couldn't have worn off that quickly. They will continuously have victory with the Lord by their side. It seems that they were in (about) the springtime of the year if they were observing the Passover. I have read that during this time the rains would have been quite heavy, and so the Jordan River would have been at full force when they crossed. It would not have been easy for them to cross if the Lord didn't split and stop the waters from rushing."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Was the stop of manna the symbol of self-reliance? Of course not!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Your fathers did eat manna in the wilderness, and are dead. This is the bread which cometh down from heaven, that a man may eat thereof, and not die. I am the living bread which came down from heaven: if any man eat of this bread, he shall live for ever: and the bread that I will give is my flesh, which I will give for the life of the world.","ref":"John 6:49-51"},{"kind":"para","text":"Their faith cannot be placed in the raining manna for them. Their faith had to be placed on what the Lord promised to them in this new land. God said He would provide for them, and we will see God do exactly that when they stay in line with faith in Him. In fact, He provides for them for years even when they are not in line with Him. Eventually, He will have enough and take the providence away for a time until they understand how needful they are of Him. Manna was a representation of Christ for us. He was the providence for salvation."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":15,"title":"A Questionable Man","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let's read the context here. I want us to really put our feet on the ground as if we were in the place of Joshua at this moment. Joshua is quite busy planning things out here. They had experienced quite the victory. They had circumcised the people, and then held a feast for the Lord's salvation. They ate the food of the land for the first time (possibly in their lives, all they ever really tasted was manna given to them by the Lord), and now he's on the outskirts of Jericho (perhaps) praying about his next move when he looks up and sees a man with a sword in his hand. Instead of Joshua being worried and running away, he just stops, looks at the man and says, \"What's your plan? Are you here to help, or are you here to hurt?\""},{"kind":"para","text":"There seemed to be something off about this man. I think it would have been obvious to Joshua if this man was dressed as the people behind the wall, but he wasn't, and so Joshua had no idea where or what tribe he came from. I think Joshua knew full well that this man couldn't do anything against Joshua at this moment. The Lord had proven over and over again that Joshua was well protected, and Joshua had done everything right in the sight of the Lord already. What could this man possibly do if he was against Joshua?"}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was a captain of angels! What a crazy moment for Joshua. There is only one captain of angels recorded in scripture!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For he shall give his angels charge over thee, to keep thee in all thy ways.","ref":"Psalm 91:11"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Bless the LORD, ye his angels, that excel in strength, that do his commandments, hearkening unto the voice of his word.","ref":"Psalm 103:20"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Thinkest thou that I cannot now pray to my Father, and he shall presently give me more than twelve legions of angels?","ref":"Matthew 26:53"},{"kind":"para","text":"It's also understood that this \"captain\" is Christ because any other angel (when someone comes to worship them) tells the person to get up and only worship God alone!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And I fell at his feet to worship him. And he said unto me, See thou do it not: I am thy fellowservant, and of thy brethren that have the testimony of Jesus: worship God: for the testimony of Jesus is the spirit of prophecy.","ref":"Revelation 19:10"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And I John saw these things, and heard them. And when I had heard and seen, I fell down to worship before the feet of the angel which shewed me these things. Then saith he unto me, See thou do it not: for I am thy fellowservant, and of thy brethren the prophets, and of them which keep the sayings of this book: worship God.","ref":"Revelation 22:8-9"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Let no man beguile you of your reward in a voluntary humility and worshipping of angels, intruding into those things which he hath not seen, vainly puffed up by his fleshly mind,","ref":"Colossians 2:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"By the way, this includes praying. Prayer is an act of worship. If you pray to angels, you are worshipping them. This man was not just an angel, though. He was the Christ and the \"captain\" of the angels."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What a cliffhanger to leave us on! Joshua takes off his shoes because it was holy ground! Wherever the Lord is will be a place of holiness. Our shoes are filthy, and (especially in those days), our feet are probably MORE filthy. Yet, being barefoot on ground that is holy will be a great way to cleanse them, and in this instance, his sandals were in the way of that. Taking off their shoes was a sign of reverence and respect. Moses took his shoes off when near the burning bush. Joshua is taking his shoes off when near the captain of angels. For both men, it was not automatic. They both had to be told to take their shoes off."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he said, Draw not nigh hither: put off thy shoes from off thy feet, for the place whereon thou standest is holy ground.","ref":"Exodus 3:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"Their first reaction was not to take off their shoes, but to fall in worship. I think that's a healthy reaction, but the ultimate sign of reverence and humility to someone you truly care about is to remove your shoes. I don't know of any other scripture where an angel would command such reverence before his people…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":15}]},
    "6": {"chapter":6,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 06"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see the Israelites make camp on the banks of the Jordan, and the news about their appearance arrives to the Canaanites in Jericho. Joshua is then tasked to circumsize the men here, and to take some time to rest until they were fully healed before going to look at this first city they were to take. They are not longer recieving manna of the Lord, but they are gathering their own food from this new land (I wonder if the manna tasted better?) At the end of the chapter, Joshua meets this man who he wasn't sure was an enemy or a friend, and he comes to find out that it's the Lord standing before him ready to help him navigate this takeover."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we get the master plan for the taking of this city, and the enactment of it. They take Joshua's leadership seriously and they keep everything very firm as far as what they are told to do. There was no room for error here as God will prove Himself to them, and they will have no excuse but to move forward by the Word of the Lord and in faith to take this city."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"The Lord's Battle Plan","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The people of Jericho knew that they were going to be taken over. They literally KNEW that this land was not theirs to live in. They honestly had no reason to be there, other than to dwell in a land that they think they deserve, rather than take the truths of God's Word. They knew what God had promised, and that information was seemingly passed down from generation to generation at this point. So the people of Jericho are now in defense mode, and they aren't sure what the Israelites are going to do. They figure, if they are going to have to fight the Israelites, they will do it inside the safety of the walls. The walls are there as a means of defense against enemies of all sorts, and if the Israelites wanted to take the battle onto these people's turf, they would have to use the front gate."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God says, “See?\" Joshua likely thought, \"No. I don't see. I have been looking at this wall they have for quite some time, and I don't see how you have possibly given this city to us when they know that we are here and they are on high alert.” God says, \"I am giving you the king of this city and their strongest men here.” Joshua likely trusted that God would do it, but I don't think Joshua understands how at this moment. Now God will tell him how it will be done..."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Okay… So the plan of attack is to circle around the city walls once a day for six days. So far, this plan doesn't seem very stable..."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The priests likely would think, \"Okay. So we are tasked (not with fighting) but with carrying this ark around this wall every day for six days straight. Then on the seventh day, we are going to have a walk-a-thon around the walls for pretty much the entire day. And then we attack!... by blowing trumpets?”"},{"kind":"para","text":"There's a lot of useful information here with these trumpets that I think are applicable to the last days as mentioned in Revelation. I won't hang on it too much, but the trumpets are a good source of obvious signs of attack. They wouldn't just sneak into the city like ninjas. No, they will sound trumpets and make themselves well known to this entire city. I don't think it's by mistake that there are \"7\" trumpets either. But I don't want to get too far ahead of myself."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The priests were tasked with blowing the trumpets every time they circled that wall. The first day, I imagine that the priests were quite excited at the thought. The trumpets would blow, and the men would all be ready for what the Lord was about to do. They didn't quite understand the plan here yet, but they would know by the fact that the walls fell that the Lord was obviously working for them. Their last shout of acclimation (something that (once again) is mentioned in Revelation) will be a symbol of their readiness and eagerness to glorify the Lord in this final push for their land rights."},{"kind":"para","text":"In the church today, I don't know of a lot of people who are quite willing to love the Lord and shout praises to Him. They are more like \"inside\" worshippers. They worship from their brains and their hearts, but nothing is shown outwardly. This was not to be the case with the Israelites. They were all to shout their worship on this day."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So, we must remember that God is giving Joshua a plan here (the captain of the angels rather). He tells him what he is supposed to do, and Joshua is ready for the task. It doesn't seem (however) that Joshua tells the rest of the people the battle plan. Rather, he just sets the plan in motion, and everyone is thinking they are about to take this city today. Little did they know that they have six more days of this..."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":11,"title":"Starting the Master Plan","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Rereward\" means \"to gather\" or \"to bring up the rear.” In other words, there were the battle-ready men in the front, the priests and the horns behind them, and then the rest of Israel (those that were maybe hurting, old, children, or women unable to help) following behind the ark. Everything was working just as the Lord told Joshua, and Joshua was ready to see the Lord work in the seven days that they would be doing this. But of course, the fun doesn't last forever..."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It doesn't seem very fun to not be able to speak or anything while they are circling the city. Now, we don't have too much evidence (other than archeological finds) about the exact measurements and specifications of this city, but from the evidence that is found we can determine that these were decent-sized walls at about 12-15 feet high, and about a brick’s thickness in the front, and then 20-26 feet tall (just behind that) and about 6 feet thick. These walls were no joke. It would have been about six acres of property that they were circling, and would have taken them about 1 hour to circle the entire city one time. That's not much (though it's quite a bit when you are carrying a heavy ark)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Another man did the math on this, and I think he is likely to be accurate. “If the Israelites were walking at an average speed it would have been about 1.24 miles/hour.” I estimate it took about 1 hour for the Israelites to march around Jericho every day for 6 days and then about 7 hours (maybe 10 with short breaks) on the seventh day. They had to keep a little bit of a distance from the wall so they wouldn't be shot by arrows.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"This would have been quite simple. Stay quiet, walk around the wall, blow the trumpet, head back to camp and wait for the day. An hour’s worth of work for a lifetime of provision in seven days? Seems like an unfair trade, but that was how the Lord was going to provide for them."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":14,"title":"They Continue","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I imagine the first day, the watchmen on the city walls were very confused as to what the Israelites were doing. They might have thought, “Oh, they are blowing horns over here, they must be ready to attack! Everyone get ready for battle!\" Then the Israelites just walked away. How confusing that must have been for the people in Jericho. They were anticipating a battle and nothing actually happened."},{"kind":"para","text":"The second day, I imagine the same thought took place, \"Oh, they are blowing horns over here, they must be ready to attack! Everyone get ready for battle!” After about the sixth day, it just becomes noise to them. Perhaps they thought that the Israelites were just going to do this forever. This was their life now. They wouldn't be woken up by roosters, they would be woken up by trumpets every single morning..."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":21,"title":"The Walls Fall","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This was the day, and the people were ready. They had gotten seven more days of exercise, mind you. It was only for an hour per morning, but this day must have been twice as exhausting. Imagine spending 7-10 hours in your day walking in circles and then turning to go into battle. That would not seem very fun to me. I know it's for the Lord, but I also know that there are a lot of other ways the Lord can give us the victory, yet that's not what He has chosen to do here. I believe the Lord provides these people with energy for this last shout of victory here because they wouldn't have had it on their own accord."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A great reminder that Rahab was to be spared. They weren't to touch that house. The house, if you don't remember, was stationed along the wall. All of the other houses would collapse at this time except for Rahab’s, and that would be another testament that the Lord wanted to protect this woman and her household because of her willingness to help the Israelites."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Everything was to be destroyed; otherwise, it could become a problem for the Israelites. They were known (as we all are) to turn away quickly from the Lord. If they took an idol, or just started dwelling in their homes, or eating of their land without the approval of the Lord, there was no doubt that they would turn away from God. No matter how valuable they thought the thing was, or how it could be used in their lives and eyes, they could not take it. They must destroy it all, and Moses had commanded them to do this. Joshua tells them to take the precious materials (silver, gold, brass, and iron) to commemorate the Lord and the Lord alone."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It was not a very hard takeover. I imagine that the moment the walls fell, they were all baffled and unprepared. They assumed that the lot of them would come through the gate, not through the walls. What an amazing account to read about here. There have been a few things already that Joshua has led through that were miracles, and this is certainly one of them."},{"kind":"para","text":"Most people would know this story today, and there is plenty of evidence dictating that these walls fell. Debate all you want on how it happened, all I know is that the Lord's Word is true, and that He can make all the walls of this world fall with just a shout of acclamation. That is what He will do at the last days when the Lord returns."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For the Lord himself shall descend from heaven with a shout, with the voice of the archangel, and with the trump of God: and the dead in Christ shall rise first: Then we which are alive and remain shall be caught up together with them in the clouds, to meet the Lord in the air: and so shall we ever be with the Lord.","ref":"1 Thessalonians 4:16-17"},{"kind":"para","text":"To be in the land when the shout is made is what all of God's children are anticipating!"}]}]},{"start":22,"end":27,"title":"Joshua Becomes Well Known","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They took Rahab and (as it seems) a whole lot of people that were in her house, out of the city to finish the job. They kept to their word to protect Rahab and her family, and they were now part of the camp of Israel. They were a huge part of history. I often think about the names of Scripture. In Scripture, if a name is mentioned, I feel that is a very important sign that the person is something of value to the Scripture. Some names are evil, and some names are righteous. Rahab had a name that was kind of both."},{"kind":"para","text":"She was a harlot, you know, and that kind of occupation carries around with it a sort of stigma that most people would probably not forget. But her name becomes something extremely important to the building of Israel, and if it wasn't for her kindness and security for the Israelites, they might have been lost in the wilderness forever. It is also Rahab that will continue the lineage of the Savior. If they didn't rescue Rahab and her family here, the line of David would look very much different."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Abraham begat Isaac; and Isaac begat Jacob; and Jacob begat Judas and his brethren; And Judas begat Phares and Zara of Thamar; and Phares begat Esrom; and Esrom begat Aram; And Aram begat Aminadab; and Aminadab begat Naasson; and Naasson begat Salmon; And Salmon begat Boaz of Rachab; and Boaz begat Obed of Ruth; and Obed begat Jesse;","ref":"Matthew 1:2-5"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Rachab\" is likely the Greek spelling for “Rahab.\" Their names are both the same meaning, and we don't have a mention of any other Rahab in this line. \"Rahab\" means “wide.\" The timeline of all of this seems a little close, so I don’t want to put too much stake into it, but I am not sure what other Rachab could be spoken about in Scripture."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Israelites did everything right! They didn't run away, they didn't keep some idols or some tools or weapons of sorts. They didn't spare anyone their lives (they were told to take them all down). They didn't stutter in fear when the walls fell. They kept to their tasks and they saw a victory this day because of it!"}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You might think, “Well, who is dumb enough to build this city again?” Well, there will be someone dumb enough to do it (we read about it in 1 Kings 16), and the curse that Joshua is giving here is one that is kept in that chapter. At least the Israelites were wise enough to keep themselves from being cursed like verse 18 suggests... right?"}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Say what you want about the legitimacy of scripture, these people believed what they heard about Joshua, and it was evident by the fallen walls of Jericho. They didn't need proof to believe that Joshua was something special. They just needed word of mouth, and people were astonished by what happened. There was a great chance that others would hear and then want to see the city for themselves, but most of the people would just accept the news as accurate because of the people that were talking about it. It's quite difficult not to believe something when everyone else has specific viewpoints on it and has perhaps seen it themselves."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, there were some, no doubt, who didn't believe that this was true. Those people are ignorant fools for not looking into it to see. Maybe there was something keeping them from going too close to the city. Do they just give up and assume everyone else is wrong, or do they try a different route to find the evidence?"},{"kind":"para","text":"I connect this with our modern culture because it is what all atheists do with God's Word. They just give up and assume everyone else has to be wrong without looking for the evidence themselves. Such a pity to be a very small remnant of unbelievers simply because you are too calloused and too lazy to search for the truth written in front of you."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":27}]},
    "7": {"chapter":7,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 07"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: The Israelites have been having a very prominent moving forward as they have been victorious in their first steps of taking over the land. They have kept to the Lord's calling and will, and they have obeyed His moves so far. In fact, they took this first portion with relative ease, and it is getting the rest of the lands around them into a concerned state. The Israelites didn't seem like much, so how are they able be so prosperous?"},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, they move forward to take more land just like they were told, except things don't go how they expect, and they can't figure out why..."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":1,"title":"Falling Together","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They have done so well up to this point. We see one man (Achan) do something stupid while they are going about this takeover, and he does it with the knowledge of it being stupid. From Deuteronomy, we get the clear picture that everything that the Israelites do, they do together. They were to be one unit, even though they are twelve tribes. So when Achan makes this grave mistake here, it doesn't just affect Achan, as it says, it affects \"the children of Israel...\""},{"kind":"para","text":"Just as if a church member today were to make a grave mistake in the church house, the entire community would be treating the church as an enemy (as is often the case). A little leaven... This one move made their previous momentum forward come to an abrupt halt. This is important, because they have been on a very steady incline with the work of the Lord thus far. They have explained their course of action, and have reiterated over and over again what will happen if they make mistakes and yet here they are RIGHT at the beginning making a mistake. I am glad, however, that the issue was noticed right away, and instead of ignoring their duties in this area, they address it..."}]}]},{"start":2,"end":5,"title":"A Defeat From Ai","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So they are pretty confident at this point about their victory. They have had a victorious streak for a little while now, and if they were able to take this first area down without much of an issue, then having even less people to do the work would be even better because there can be some that stay, clean up, and rest. It seems they don't know about what Achan has done yet, but they will soon find out..."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They knew that the Lord was on their side, yet God doesn't give them a victory. They are confused at this point, thinking, \"Why would God get us this far, bring us this close, and then take our victory? I thought He wanted us to take the land?\" I think it's sufficient that moving forward must be a steady progress."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sometimes we let our ADHD get in the way of our short progress in the Lord. We think like they do, \"We have had one victory, let's just keep moving for more.\" We should be taking time (unlike they did) to stop, reflect, and evaluate the situation. We should be making sure that everything is ready to move forward first. Honestly, we should be praying about the next battle that we must face before entering into it. Let the Lord guide, because it's far easier and peaceful when He does."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":9,"title":"Joshua Prays","entries":[{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This will be a pretty common practice throughout scripture."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And there ran a man of Benjamin out of the army, and came to Shiloh the same day with his clothes rent, and with earth upon his head.","ref":"1 Samuel 4:12"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And it came to pass, when the king had heard the words of the law, that he rent his clothes.","ref":"2 Chronicles 34:19"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And it came to pass, when king Hezekiah heard it, that he rent his clothes, and covered himself with sackcloth, and went into the house of the LORD.","ref":"Isaiah 37:1"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then the high priest rent his clothes, saying, He hath spoken blasphemy; what further need have we of witnesses? behold, now ye have heard his blasphemy.","ref":"Matthew 26:65"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Which when the apostles, Barnabas and Paul, heard of, they rent their clothes, and ran in among the people, crying out,","ref":"Acts 14:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"I think we get the picture. This sign of \"renting clothes\" was a symbol of \"undoing.\" They were so distraught and hurt that they couldn't think of any other way to express their anger than to rip their clothes apart. I don't know if any of you have ever felt this way, but I certainly have felt undone enough to want to break something. I've never wanted to rip my clothes, but to each their own."},{"kind":"para","text":"The \"dust\" on their heads was a symbol of humility. They would do this as an act of belittling themselves. Now, they aren't doing this just as a show. They are not trying to earn favor with the Lord, I want that to be clear. They are doing it because they are truly undone, and they don't know what else to do. They are losing the battle all of a sudden, and they don't know where to turn. They had been turning to God this whole time and they were told about victories, not about defeats. If the Lord wasn't with them, how were they supposed to move forward? It's the question of the ages, and it's one we must address as well..."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Environ us around\" just means to \"surround.\" Joshua claims that he was concerned about how God's name would look if the children of Israel were destroyed. God's name has a bad reputation today. There are a lot of people in this world who cringe at the name of God or even at the name of Jesus. They view God a lot in the light of what Joshua is saying here. The thing is, God's name cannot be anything less than great. Even if He destroyed the whole Earth (which He does with Noah and will do again), God is great and He is perfect. He knows what He's doing, even if Joshua doesn't. However, Joshua should have done this to begin with so they wouldn't have seen defeat, but it's a lesson to be learned, and I am hopeful that Joshua has learned the lesson here."},{"kind":"para","text":"Israel is God's chosen people, and God has made that clear since the beginning. But just because they are His chosen doesn't mean they are okay to go against His mercy and Will. That is what they have mistaken today. It's what they mistaken during the time of Christ. They think to themselves, \"Yeah, I am an Israelite Jew, therefore no matter what I do, I will always be chosen of God.\" However, their acts prove they are not of God, and they are nothing better than your worst atheist."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":15,"title":"The Tresspass","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God lets it be known that it was not because He doesn't want to give them a victory, but rather it's because He doesn't want them to fall into sin. God is not going to allow you to live comfortably in sin. I think there are a great deal of people today who don't see victory because they aren't willing to address, locate, and destroy their habits in sin. That is not a good sign of a faithful Christian."},{"kind":"para","text":"More than victory for His people, He wants obedience from them, and I think there are a lot more people who are looking for a God-given victory without the desire to give up what He wants them to give up. Joshua, however, doesn't know that this happened in their camp. He's learning this for the first time. He had warned them about what would happen if they mess up (even if they mess up in a small way), and this was not something small to mess up in. Achan had to be feeling pretty guilty at this point. He knew what he had done. He knew what had to happen. He knew that he was going against the Lord's, Joshua's, and Moses's commands by doing this very thing because they were all told to destroy the idols, and everything that was there..."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, it will be the tribe of Judah and this family that will be found to be guilty. It seems like a tedious task to have to do it this way, but it's worth the amount of effort and time it will take in order to get to the bottom of this. This seemingly small act was so serious that they had to be burned with fire, and all of his belongings were to be burned with him as well. Let's hope he isn't a married man."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":21,"title":"The Great Reveal","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So, as the reader, we already knew the end of this mystery. We knew that it was Achan. Now it's time for Achan to confess to the transgression. The frustrating part is Achan could have come forward a long time ago. I don't know how long it took for Joshua to go through all of this, but he started early in the morning, and it is finally coming to a close..."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua seems a bit tired here. He is trying to keep patience with Achan. He wants Achan to admit his faults, because without a genuine confession, they will not be able to move forward with their punishment. Joshua is pretty confident that it's Achan, though. All signs point to him."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Friend... There is no amount of gold, or silver, or garments in this world that is worth more than your following of the Lord. God is the value of mankind, and without Him we are about as cheap as gum on the bottom of someone's shoe... Notice that Achan recognizes that it was a sin. He did it willingly. He even admits that the sin was of \"coveting.\" He has everything valuable given to him. He hasn't had to do much of anything to receive true riches and wealth and honor, except follow the Lord and everything will be given to him."},{"kind":"para","text":"I commend Achan (though it took him a very long time) for actually confessing his sin and calling it out, knowing that death was likely looming on the horizon. There are not a lot of people today who are willing to call out their sin. I believe it's because they know that there is punishment for that sin. Little do they realize that the punishment will come for sin whether they openly admit it or not... This was a very foolish mistake."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":26,"title":"The Hurtful End","entries":[{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He had a lot going for him already. His family alone should have been his greatest treasure. Yet he found more value in the things of the people before him instead of the Lord and what He has already been providing. That is what coveting really is, anyway. It's not being content with what God has already given you. The real sin involves taking it because you want it. Your desires often lead to actions, especially when it's in your own flesh. We really are our own worst enemies."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I don't believe they felt great about this situation. They had to stick to the Lord's commands on the matter, however, and so they did. They stoned him and burned everything he had. His family (that is, his line and name) was cut off just like the Lord said would happen. If the Israelites didn't do it, there would be no moving forward anyway. They would have been defeated, likely, by Ai next, and the Bible story would end here. They obeyed the Lord here, though, and it's a lesson for them all to follow. They have to take the Will and following of the Lord seriously if they want to move forward; otherwise, it will lead to their death..."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The fact that they named the valley after him is a testament that the Israelites were not callous to the idea of stoning and burning him, his family, and his belongings. There are likely plenty of people who will ask, “Well, why did God burn the whole family? Why couldn't Achan be the only one that was burned?\" It's a fair question. I want to point out a couple of things before we address the family, though."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Achan\" means \"troubler.\" Now, right off the bat, we can assume that this is likely not Achan's first offense. It's his first, possibly, in the new land, but not in his life. Your parents aren't going to name you this unless you have a rebellious spirit. Of course, you might have noticed that the name of the valley is spelled differently, though. \"Achor\" means \"disturbance\" and it can also mean \"trouble.\" It's a name that fits both the person and the situation."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now we come to the question about his family. We have read a few times, already, that if there be men who sin, they will be in danger of being \"cut off.\""},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Seven days shall there be no leaven found in your houses: for whosoever eateth that which is leavened, even that soul shall be cut off from the congregation of Israel, whether he be a stranger, or born in the land.","ref":"Exodus 12:19"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Moreover the soul that shall touch any unclean thing, as the uncleanness of man, or any unclean beast, or any abominable unclean thing, and eat of the flesh of the sacrifice of peace offerings, which pertain unto the LORD, even that soul shall be cut off from his people.","ref":"Leviticus 7:21"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But the soul that doeth ought presumptuously, whether he be born in the land, or a stranger, the same reproacheth the LORD; and that soul shall be cut off from among his people.","ref":"Numbers 15:30"},{"kind":"para","text":"We could argue that it doesn't talk about \"cutting off the lineage in these verses\" and you would be right to argue this. It talks about one man. It also says in Deuteronomy:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The fathers shall not be put to death for the children, neither shall the children be put to death for the fathers: every man shall be put to death for his own sin.","ref":"Deuteronomy 24:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"So we must address two factors then. One, it is unlikely that the family wasn't involved in some way. They were all moving together, and there is no way they didn't know about the treasures hidden in their tent which they lived and slept in together. Two, (and more prominently is to be believed in my opinion) the family wasn't stoned and burned…"},{"kind":"para","text":"The verses corresponding to this state that Achan alone was found guilty. God tells Joshua to burn the man involved (not his family with him). When we get to these verses here, it clearly states that Achan was the only one stoned to death and was burned with \"them.\" We are to assume that \"them\" was his belongings, as well as the silver and gold he stole. I don't see how someone could get the idea that the whole family died by the transgression of this one man unless there was an insinuation that the whole family was in on this sin."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don't believe the rest of Achan's family was killed, but I wasn't there to see it take place. Based solely off scripture (and that based on the law of God), there would be no reason for Joshua and Israel to destroy the entire family. Either way, this was set to be an example as to how serious the situation was for the children of Israel. They had to start off right, and there was one already making grave mistakes against the Lord."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":26}]},
    "8": {"chapter":8,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 08"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: We ended our last chapter on a bit of a sour note. It was not one that is very fun or happy to remember, but it was a great representation of our fleshly nature to want what is in the world rather than seek after God's desires first. Achan was killed for taking from Jericho and then trying to hide it (and it was not something that Joshua, nor the people of Israel really enjoyed being a part of). They are then left to burn everything he had and set a memorial for him of what he had done."},{"kind":"para","text":"They couldn't take much time to weep, however, because there was still a big issue with the city of Ai that needed to be taken care of. In this chapter, we will see the aftermath of Achan's sacrifice, as well as the gained trust of the children of Israel towards their holy and perfect God."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"Take the Spoil","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It is interesting to note that God tells them to take \"the spoil and cattle of Ai\" but restrains the Israelites from taking the spoil from Jericho. Why has God changed His mind here? I've heard varying degrees of explanation as to why God would allow this all of a sudden. Perhaps it's because of some sort of disease that the Lord knew about in Ai. Maybe it's because of some sort of \"first fruits\" idea that others have claimed. I assume it's because the LORD wants to provide for His people, and Achan was preventing that from happening. Now that the Lord has given them the go-ahead, there is reason for them to take the spoils, whereas before they had to do it in secret (and they still wouldn't get away with it). Perhaps, maybe, it's a combination of everything. I don't know, but I do know that the Lord has a reason for it, and He gives an example through Achan about what it looks like to trust the Lord. Had Achan just waited, there wouldn't have to be this whole scene in the last chapter..."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":9,"title":"Tricking Ai","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The first time they were going to Ai, they only requested two to three thousand men. This time, they are requesting thirty thousand who were \"men of valour.\" I think this is a symbol of the Israelites’ lack of trust in the Lord, even though the Lord has given them reason to know that He is now on their side. They took care of the issue that was preventing them from moving forward. There was no reason for them to feign trust in the Lord at this point. Either way, they didn't want to run this time, and perhaps the people of Ai would have just laughed at them anyway if they saw the same number of men coming. This time, Ai would know there was more power behind them than before."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua is going to use their failure to gain the victory. The funny part is, the people of Ai are going to feel more arrogant and prideful about their previous “victory,\" and so they will think they have the better chance against the Israelites this day as well. But the remaining Israelites will be waiting on the other side of the city, and when the people run out to chase the few, the majority will go in and destroy the city quite easily."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This seems like a foolproof plan. Now, I have seen people approach the city of Ai as a representation of our flesh. It is battling against our new nature. Perhaps that is a good way to view this scene."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"So then they that are in the flesh cannot please God.","ref":"Romans 8:8"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For we wrestle not against flesh and blood, but against principalities, against powers, against the rulers of the darkness of this world, against spiritual wickedness in high places.","ref":"Ephesians 6:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"The flesh (as I have no doubt we are all familiar with) tends to sneak up on us. We think we have the victory over it until we get into the battle without the Lord and end up hurt and wounded. Thankfully, we have an advocate..."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"My little children, these things write I unto you, that ye sin not. And if any man sin, we have an advocate with the Father, Jesus Christ the righteous:","ref":"1 John 2:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"However, we cannot approach the battle until we have come to genuine repentance over that sin. It's not about \"feeling guilty.\" It's about seriously forsaking and turning away from the sin. Guilt without action is not repentance at all, and so you will not overcome the flesh until you take action to overcome it. The Lord has provided means to take to get rid of sinful fleshly lusts. His Word is a help to that."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this time, Achan had to die because of it (he didn't have an advocate with the father yet, and so his sin had a great cost). Today, we have an advocate (thank God we don't have to die for our sins), but we cannot just simply say, \"God forgives me\" and then keep doing it. No, genuine repentance is a continuous labor. We must repent daily while we live, because the flesh is always there to keep us from moving forward. In this scene, Joshua is giving instruction to move behind Ai, and they will be enticed into thinking that they will have a victory again yet they will be destroyed by surprise. Perhaps that is a good way to view how to combat our flesh. Destroy it by surprise. Trick your flesh with God's goodness. The book of James is a great book about how to overcome your flesh (and 1 Corinthians even)."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Destroy it all, and don't look back. There's nothing worth saving (except some things that will perhaps help them to move forward)."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps it's good to note that when taking care of Ai, Joshua had an army of people behind him. If this is a representation of the flesh, we should also take this as an encouragement to have an army behind us as well when we are dealing with our sinful nature. We need the Lord, first and foremost (of course), but even the Lord knows that it's not good that man should be alone."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the LORD God said, It is not good that the man should be alone; I will make him an help meet for him.","ref":"Genesis 2:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"And while dog is man's best friend, he is not sufficient enough to replace human help."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":17,"title":"Facing Ai Head On","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let me just note (for personal help’s sake) we cannot control our flesh by our own strength. I hope that the previous statements have not come off as such. In order for us to come to terms with handling our flesh, we must first come to the Lord with the matter. See, there is no desire in man to deal with the flesh without the Lord revealing the need to do it. If we do not have a desire to deal with it, we are likely not as in tune with the Lord as we might hope we are. God will always convict us of our sin, and that is when we must take to the battle WITH Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"We get the picture here, of Israel sitting across the valley staring at their issue, formulating and enacting upon the plan that was made for them. They are desirous to live in prosperity before the Lord, and they can't continue forward unless they deal with Ai first. If they can't deal with Ai, they can't deal with anything bigger. They've already seen what the Lord can do through Jericho, and so they must rely upon the Lord through this battle as well to see a victory. It is the same with us."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will not see victory if we are not relying upon the Lord to provide it. We can either come face to face with our sin and say, \"It's too hard to overcome, there's nothing I can do.\" or we can say, \"By the might of the Lord, I will join the battle and may He provide the victory.\" And may the people around us say, \"I'm here with you. If you fail, we all fail. If you succeed, we all succeed.\" That is what we are looking at here."},{"kind":"para","text":"Three thousand people went to attack Ai before, and there were only killed about thirty-six men (I say only, though that is a lot, it could have been far worse if they hadn't retreated). But all three thousand lost together over those thirty-six. If anyone dies today, it will be the defeat of all thirty thousand men even if there is only ONE that dies."},{"kind":"para","text":"We view it in the same way as the entire earth. The earth was defeated the day Christ was killed. We were all doomed and destined for an eternal torture. If a perfect man died a heinous death and it ends there, what hope do we have for ourselves. But thank God the perfect man lives today, and so through one man, we all have the victory over eternity and over sin."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wherefore, as by one man sin entered into the world, and death by sin; and so death passed upon all men, for that all have sinned:","ref":"Romans 5:12"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For as by one man's disobedience many were made sinners, so by the obedience of one shall many be made righteous.","ref":"Romans 5:19"}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He had an army to help him encamp this city, but it was he alone that goes to confront it (at least for now)."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Sin has a sure focus. It is possible to trick our own flesh. I have done a lot of research on brain psychology, and the thing that fascinates me (the most of it all) is the fact that you can literally rewire your brain to think differently if you take the time and effort to do it. People usually don't because they find it to be tedious or too much effort (they have no desire to change), but when we have the Lord, we have plenty of reason to put in the effort. We can trick our brains into thinking that it is more rewarding to feed the Spirit than it is to feed the flesh (and those that know Christ know how much more rewarding it actually IS to feed the Spirit). Our flesh gives us rewards when we feed it, don't get me wrong. We will have a high every time we feed the flesh, but that high doesn't last very long, and so we have to feed it again and again and again. Yet, when we feed the Spirit, we are fed for all of eternity!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Blessed are they which do hunger and thirst after righteousness: for they shall be filled.","ref":"Matthew 5:6"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But whosoever drinketh of the water that I shall give him shall never thirst; but the water that I shall give him shall be in him a well of water springing up into everlasting life.","ref":"John 4:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"It is also worth noting that Joshua takes care of this very early on. He could have waited a little while longer before going in to try and take down this city, but he wants to take care of it right away. Even in the middle of the night. No doubt these people were tired, and the people of Ai notice him and are forced to wake up early to take care of it."},{"kind":"para","text":"In the same sense, it is wise for God's children to keep short accounts with the Lord. The moment we are conscious of our sin, we should address it right away rather than put it off and let it fester..."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It's funny to me because the definition of \"Ai\" in Hebrew means \"Heap of ruins.\" There was not much here, and that is perhaps the reason that Israel thought they could take it over so easily. Ai really wasn't worth much, and in the end that is truly what it becomes \"a heap of ruins.\" Interestingly enough \"Bethel\" gets involved here. \"Bethel\" means \"house of God.\" It's something we will look into more later on. I don't believe it was known as \"Bethel\" at this moment, but it will come to be known as Bethel once Joshua takes over, and that is the name in which it will be called. He is referring to it as \"Bethel\" now because it's possible that this city was nameless before. Maybe it was just a part of \"Ai?\" Either way, scripture does this a lot in the Old Testament (especially in Genesis because a lot of places didn't have names at that time). Bethel, however, won't have any sort of advantage against Israel because they were both looking the wrong way."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":23,"title":"Ai Destroyed","entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That was their sign to take the city. I'm curious to know how easy it was to spot Joshua's spear. They had to have some distance between them and Joshua, but it seems that the rising of this spear was their code to take the city. It would have also given the people of Ai ample purpose to turn and see what Joshua was about, and by the time they turned around to look, it was already too late for the people of Ai."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems Ai gave up rather quickly. They didn't even put up a fight as soon as they saw that their city was on fire and being destroyed. There was no reason for them to continue anymore. When we have the victory over sin, we will often see this happen with our flesh. We will be thinking one thing, but when we reward our Spirit more than our flesh, we stop desiring the fleshly things as much. Our flesh just kind of gives up. That is what every child of God longs for towards their flesh. They want their flesh to give up."},{"kind":"para","text":"Does this mean that sin will not exist anymore in your life? Of course not! That is a battle we will fight for the rest of our lives. However, it's a battle that we know we will have the best victory if we are involved with it in the power of the Lord, and we can only say (like the Israelites) \"Look what God has done in my life!\" They take the king (the only one remaining in Ai) and present him before Joshua. This is the last thing to be taken care of before they officially have the victory."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":29,"title":"Everything Accomplished","entries":[{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Twelve thousand is a pretty hefty number. Twelve thousand killed this day, and not a single death in the Israelites’ camp. It was a hard-fought victory that they won together (though the people of Ai didn't put up much of a fight). The people of Ai saw their \"heap of ruins\" as something valuable. We are a lot of the time like Ai in our material property."},{"kind":"para","text":"We think that is the most valuable thing, and without it, there is no reason to continue on with life. We might as well give up right then and there and let the enemy win. We should be more like Joshua. We have a hope of better promises if we just move forward in faith, and we can see victory today, rather than defeat when it's all taken from us. Faith in the Lord is a real value (and it leads to bigger treasure if we stay consistent)."},{"kind":"para","text":"I have heard people say, \"Why waste your time in ministry? You don't get paid to do it, you don't have much reward from people, and it seems like it's very tedious and exhausting. What's the point of staying in something that only seems to have a small payoff?\" I think it's a fair question. If we take the world's view of my ministry, it certainly doesn't make sense to spend the time that I do on it. It doesn't make sense that our pastor continues to pastor, or teachers continue to teach. But when we have a biblical view of it, we know that the treasures we are storing up are not found here on Earth. What might seem like a small payoff now, will affect the course of history (as well as eternity). \"Small\" payoffs today are huge wins in the Lord later on. We are making small moves, perhaps, and it seems like there is very little change taking place, but small moves and little change consistently over a lifetime accumulates to huge impacts in the end. If we totaled up the amount of change we will make at the end of our lives (by staying consistent with the little things), we will be able to see a much larger portion of impact than anyone else in the world could do in their own power (or rather by not doing anything at all (for they have no motive))."},{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua just destroyed a \"heap of ruins\" and that might not seem like much to anyone else in the world, but it will make a big difference later on. There is no telling what kind of damage this heap of ruins could have caused if it were not dealt with at this moment. Honestly, that is what makes a successful Christian. It's not about the work you put in. It's not about how many hours, and how many late nights you have. It's not about how much money you spend (or don't spend) in the ministry. It's about staying consistent in the work of the Lord, and learning to love His Work as if it were new every morning. You can do the same thing a billion times for the Lord and learn to refresh your care for it every day if you stay consistent. Many people don't stay consistent (however) and that is what causes their downfall..."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ai lives up to its name. There isn't anything left of it, and the Israelites made sure that it didn't come back up. Joshua had been through too much, and had come too far to see it rear its ugly face again in his lifetime (or that of the rest of the Israelites’ lifetime)."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The king of Ai served as a testimony to the rest of the cities who supported Ai. It was to say, \"We are not down and out. We are not giving up. We are not defeated.\" The world will get the picture..."}]}]},{"start":30,"end":35,"title":"A Choice is Set","entries":[{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Moses told them to do this and set the stones up in the mountains."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Behold, I set before you this day a blessing and a curse; A blessing, if ye obey the commandments of the LORD your God, which I command you this day: And a curse, if ye will not obey the commandments of the LORD your God, but turn aside out of the way which I command you this day, to go after other gods, which ye have not known. And it shall come to pass, when the LORD thy God hath brought thee in unto the land whither thou goest to possess it, that thou shalt put the blessing upon mount Gerizim, and the curse upon mount Ebal. Are they not on the other side Jordan, by the way where the sun goeth down, in the land of the Canaanites, which dwell in the champaign over against Gilgal, beside the plains of Moreh? For ye shall pass over Jordan to go in to possess the land which the LORD your God giveth you, and ye shall possess it, and dwell therein. And ye shall observe to do all the statutes and judgments which I set before you this day.","ref":"Deuteronomy 11:26-32"},{"kind":"para","text":"One was to go on Ebal (that is, the side of curses), the other to go to Mount Gerizim (that was the side of blessing). It was a symbol to the Israelites that they had a choice to make. They could trek either path with the same effort, but one led to blessing, the other led to cursing."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There were strangers living among them who were of the family of Rahab (in case you were wondering where they came from all of a sudden). These mountain peaks were a perfect picture of the Israelites’ decisions moving forward."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua read every single word of God's law to the people. He took the writings of Moses and read it all in that same day. I assume it took them a few hours to read it all, and I assume they were enamored by it (especially because they are currently living out what Moses had tasked them with and promised them). It's something that should be remembered forever by the Israelites. Sadly, however, the Israelites will forget about all of this (but that's a spoiler)."}]},{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Everyone heard it. Even the children were there to hear it. It was important that they hear it and remember it, and utilize what Moses had commanded. It was important that they understand why they are able to stand on those mountain peaks this day. It was not by luck or fluke that they were standing there. It was (obviously) not by their own power that they were able to get to the top of these mountains. They were there by the power and leading of the Lord, and that alone is the reason they (or we) are able to stand today to hear this great Word of God."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":29},{"start":30,"end":35}]},
    "9": {"chapter":9,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 09"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see the victories of the Israelites take place against the people of Ai, and we end on a more victorious note than we did in the chapter before it. They have finally got back on track, and they are then left at the end of the chapter with putting up the blessings and the curses on the mountain tops to display the choices they are able to make and the rewards for such choices."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, they are living and growing comforably in this land, and the people around them are not very happy about it. They know that the Israelites are come in to destroy the people, and so the people are trying to figure out the best plan of attack so that they don't get destroyed all at once."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"Rallying Troops","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I have read that a good representation of these people would be that Jericho represents the world; Ai represents the flesh; and the Gibeonites represent Satan. Perhaps that is a fair comparison, as we will soon see the kind of tactics each of these people takes to combat against Joshua. They have (apparently) heard of the power of these people, and they are now teaming up to take down Joshua and his men. As it is with us today."},{"kind":"para","text":"If Satan can't get you to follow the world, he will try and get you to follow your flesh, and if not the flesh, then he will get you to follow him. He has plans for all three, and usually those plans are pretty redundant throughout history. Satan hasn't really changed his tactics, and I don't think he ever will. What's crazier than that is we have a book that is solemnly dedicated to pointing out the tactics of Satan, and what to expect, and yet there are people who are still blindsided when Satan attacks. We are a weak people, that is why the Lord had to come for us. They have conquered (for the most part) Jericho; they have (finally) defeated Ai and thrown it to ruin; and now it's time for them to take on the rest (that is Satan)."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":15,"title":"The Israelites Are Tricked","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The way this is written gives off the impression that the Gibeonites were not super impressed by the leading of Joshua and the overthrow of the other places. They are formulating a subtle plan to try and harm the Israelites by making themselves look needful, and hoping to play on the goodness of the Israelites. Satan does this often. He dresses up like someone who is in distress, because he knows that is a good way to get close to someone."},{"kind":"para","text":"We see this happen pretty often in the church. In fact, if Satan can get you away from the church through one bad apple, he will certainly find a way to do it. Thankfully, our church attendance doesn't depend on the rest of the people attending. We should be attending the church we attend because the grace and Spirit of God prompts you to. The Gibeonites are trying to play a mind game more than outright physically attacking them. They seem to think that they can win over the Israelites."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They didn't come from a far country (obviously). They lived pretty close, but they were playing a part, remember? They are trying to act as if they heard about the fame of Joshua from far away, and they want to try and make a \"pact\" with Joshua so they can work together."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have spent a good portion of Deuteronomy (and even some mention of it in this book of Joshua) about how the Israelites were not to spare the people here. They could not mix with these people and (for obvious reasons) they weren't to dwell with them in the hopes and fears that they wouldn’t be pulled away from following the Lord. This is exactly what the Lord and Moses were warning them about. These people were not there to serve their God, and that will be proven in a moment."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Well, this isn't necessarily a lie. They certainly heard the fame of the Lord and what He did with the Israelites in Egypt. That is proven through the conversations in Jericho. Rahab knew that the Israelites’ God was protecting them, and that seems to be a pretty big conversation for these cities. However, the \"We are thy servants\" and the \"from a very far country thy servants are come because of the name of the Lord thy God\" is a complete lie, and they could care less about the Lord that the Israelites served."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You will notice a couple of things Satan does throughout Scripture. First, he will use common sense to get you to trust him. He will not trick you with outright lies. He sprinkles in truth to make the lies more believable. Second, he will use obvious signs of the Lord to entice you. Satan notices what God is doing throughout every major event of Scripture. We see this in the birth of Jesus, when Satan uses Herod and tries to kill all the babies two years and younger in the hopes that one of those babies will be the Messiah. We see this again when Jesus is in the wilderness fast being tempted by Satan, and Satan tries to use (once again, common sense and obvious signs of the Lord) everything against Jesus. We see it when Jesus comes to the end and Judas has been used by Satan. We see it throughout the New Testament church, and constant persecution of the churches, as well as of the martyrdoms of the Christians. We will see in the end as well, when Satan will deceive the masses because of his sprinkling of truths mixed with his lies."},{"kind":"para","text":"His whole goal, in the end, is to destroy God's people. He is forming an army of people who are not the Lord's and he will use those people, and will use every means necessary to try and trick the elect, or try and sway the saved from following after the Lord and doing what they are told. Sadly, there are some who have been hit by these tricks and have not succeeded in battling against them. Others are still battling and having a hard time seeing the victory. Perhaps they are on the verge of giving up. Others are strong and fit and battling at the top of their game."},{"kind":"para","text":"Do be warned, though, if you feel you are doing well. Satan will even use your own skill against you and trip you up when you think you are on top. Christ, however, is our victory, and so the battle doesn't need to be won in the sense of complete victory here on Earth, because the real victory comes in eternity. Does this mean we can give up and accept Satan in? Absolutely not. Rather, it means we should keep fighting, but when we slip, or when we are wounded for not paying attention, the Lord is not ready to strike you. Rather, He is ready to lead you back on track again."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These Gibeonites were great actors, and the children of Israel are falling for it. They should really have known better than to trust anyone from any country around them during a time of war. Their biggest flaw is referenced in verse 14: that they didn't ask anything about this and receive instruction on what to do by \"the mouth of the Lord.\" It was not very wise of them, and they should have learned their lesson from the mistakes of Ai and Jericho."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is even worse. Satan has been given a spot with them by their own mouths, but also given immunity with them because of it! They say, \"We won't just make peace with you, we will make sure that we take CARE of you.\" This is an advisory for all Christians in this world. You cannot give care to Satan. You cannot make peace with the one who is looking for destruction. When you do make peace with him, you will not have peace in you. The Lord says to love your enemies;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But I say unto you, Love your enemies, bless them that curse you, do good to them that hate you, and pray for them which despitefully use you, and persecute you;","ref":"Matthew 5:44"},{"kind":"para","text":"To make sure we are all on the same page here, Jesus isn't talking about Satan. Jesus had compassion on the demons when putting them into the pigs, but he also doesn't have peace with the demons. He can't have peace with them. He knows their end goal, and He knows their destination. Demons don't get a second chance, and they never will. Demons are an interesting creation, because they have free will (obviously, that is how they became demons), but it seems the moment they made their decision about which side they were taking, their will remained to the one side they chose. If they chose the Lord, there was no changing their minds. If they chose Satan, there was nothing that could be done to change that choice."},{"kind":"para","text":"I would love to dig a little deeper into the doctrines of angels and demons in a different setting, but what I want to say is that we cannot \"love\" our enemy Satan. He is unloveable. In fact, just because I am talking about him here is giving him a prideful and arrogant look at himself. He likes it when we hang onto him and get our eyes focused off of Jesus Christ. The more we can talk about him, the less we will talk about Jesus. He wants to be the center, but Jesus has already taken the center of the universe. Satan is just the antagonist of the story, and he's not even a very good one. He has the same lines, plays the same roles, and does the same thing over and over again."},{"kind":"para","text":"Growing up, I loved playing Nintendo games. The thing that made them so appealing was at the end of the levels (like in Sonic) there were boss battles. In those battles, the boss would have some sort of machine to try and attack Sonic, and you would have to dodge and jump and hit the boss on the head a certain amount of times and eventually his machine would break and then he'd run off until the next level. The next time you see him, his machine is a little bigger and there might be more obstacles, but the gameplay is still the same. You are to jump, dodge, and hit the boss on the head. Satan is the same way."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And I will put enmity between thee and the woman, and between thy seed and her seed; it shall bruise thy head, and thou shalt bruise his heel.","ref":"Genesis 3:15"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And I saw one of his heads as it were wounded to death; and his deadly wound was healed: and all the world wondered after the beast.","ref":"Revelation 13:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"Satan's head will be wounded over and over again, but the final blow comes when he is placed into Hell for all of eternity. For now, however, we must deal with his continuous mundane routines of enticement."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":21,"title":"Keeping Their Word","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, I don't know how they found this out, but I can assume that the Gibeonites were probably doing things to them to make their lives difficult. Perhaps they weren't killing the Israelites, but I like to think they were pulling some pranks on them or outright vandalizing the property of the Israelites. Over time, the Israelites started to wonder \"Why is there a leg missing from my table?\" or maybe \"How come the begs that hold down my tent keep disappearing?\" Or perhaps, \"Why does my soup taste like cow dung?\" I am just creating a narrative here (this isn't found in scripture), but I assume the Israelites found out through means that were not kind to them. Satan is a wily lot."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Put on the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil.","ref":"Ephesians 6:11"}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a HUGE mistake... The Princes made a pact with these people, and they were not about to go back on their word."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"When thou vowest a vow unto God, defer not to pay it; for he hath no pleasure in fools: pay that which thou hast vowed. Better is it that thou shouldest not vow, than that thou shouldest vow and not pay. Suffer not thy mouth to cause thy flesh to sin; neither say thou before the angel, that it was an error: wherefore should God be angry at thy voice, and destroy the work of thine hands?","ref":"Ecclesiastes 5:4-6"},{"kind":"para","text":"Man's word (back then) meant something. Because the Israelites were not to be found liars (and neither should the Christians today), they were tasked to keep their promise, even if it goes against their own best interests. They really should have spoken to the Lord about it first..."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So these people will be workers for the Israelites. Now, they could easily separate the people away from their own people, but that is not what is going to happen. They (spoiler alert) in due time will mix together with a lot of the land’s people, and that will ultimately draw the Israelites away from the Lord over and over again. It is what Satan did in the Garden of Eden, and it is (yet again) what he is doing here in the Promised Land..."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":27,"title":"A One-Sided Contract","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Satan doesn't care about how he comes into your life. He is perfectly fine with being your slave as long as he's there to make a mess of you."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There were other means of earning the trust of the Israelites. I believe the Israelites would have been a pretty giving and forgiving people had the places near them turned wholly to the Lord their God. Rahab did it. Of course, God knows the end of them, so it would have to be the Lord that would ultimately give the Israelites the right to provide safety for these people, but the Israelites didn't seek out an answer from the Lord about it. Joshua really can't be mad here because he was just as much at fault as everyone else was. He probably knew that too. He probably thought to himself, \"Why didn't I go and speak to the Lord about these people before making a decision on them? I am such a fool.\" But hey, that is the summation of all of our impatience, isn't it? We just want to have an answer and move on."},{"kind":"para","text":"I was asked not too long ago about what it was like back then when Moses prayed to the Lord. The man that asked me said, \"It's almost as if he turned around, prayed, and the Lord just gave him the answer right away, and they knew what to do.\" That is essentially what would happen. That wasn't always the case, though. Sometimes (for Moses) it took forty days and forty nights of no eating, sleeping, or drinking anything to hear from the Lord. Other times it was an instant answer, and they would just go. I think it works the same still today. God will give you an answer from His Word sometimes right away, and other times weeks (even years) in the future. You are better off waiting for the Lord’s answer on a matter and being confident and safe than to jump the gun and regret your decisions later on and not have a means of escape because of it."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Well, hey, at least they were being useful for the time being. Not that that is any sort of encouragement for their deceit. The Israelites are stuck living with the enemy. That's a pretty scary thought. While the Israelites have made a promise not to destroy them, the Gibeonites haven't made such promises. What would stop them from attacking the camp at any given moment? May that be a lesson to us today. A contract with Satan is always one-sided. You will lose more than you can give, and Satan won't give you anything in return for what you've lost."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":27}]},
    "10": {"chapter":10,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 10"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see the Israelites make yet another mistake in excepting some people from the land into their territory and to mix with their people. They don't go to the Lord for advice about the people, but they just make their own decisions based on their own ideas, and that doesn't suit them well."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, God uses their mistake to help them take some land quicker than anticipated, and this chapter shows a very crazy amount of provision from the Lord as well as miracles for the working of Israel."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"Kings Against Children","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Ai didn't seem like a very mighty city to begin with. Even judging by the name. Now, whether Ai was named this prior, or after I am not sure (it's likely named after the demolishing of it), but the noise of these battles was starting to sound throughout the rest of the cities and valleys around them, and when this city \"Jerusalem\" finds out, they start to freak out a little. Gibeon was a mighty city (apparently), and if Israel is able to make an alliance with this big, great city, there was nothing stopping them from coming after greater cities nearby. The thing is, these people knew that this was not their land. They knew this moment would come. They probably suspected it would come sooner rather than later, but it's been a very long time since any of the promises had occurred. They had been building and thriving for the better part of four hundred years now, and it seemed like the old wives’ tales were come to nothing. Israel wasn't coming... Until they did, and now it's time to worry."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are a lot of rich connections to make here to our modern society that I think is worth noting. The most prominent (and arguably the most important) is the idea that all human beings have, which is \"I have plenty of time before I need to do that.\" You come to them with the gospel message, and they say, \"I have plenty of time in my life before I need to accept Christ.\" When they don't understand the urgency of His next coming. You tell the Christian about their need to repent and turn to the Lord, and they say, \"I have plenty of time to clean myself up and get things right\" when they are not promised any other day."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Whereas ye know not what shall be on the morrow. For what is your life? It is even a vapour, that appeareth for a little time, and then vanisheth away. For that ye ought to say, If the Lord will, we shall live, and do this, or that.","ref":"James 4:14-15"},{"kind":"para","text":"Let’s take it on a smaller scale for easier understanding (there are no doubt some of you who are agreeing with these statements but are deflecting it from your own hearts). Perhaps you have an upcoming marriage anniversary coming up and you have not thought about a gift and so you say, \"I have plenty of time before I have to figure something out\" and every single man that reads this will instantly know that this is a foolish judgement to make. You likely don't have four hundred years to figure out a plan for your anniversary gift, and this generation living in Jerusalem didn't think they needed to worry about the coming of Israel at this moment either."},{"kind":"para","text":"I struggled very much to figure out this name \"Adonizedec\" because it's not established in the concordance I have. Furthermore, it doesn't seem like there's very much information on this fellow in many of the commentaries I have. So I had to do some research to figure this one out. Supposedly \"Adoni\" means \"Master of\" and \"Zedec\" means… well... \"Zedek.\" \"Zedek\" stands for \"righteousness\" which is the same word used in Hebrew that names this city \"Jerusalem.\" Interestingly enough, this same name is reminiscent of another Zedek we met in Genesis \"Melchisedec.\" A little different in the way that it's structured (obviously) but the same ending word \"Jerusalem.\" One was an image of a man of God (an Old Testament example of Christ), and the other is a self-proclaimed \"master\" of Jerusalem. Perhaps these two were related, but they certainly weren't like-minded. We will see in the lineage of Israel that the kings which will be set up later on will have children throughout their generations that will not be like-minded to their fathers..."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Hoham\" means \"whom Jehovah impels.\" Interesting name! \"Piram\" means \"Like a wild ass.\" Interesting name (for a different reason)! \"Japhia\" means \"Shining.\" Not that interesting of a name. \"Debir\" means “Sanctuary.\" And when we combine these names together, we get \"Whom Jehovah impels like a wild ass in His shining Sanctuary.\" That certainly paints a picture of a few instances in the Israelites’ journey (and that of all humankind’s journey even), but I specifically liken it to their first king’s conquest (we will study about Saul and his turbulence in 1 Samuel). As a bit of a glimpse, let’s get the first look at Saul."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the asses of Kish Saul's father were lost. And Kish said to Saul his son, Take now one of the servants with thee, and arise, go seek the asses.","ref":"1 Samuel 9:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"There's a great joke appearing here that I hope the context will give clarity to when we get here, but I find it interesting that Saul is looking for donkeys at the same time as Israel requests a king. A brother pointed out, \"Saul was tending to donkeys, and David was tending to sheep.\" Great picture. But in this context (here), the people that were currently living in these areas were the donkeys. The Israelites have made some mistakes, but they have also come back to the Lord in forgiveness and repentance. These kings and kingdoms here will not be looking for the Lord, and they won't be desirous to follow after the God of Israel."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Jerusalem\" means \"Righteousness.\" \"Hebron\" means \"association.\" \"Jarmuth\" means “heights.\" \"Lachish\" means “invincible.\" \"Eglon\" means \"calf-like.\" These places are not living up to their names at this moment. Well, perhaps Eglon is... but the rest certainly weren't! These lands will become something prominent to the Israelites, but I really want to pay attention to Lachish, Hebron, and Jerusalem (specifically) because they will be a land given to Judah as an inheritance. Lachish and Hebron will walk together (I believe that is why Hebron is named such; when we are walking in the Lord, we are borderline invincible if we are walking \"worthy\" in our association with the Lord)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I therefore, the prisoner of the Lord, beseech you that ye walk worthy of the vocation wherewith ye are called, With all lowliness and meekness, with longsuffering, forbearing one another in love; Endeavouring to keep the unity of the Spirit in the bond of peace. There is one body, and one Spirit, even as ye are called in one hope of your calling; One Lord, one faith, one baptism, One God and Father of all, who is above all, and through all, and in you all.","ref":"Ephesians 4:1-6"},{"kind":"para","text":"Jerusalem won't be given to Judah right off the bat, but it will become theirs when the kingdom is split (but that's a spoiler that we will read about later). In order to live in righteousness, though, we must associate ourselves with the invincible nature of the Lord. Let me just say, if you were to visit Jerusalem today, they are anything but \"righteous.\" Don't get me wrong, they have Christian men and women there even still today, but they are as a majority in unified hatred against Jesus Christ and His message. They will be in the New Testament, and there will be many in the last days that will still be against Him. This is why there is a need for new righteousness!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And I John saw the holy city, new Jerusalem, coming down from God out of heaven, prepared as a bride adorned for her husband. And I heard a great voice out of heaven saying, Behold, the tabernacle of God is with men, and he will dwell with them, and they shall be his people, and God himself shall be with them, and be their God. And God shall wipe away all tears from their eyes; and there shall be no more death, neither sorrow, nor crying, neither shall there be any more pain: for the former things are passed away.","ref":"Revelation 21:2-4"},{"kind":"para","text":"Right now, this is the beginning of Jerusalem, and this is where we will see how the Israelites will be led by the Lord."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":11,"title":"Israel's Next Victory","entries":[{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is kind of funny to me. I mean, Israel is tied to helping the Gibeonites, but the Gibeonites should have seen this coming. They switched sides, and it seems that the other cities weren't happy about that. So, they cash in on their oath from Israel to be taken care of by them..."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It doesn't seem as if Joshua really even asked the Lord's opinion on the matter. In fact, it seems to me that Joshua was just keeping to his end of the bargain. God makes it clear, though, that Joshua and his men have nothing to worry about, and the battle will be a piece of cake for them."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They didn't just destroy these people, they chased them out of there! These kings (perhaps) thought they had one over on Israel, but they didn't stand a chance against the God of Israel. Joshua could have gone alone with the Lord's promise and still have been prosperous."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Bethhoron\" means \"house of hollowness.\" \"Azekah\" means \"dug over.\" \"Makkedah\" means \"place of shepherds.\" So the Israelites chased them into the house of hallow, beat them in the dug over, and strew the bodies all the way over to the place of shepherds. These are all places that will be located in Judah's territory, mind you. What that means for us now? I don't know. But it's a good note to make in case it becomes something we can recognize later on and say, \"Oh, that's why that exists!\" That's usually how scripture works."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is crazy. If I were Joshua, I would have said, \"If you could have just done that this whole time, why did we have to go to battle?\" God wants us to take some pride in the getting of our land. He wants a place that is worth fighting for. If God takes care of all their battles, they won't be humbled to their land; they will be lazy to their God. I think it's important to note that when God calls us to a work (and He certainly called Joshua to it, reminding him that he will have a victory in this battle), God is always alongside us for it. It was God directing the steps of the Israelites, and it's God who protects them through the fight, and it's God who ends the remainder of the people that Israel couldn't defeat or missed."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know how big these blocks of ice were falling from the sky, but I know that baseball-sized hailstones can cause quite a lot of damage, and there's video testimony to this today. I imagine these hailstones were much bigger, and it's pretty likely that the place was covered in ice at this point. But God doesn't stop His power there..."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":14,"title":"The Miracles of God","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is crazy! Now, it's said that during this time the temperature would be about one hundred and twenty degrees in the shade, and there was likely no shade for the men to rest in. There's also some debate (it seems) as to whether or not the sun was darkened, or it just boiled them while they fought against these people. I don't know all of the answers to this, and I won't try to pretend I do. I don't think it's a far stretch to assume that the hail may have helped cool the land at the same time as the sun stood still. Perhaps the Lord provided shade for the Israelites while they were fighting here."},{"kind":"para","text":"The more interesting part to me (however) is the reference to Jasher. \"Jasher\" means \"Straight, upright, correct, or right.\" It's used once more in 2 Samuel."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"(Also he bade them teach the children of Judah the use of the bow: behold, it is written in the book of Jasher.)","ref":"2 Samuel 1:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, there are people who have taken a literal stance on this, assuming there is a book of the Bible that is not included in the Bible (this is how we get inclusions like the Book of Mormon or the Apocrypha, mind you). I believe it's more literal than that. I think when it talks about this (and it only says it twice), it is referencing the only straight and upright book the world has. It's referencing itself. It's saying, “This is not a lie. If it's written in this Holy Book, you can believe it to be literally true.” There was nothing else to compare it to at this particular moment in time. All they had were the writings of Moses, so they couldn't say, “Well, it's written in the Old Testament.\" No. They were experiencing it in their own lifetimes and they are attesting to themselves as being true because it's written in a book that can ONLY speak the truth."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I say the truth in Christ, I lie not, my conscience also bearing me witness in the Holy Ghost,","ref":"Romans 9:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"When you are truly God's people, it is impossible for you to sit comfortably in a lie. We perhaps will read this situation and think, \"There must be a misunderstanding... Did God stop the sun for Joshua? Did Joshua just command the sun to sit still on his own whim and the Lord obeyed him?\" Let's read the next verse..."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God DID listen to Joshua in this moment. He did what Joshua wanted because He knew what Joshua wanted it for. I also believe that the Lord continuously gave them strength and energy to fight the entire time the sun was up (which sounds like it was about 24 hours). Perhaps this is why we have leap years? (I'm kidding, of course.) This is certainly a very interesting thing to take place, and it should be amazing to all of us, but this is still less amazing than the fact that God would send His only Son down to die a death He didn't deserve so we can have a home we don't deserve, reserved in Heaven with the One we don't deserve to be with. That's a real miracle."}]}]},{"start":15,"end":21,"title":"Plenty of Time to Decide","entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There was no need to kill the kings in there yet. Instead, they can just sit there for a while while the Israelites take care of the rest of the people. This might sound cruel, but once again they would have never turned to the Lord. They have had over four hundred years to make their decision on whom they will follow, and they chose poorly (perhaps assuming they had more time)."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we come to Revelation and see the end of all humanity and creation as we know it, we can have the same mind then. There have been thousands of years for this world to make up their minds, and during that time it will be the worst it can possibly get. We should not feel sorry over the deaths and sufferings of pure evil in this world. There should not be shed one tear for Hitler or Stalin, or Bin Laden. There shouldn't be shed a tear over the deaths of people who massacred, mutilated, destroyed, raped, and hated so many people in a lifetime. The only thing we should be crying over is their extremely lost souls..."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It was a victorious day, and now they are just sitting and basking in the circumstances that just took place. Perhaps they were still in awe at the fact that the sun had stopped moving for so long. Maybe they were amazed at the great hail that came down and killed a great portion of the people for them. Maybe they were just amazed at the fact that they had the energy to continue for as long as they did and the fact that they were able to get rid of so many people at once now. Whatever they were contemplating, nobody said anything more that day, and there was no enemy that could even speak against the Israelites just out of sheer amazement."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":27,"title":"Dealing With the Problem","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua had a representation to give to the Israelites this day. God allowed specifically all five kings to be waiting for this moment of symbolism to the Israelites."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Hey, we are not conquering kingdoms today, but we are certainly receiving victories still! We can put our feet on the neck of our sin and say, “Fear not, don't be dismayed, be strong and of good courage because the Lord will destroy these sins that we fight against.\" Then take your weapon (your sword is God's word) and cut up the enemy, and leave its dead carcass on trees for the world to see because..."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"(for he that is hanged is accursed of God;)","ref":"Deuteronomy 21:23b"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Christ hath redeemed us from the curse of the law, being made a curse for us: for it is written, Cursed is every one that hangeth on a tree:","ref":"Galatians 3:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"When we slay our sin, we should cut the heads off so it doesn't return. Sin can come back if you just cut off the arms. It can come back if you just sever the tail. It comes back when you don't fully get rid of it."},{"kind":"para","text":"When I was younger, my dad didn't mess around with mice. He did a lot of woodworking, and the mice would chew up his wood and his products, and so he set up traps so that the mice would die! He noticed, however, that after a while the mice learned how to set off the trap without getting hurt and steal the cheese, leaving an empty trap. He eventually bought an electric trap, but half the time it didn't work. So he bought a new one (and I apologize ahead of time for the analogy, but this is the best reference I can make). This one was a very intricate design. He had tried trapping the mice before and letting them go somewhere else, but there were so many mice (especially living in the desert) that there was no end to it. This last trap had a little grinder in the middle, and the moment a mouse got to the center of it... Well, you can guess. It's sufficient to say that the mice problem was being handled. The only thing that my dad would have to do (which wasn't pleasant) was dump out the trap every once in a while. It seemed to be effective though because I didn't see the mice very often after that."},{"kind":"para","text":"I say this to say the same thing about our sin. We cannot simply wound it or slyly push it away. No, we must chop it into pieces and cast it out. God's word is the macerator for our sin, and it's sufficient to deal with even the deepest-rooted problems we might face and struggle with. God gives His children victory if we continue in the fight with Him. It's the moments we give up that God won't stop the sun for us. It's the moments that we surrender to sin that we face defeat. That is the theme of scripture. God wants to give His children victory, but we have to be willing to trust in Him and work with Him. That might take a lifetime of battle, but it's worth the fight, I promise you that."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think the kings being starved to death in that cave would have been worse than just being killed. The Israelites actually showed some pity for the kings. They kept to Gods Word on the matter of hanging, and they took down the bodies when the sun began to set."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"His body shall not remain all night upon the tree, but thou shalt in any wise bury him that day; (for he that is hanged is accursed of God;) that thy land be not defiled, which the LORD thy God giveth thee for an inheritance.","ref":"Deuteronomy 21:23"}]}]},{"start":28,"end":39,"title":"Taking More Land","entries":[{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Another place was added to their ever-growing list on the same day. It was quite a victory on this single day."}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Libnah\" means \"pavement.\" This place will also be given to Judah, and it will be used as a Levitical city for the children of Judah."}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua and Israel are on a victory high now. They are right with God, and they are right in their walk. This is the feeling that most children of faith get when they are in the right stance with the Lord. If only it lasted forever..."}]},{"start":39,"end":39,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So, as you see, they are going to the cities of the kings they had just defeated, and finishing their job and the commandment that the Lord gave to them. And they weren't taking very long to do it either. It seems they took down and destroyed every single person in each of these cities in \"two\" days. I say, \"two\" because one day was not really a day. It was an extended day. Either way, it's quite the victory for these people to take down five cities and the remaining areas all in forty-eight hours. That would be unheard of today, and if we saw that happen, we would all be in shock and confusion."}]}]},{"start":40,"end":43,"title":"A Huge Portion in Two Days","entries":[{"start":41,"end":41,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Goshen\" means \"drawing near.\" I had a really difficult time finding any information about how many square miles this was in two days. My assumption (based on very limited resources, and perhaps someone can give me a better picture and equation to this) is that they took about two thousand three hundred square miles of land in these two days! That is a lot of territory to cover. To help us understand this in math-adjacent terms, if we measured Alaska (our largest \"square\" state (and we would have to square it to make sense of this number) from east to west, it's about two thousand four hundred miles. That's about one hundred miles larger than we need, but for illustration (and ease of my own sanity) sake, I will keep two thousand four hundred. If we split that in half and square it (so that the north, south measurement was equal to the east, west measurement) we would get one thousand two hundred miles east to west, and one thousand two hundred miles north to south in Alaska (which is about how big of a territory they took)... IN TWO DAYS! Alaska is a big state, and it would be absolutely insane to see a small army of men come in and just completely demolish a quarter of the state in two days’ time. If they kept up with that pace, they could take over the entire state of Alaska in a week!"}]},{"start":43,"end":43,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They are not even nearly done with their conquest. They have only captured the southern part of the land. They still have the entire upper half (that is the west, east, and north parts) to conquer, and MORE. But we will get to the more of it later…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":39},{"start":40,"end":43}]},
    "11": {"chapter":11,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 11"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Joshua had some amazing victories against five kings who thought they could get the better of him and some of the people he was protecting. Because of this, he was able to see the Lord provide in miraculous ways that are not very commonplace today. Stopping the sun for a full day would be a very confusing thing for us to fathom."},{"kind":"para","text":"*I went on a cruise ship once to Alaska, and I was there during a time when the sun was up most of the day. This ship was huge, and so my cousin and I were consistently exploring as much as possible. We were walking around so much that the staff eventually learned our names and they would do magic tricks for us. We were walking around about the 2nd day in and we noticed that we were just extremely exhausted, but the sun seemed to still be up. I went and asked my mom what was happening, and my parents told me that the sun doesn't set in that part of Alaska except for a couple hours at a time. We somehow missed the sun setting, and it really threw us off. We had a lot of energy for the first day, but the second day we were toast, uncertain why we were feeling like this.*"},{"kind":"para","text":"The Isralites seemed to be favored with a full extra day and the energy to get through it. This is not to mention the great hail storm that killed a good portion of people, and the fact that they were able to destroy five kings at once."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, word gets out about the prosperity of the Israelites and the surrounding kingdoms don't like it. They take it upon themselves to do something about it, and try and put an end to the victory theme the Israelites were experiencing. Obviously it won't go too well for them."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"A Multitude of Enemies","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a lot of people being mentioned here. Israel has had a very good go of victory, and the kings of their day were foolish to think they would stand a chance. Again, Israel is small, and this is a very large army gathering together. It's a great picture of the end times when there will be great armies fighting against God's people, and yet God protects His people through all of it. Maybe I'm just jumping the gun. Maybe they are gathering together to turn from their wicked ways and join the Israelites in worshipping the God of creation. Let's find out in the next verse."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Jabin\" means \"whom God observes.\" \"Hazor\" means \"castle.\" My assumption is that this man \"Jabin\" was a very royal man compared to the rest of the people. This is probably why he had so much influence in unifying the surrounding cities to take up arms and join him. It is interesting that his name is \"whom God observes\" because he is living as if he is not concerned about what God sees. Also interesting that this name will come up again in Judges as another king takes over the Israelites."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the LORD sold them into the hand of Jabin king of Canaan, that reigned in Hazor; the captain of whose host was Sisera, which dwelt in Harosheth of the Gentiles.","ref":"Judges 4:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, these are different kings ( with the same names) in the same kingdom. But of course, I am spoiling some things here, so I will leave future Jabin for a study through Judges. This Jabin, however, was not seeking to make friends with the Israelites or with God's people. This Jabin wants to destroy them, and he wants to do it while God observes him. The funny thing is, he won't stand a chance fighting against the chosen of the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think it's funny when we see very precise names today. We might meet a young lady named \"Precious\" who has the worst attitude and gives off the worst impressions because she doesn't like people. She especially doesn't like God's people. She is anything but precious, but that is the name in which the parents gave her. It is possible that God can change her life to match her name, but it's quite rare for people to live up to their names these days. Apparently, it wasn't very common back then either."},{"kind":"para","text":"Set yourself in this scene here, though. They start by defeating one city, then another, then five cities, and now they have a multitude (that seems innumerable) surrounding them ready to try and destroy them. By sheer number alone, the Israelites didn't stand a chance. The fact that they were not nearly as prepared, trained, and strong as these people was enough to make them faint. They have to do something, though, because they are being surrounded."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":9,"title":"Don't Be Afraid","entries":[{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua didn't have to concern himself with the numbers of people against him. God just gave him assurance that He would take care of it. He says, \"Don't be afraid of them.\" That is a comforting sentence to hear from the Lord. It's something He will remind His children in Scripture."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And thou, son of man, be not afraid of them, neither be afraid of their words, though briers and thorns be with thee, and thou dost dwell among scorpions: be not afraid of their words, nor be dismayed at their looks, though they be a rebellious house.","ref":"Ezekiel 2:6"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But he saith unto them, It is I; be not afraid.","ref":"John 6:20"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then spake the Lord to Paul in the night by a vision, Be not afraid, but speak, and hold not thy peace:","ref":"Acts 18:9"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"In God have I put my trust: I will not be afraid what man can do unto me.","ref":"Psalm 56:11"},{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps we have experienced this whisper from the Lord before in difficult or uncertain seasons. It's something very encouraging and comforting to us when we hear the Lord give us assurance of His presence in the situation."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Zidon\" is another name for “Sidon,\" which means \"hunting.\" That's quite ironic. \"Misrephothmaim\" means \"burnings of water.\" Perhaps they were receiving some burnings of water. This is north of Palestine near Sidon. \"Mizpeh\" means \"watchtower.\" The Israelites were bringing them back to their holes. They were not expecting the Israelites to be so strong. The Israelites were not expecting to be so strong either, but they knew that the Lord was the one providing them with the strength. The enemies had no idea how the Israelites were doing this."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":15,"title":"A Huge Victory","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Remember, \"Hazor\" means \"Castle.\" It's evident by the name alone that these people thought of this place as leadership and safety. They probably didn't think anyone could stand against it. However, they learned very quickly that there's a God in Heaven who will have His way. Again, these people have had four hundred plus years to get things right with the Lord, and they have not. There is no pity for these cities who knew the promises of God and have decided to try and fight against it. The Lord observed these people and kings, and He saw the terrible leaders they made, and the ways they led their people into terrible things against the God of Heaven."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sometimes when we read scripture, we come into danger with thinking that God's promises and working was only revealed to the Israelites alone. We know this isn't true by men like Melchizedek (who we speak about in Hebrews, and we spoke about in Genesis) who just seem to come out of nowhere already following the Lord holding the promises without being in the lineage of Abraham. Or look at Pharaoh. He was warned of what was to come. He was told what the Lord wanted. He was aware of what God was looking for. He chose to go against it. These people were also aware, and they have spent their entire lives teaching their children and the following generations to live against it. We should view this in the same light as today."},{"kind":"para","text":"I have seen people weep and repeat the same old tactics to the same ol' people over and over again, begging them to take Christ. We should not want people to come to Christ because they feel like they are doing you a favor. We want them to come in genuine need of Him. I've seen people cry and plead for their loved ones to come to Christ, but they never do. Sometimes they go to the same house over and over again, day in and day out, to reach that one soul. Perhaps there is a place for them in this life. For me, I feel you have done your part, now it's time to let God do His part. If we keep going back to the soil we planted and planting new seeds, we will likely not see much grow. We need to plant, and someone else needs to water. It is, of course, GOD that gives the increase."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I have planted, Apollos watered; but God gave the increase. So then neither is he that planteth any thing, neither he that watereth; but God that giveth the increase.","ref":"1 Corinthians 3:6-7"},{"kind":"para","text":"Don't be concerned about forcing the increase. Be concerned about planting and walking away. If they don't take the seed, and nothing is able to grow, God will do what He wills with it. That is something that is difficult to come to terms with, but it's the truth. We should have compassion enough to weep for the souls of others, but we should also be wise enough to trust that God will do what He chooses with His Word."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"So shall my word be that goeth forth out of my mouth: it shall not return unto me void, but it shall accomplish that which I please, and it shall prosper in the thing whereto I sent it.","ref":"Isaiah 55:11"},{"kind":"para","text":"If the seed doesn't sprout, God has a different idea in mind. May we be content with the simplicity of planting seeds and watching God do the work after."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The cities that didn't seek to attack Joshua were left alone (at least for now). The Israelites shouldn't have had the ability to take on this many people and cities at once, but the Lord provided, and it was a great victory for them."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua took his instructions very seriously. He stuck to what he was told and commanded, and accomplished what the Lord led him to do. God always takes care of His children who are obedient and willing. Those that are not willing are not obedient, and if they are not obedient, they will sit in defeat."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":20,"title":"The Israelites Prosper","entries":[{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Goshen\" means \"drawing near.\" \"Halak\" means “smooth.\" \"Seir\" means \"hairy.\" Of course, we know that Esau was a hairy man. That is what \"Esau\" means. \"Baalgad\" means \"lord of fortune.\" That's really unfortunate for these people..."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will spend some time in our next chapter looking a little deeper into the names of these kings and places, so I don't want to take too much time here to do it. I just find the names of the places and kings fascinating because they speak a lot about the situations and thoughts of the people during this time."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There was no place that was willing to even try to turn to the Israelites except for the Hivites. For some reason, the Hivites didn't want to deal with it. Everyone else was ready to take them on, and they failed greatly. Joshua spent years taking these kings and kingdoms on, and it was increasing his leadership and faith every step of the way. The fact that this was taking so much time and the Israelites were seeing victory after victory should have been a testament for generations to come! It, however, won't take them long to forget Joshua..."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And also all that generation were gathered unto their fathers: and there arose another generation after them, which knew not the LORD, nor yet the works which he had done for Israel.","ref":"Judges 2:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"We are all guilty of falling for this same thinking as well. In fact, we probably fall for it faster than most. We see God do something amazing and miraculous in our lives, and then two weeks later it's forgotten about, and we face a new struggle and wonder where the Lord is. It's a vicious, never-ending cycle."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have talked about God hardening hearts already in the past, and we will look even deeper at it in 1 Samuel, but it's sufficient to say that God hardens the hearts of those that ask for it. He doesn't go out of His way to just spin a wheel and randomly pick whose heart He will harden today. No, these hearts are hardened from years of pushback and conscience-searing. They were purposely trying to harden their hearts away from the Lord, and so the Lord gave it to them."}]}]},{"start":21,"end":23,"title":"They Might Be Giants","entries":[{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again, we spoke about the Anakims in the book of Deuteronomy, so I won't spend too much time here. These were the people that kept the Israelites from coming into the land to begin with. \"Anakims\" in the Hebrew actually means \"long-necked.\" They were tall, and my assumption is that they had long necks. They might have been a bit freakish looking. That could be the reason that the Israelites didn't want to even deal with them."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We will see in the future (when the Philistines rise up against the Israelites) that a lot of this land still continued to grow giants of all sorts. That is likely where the giant Goliath will come from. If Joshua was successful in killing off all the giants in their land, the rest of Scripture would look very different (especially when we are introduced to David)."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua continues to split the land up to the rightful tribes, just as Moses and the Lord had commanded him. He kept to his leadership, and he did it in strides! He was a fantastic leader, and perhaps one of the greatest leaders the Israelites will ever get again (save Jesus Christ, of course). There was never more unity in Israel than at this very moment here. If only they could stay this little and on fire forever…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":23}]},
    "12": {"chapter":12,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 12"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: We have seen quite a bit of action throughout these opening chapters of Joshua. There has been a lot of amazing feats and victories that the Isrealites have experienced, and Joshua seems to think it right to take a second to recount everything that has happened so far."},{"kind":"para","text":"This chapter is a moment of breathing. I take these chapters as intentional pauses for the reader to stop and reflect the things they have currently read. Some people skip over these chapters because it's just a list of names or title of places, and there's not much significance to them here. But I like to take a moment to look upon these names and get an overall picture of what the people were like back then or what the places might have looked like. My goal (as we study) is to try and get the reader as personally there as possible (without of course actually being there because we can't do that)."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":6,"title":"Outside the Land","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This chapter might seem a little stagnant compared to the others because we are getting a list of names and locations, but I like to take these chapters and break them down into definitions (it helps us to remember what they are doing and the kind of people they were up against). From the river Arnon to Mount Hermon is about one hundred and forty miles in a straight line. One hundred and forty miles doesn't seem like much, but when you are battling kings and taking down whole cities and armies, it's quite a bit. They didn't seem to take too much time doing it either."},{"kind":"para","text":"This was on the \"other side\" of the Jordan. So this took place in their forty-year wilderness trek. From here forward, however, it seems that they are establishing themselves in this new land, and it seems to take a lengthier amount of time while trying to take more land. Their conquest, sadly, will end with Joshua, and they will have a hard time following the lead of Moses on their own. But this is the land they have at the moment, and these are the people they had to face to receive that land..."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think it's safe to point out what Amorite means first, so we get a just picture of the type of people living in the land. \"Amorite\" means \"a sayer.\" Perhaps they liked to spread a lot of gossip and lies. We know the Lord was allowing them their work though, because they are mentioned as far back as the book of Genesis. In fact, God says this about them..."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But in the fourth generation they shall come hither again: for the iniquity of the Amorites is not yet full.","ref":"Genesis 15:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"It's evident that God waited for quite a long time for the Amorites to fulfill their wickedness, and here they are, still doing what they can against the Lord. Perhaps they were good at enticing people away from the truth of God? I don't really know. Just like \"Israel\" (which if you don't remember means \"Wrestles with God\") lives up to their name and the way they walk in this life, these other countries and cities dwelling close by are also very much like their cultural name."},{"kind":"para","text":"Today, if we point out people based on their culture, they tend to get upset and call it \"cultural appropriation,\" eventually noting you as a racist and a bigot. The truth of the matter is, people are influenced by their culture. It's not a stab or a racist remark to point out the evidence that people say and do the things they say and do based on the influences around them. Let me point the finger at myself for a moment. My dad suffered from immense anger problems. It would not have been rare for him to break things and throw things because he was angry. But his father was even worse than that. My grandpa would make my dad and his sisters fistfight each other if they were not getting along. You can obviously point out the fact that my father has anger problems because of the culture he was brought up in. His parents entrenched that into him."},{"kind":"para","text":"Does this mean that people can't change? Absolutely not! The gospel message is all about that change. Actually, I could argue that the ONLY way a person can change is when they fall in love with their Savior. But it is quite evident that if they don't reach out to the Savior, they will likely not change. Or at least they won't act any differently than their culture. That's why the Bible is very specific about Christian parents training their children!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Train up a child in the way he should go: and when he is old, he will not depart from it.","ref":"Proverbs 22:6"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And, ye fathers, provoke not your children to wrath: but bring them up in the nurture and admonition of the Lord.","ref":"Ephesians 6:4"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Correct thy son, and he shall give thee rest; yea, he shall give delight unto thy soul.","ref":"Proverbs 29:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"It's sufficient to say that when you have been saved, you should no longer be bringing up your children in a worldly culture, but in a Heavenly one. Of course, those that reject the Lord will continuously indwell in the world's culture and then complain because you are not bending to their idea of good and right (mostly because their idea of what is good is a steaming pile of evil). In this time, the Israelites were tasked with taking down the \"worldly culture\" around them. Why? Because God doesn't want His children falling into the evils of that culture."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we follow God, we have no evils in a Heavenly culture. We know what's right and true. When we don't, we mix what is good up with what is evil and end up doing things that will impact the world around us in heinous and terrible ways. We still have the same mission as the Israelites today. We may not be killing the lands around us, but we certainly are requested of the Lord to block the culture from coming into our homes."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Sihon\" means \"Warrior.\" I'm sure people looked to Sihon as the greatest fighter this world had to offer. There was probably no one on this planet that could ever come close to his skill and strength. Sihon didn't seem to be too much for the Lord to handle, however. \"Heshbon\" means \"stronghold.\" \"Aroer\" means \"ruins.\" \"Arnon\" means \"rushing stream.\" It is evident, then, that in putting these places together, we get an interesting picture of what this place might have looked like. Sihon (the warrior) was protecting his ruins from his stronghold near the rushing stream. He was stationed to also protect half of Gilead over to Jabbok."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Gilead\" means \"Rocky region.\" \"Jabbok\" means \"emptying.\" Apparently, the property line stops there because that territory belonged to the children of Ammon. \"Ammon\" means \"tribal.\" The people probably lived a little differently here. They might have been extremely loyal to their land and their people, and their form of governing might have been a little more strict. Either way, they were responsible for their land after that, whereas the Amorites took care of the portion they possessed at the time."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Chinneroth\" means \"harps.\" I suspect there was a lot of music played here. Just taking a guess. \"Bethjeshimoth\" means \"house of the desolation.\" Perhaps there wasn't much to look at in this land. Or maybe it seemed like a lost cause because the people were just living in extreme poverty. Either way, the picture painted of this land is sad. \"Ashdothpisgah\" means \"slopes of Pisgah.\" \"Pisgah\" means \"cleft.\" “Slopes of cleft\" is probably better accurate to the place that's pictured here. Maybe it was on the lower part of Pisgah. Or maybe it's a place that was pulled away as part of Pisgah. A \"cleft\" is a place that is divided into two. There must have either been an issue with the people there, or some sort of Earthly event took place that caused them to be separated."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Og\" means \"long-necked.\" \"Bashan\" means \"fruitful.\" It seems there were a lot of long-necked giant people living in this land. \"Ashtaroth\" means “star.\" \"Edrei\" means \"goodly pasture.\" These two places must have been quite a beautiful land to look upon. \"Hermon\" means “a sanctuary.\" There seems to be a lot of debate about what this place was actually called. Moses calls it Hermon, likely because the people around it felt safe in that mount. They saw it as their haven from battle and this world."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"(Which Hermon the Sidonians call Sirion; and the Amorites call it Shenir;) All the cities of the plain, and all Gilead, and all Bashan, unto Salchah and Edrei, cities of the kingdom of Og in Bashan.","ref":"Deuteronomy 3:9"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Sirion\" means “breastplate.\" \"Shenir\" means \"snow mountain.\" Another beautiful place (no doubt) to look upon. \"Salcah\" means \"Migration.\" \"Geshurite\" means \"proud beholder.\" \"Maachathite\" means \"Pressure.\" It literally means \"She has pressed.\" Who is she that pressed? I don't know. There is mention of a \"Maacah\" in Genesis who was born through the concubine of Abraham's brother Nahor. If you read through the names accounted for there, it doesn't seem that Nahor's concubine was living a very pleasant life. Perhaps she had a hard time birthing this child, or maybe this child was treated as the least in this family."},{"kind":"para","text":"Either way, my thoughts are that these people have a very hardened backstory that Scripture doesn't seem to give too much detail in. They lived a pressing life through every generation. \"Half of Gilead\" is likely the other half that the Amorites were not protecting because they had their own land to protect."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Reubenites, the Gadites, and the half tribe of Manasseh took this land for themselves. This was not in the land that was promised to them by the way. The Reubenites, the Gadites, and half of Manasseh wanted that land in their wilderness journey. Moses promised them the land if they were willing to go and fight for their brothers (that is, the other tribes) when they crossed the Jordan. We read briefly about this in the early parts of Deuteronomy, and they have the land for themselves here just like Moses had promised."},{"kind":"para","text":"Interestingly enough (and I won't hang on this detail because we will talk about all of this when we get into Revelation in the future here), Reuben, Gad, and Manasseh are featured tribes in the one hundred and forty-four thousand sealed in the end. That small bit of information isn't extremely significant at the moment, but what is significant is the fact that they wanted to stay in the land OUTSIDE of the promise. It is said that God's children would want to live outside of God's provision. They want to make it on their own, and they find out the hard way that God has made us for community. We need a place and people to grow with."},{"kind":"para","text":"If we choose to stay outside of the community God has provided for us, we only inflict damage on ourselves. This is coming from the heart of someone who has been in that line of business before. There was no greater pain in my life than to be and feel neglected by the community of the Lord, and that is not a place that I would strive anyone to belong to."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":24,"title":"The Promised Portion","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These are the places we have been reading about in the past 10 chapters. This is a quick recap of how that journey has gone so far and what progress they have been making. In order to make this more streamlined and better to be managed, I will give definitions of each place, but I will focus primarily on the places of significance. These will be places that will be brought up again in later chapters that we might want to keep in a storage box in our brains. We should pay attention to their movements, because while Israel and their story is unfolding, these places are mentioned and moving in the background, and will be useful either as examples later on, or as growing points for Israel."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Jericho\" means \"its moon.\" That seems a bit like an understatement. Jericho has been a prominent place for us since the beginning of this book, but it's not much more to look at today. The story of Jericho and its walls will be useful examples in the future, and should provide some encouragement and strength for us today, but as far as the city goes, there's not much more that comes of it. We will see it rear its head every now and then, but it's really more of just a reference. Really, the only remaining people of Jericho are Rahab and whoever was in her household at that time. That is really the only reason that Jericho will continue to be mentioned. \"Ai\" means \"heap of ruins.\""}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Jerusalem\" means \"teaching of peace.\" Of course, Jerusalem will be a very important reference throughout Scripture. It's still important today. There is not much peace in Jerusalem now, though. Granted, this is largely due to its current conflict, but I also believe it has to do with its continuous rejecting of the Lord and His might. That conversation can be saved for a different setting, though."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Hebron\" means \"Association.\" We will see this place being brought up quite a bit through 1 & 2 Samuel (as well as the connected books like Chronicles and Kings)."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Jarmuth\" means \"heights.\" Perhaps this was a place on a mountain top. Or maybe they just had tall buildings? \"Lachish\" means \"invincible.\" Which is a bit ironic."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Eglon\" means \"calf-like.\" \"Gezer\" means \"portion.\""}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Debir\" means \"sanctuary\" \"Geder\" means \"wall.\""}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Hormah\" means \"devotion.\" \"Arad\" means \"a wild ass.\""}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Libnah\" means \"pavement.\" \"Adullam\" means \"justice of the people.\""}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Makkedah\" means \"place of shepherds.\" \"Bethel\" means \"house of God.\" A place that will be prominent in Scripture. I am going out on a limb to assume that this place likely wasn't called \"Bethel\" before Joshua took it. It was renamed to this later on (perhaps by Joshua himself)."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Tappuah\" means \"the apple city.\" I wonder what they were growing here? \"Hepher\" means \"a well.\""}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Aphek\" means \"enclosure.\" \"Lasharon\" means \"plain, or level.\""}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Madon\" means \"strife.\" It's likely that this place was not a very fun place to dwell in. I'm sure they continued to live up to their name here. \"Hazor\" means \"castle.\""}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Shimronmeron\" means \"watch-height of Meron.\" I guess these people watched over Meron. This next one is funny... \"Achshapah\" means \"I shall be bewitched.\" I haven't the slightest clue what was happening here, but there must have been a good deal of witchcraft in their land for the place to be named this."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Taanach\" means \"sandy.\" \"Megiddo\" means \"a place of crowds.\" Fitting for this name. Another location that will be mentioned again in scripture quite a bit. There will be a great battle that will likely take place around here."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Kedesh\" means \"holy place.\" \"Jokneam\" means \"the people lament.\" \"Carmel\" means \"garden-land.\" Carmel will be a well-established place as we will read. We have a Carmel here in California. If you ever go there, it seems quite dead and quiet. Very green, and often rainy, but not much happening there. I suppose if you wanted to walk around for some peace and quiet, you can do that there. The streets are somewhat empty, and there's not much to see other than some quaint, rustic-looking shops and homes. I view it as a great place to retire. Our Carmel is a bit of a \"garden-land\" as well, and that is perhaps the reason they named this place as such."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Dor\" means \"generation.\" \"Gilgal\" means \"a wheel, rolling.\" Gigal will be a place to watch out for as we continue to read."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Tirzah\" means \"favourable.\" I am guessing that this land was a land that most people thought was worth fighting for. It will be brought up a few more times throughout the Old Testament. Thirty-one kings, though! That's a lot of kings, even by today’s standards, for one small starting and seemingly insignificant people to kill and take land from. Whether you believe in the Bible or not, if the things that are written here are correct (and historically they are argued to be provenly correct), then these people were either elite ninja assassins, or they had a very powerful leader directing and guiding them. Imagine Israel doing this today. Our world would be devastated."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":24}]},
    "13": {"chapter":13,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 13"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Joshua gives a quick recap of the land they were able to take, and how they got to this point in the book of Joshua. They seemed to have experienced quite a lot in their conquesting (as well as their time in the wilderness), and so there was plenty to recount. However, the Lord was not finished with them yet."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, God points out that Joshua is getting to a place that will make it difficult for the Israelites to move forward, and so Joshua takes this opportunity to split the land up appropriately before it's too late and he has no more time to do it."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":12,"title":"Dividing Land","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems we have come to the latter part of Joshua's years. We have twenty-four chapters in this book, and so far we are only on chapter 13. This means that the remaining chapters will be primarily focused on the last parts of Joshua's life. The Lord makes a valid point to Joshua, that none of the Israelites really thought about. Joshua is old! When Moses explained the task of taking the land to the Israelites, they (perhaps) thought that all of the victory, accomplishments, and goals would be completed with Joshua as their leader. According to God, they didn't even put a dent in what they were supposed to have. They were promised much more than what Joshua was capable of leading the people to take. Now it's up to Joshua to gear the men towards receiving what was left."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"For time-appropriate acuity (this might help us get a rough idea of where we are currently in the history of Israel), I want to give my best estimate of what Joshua has covered, how much left he has to cover, and how old he might be at this point (how many years it took him to get here). With that said, there is not a lot of information about the lengths of time it took for each takeover. I assume it wasn't something that happened very quickly. If it was, Joshua and the Israelites would have already taken over these lands of the Canaanites, and the Gibeonites and (as we will soon see), the land of the Sidonians."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So let’s see if we can get an accurate (somewhat anyway) depiction of Joshua's age and the next plan of attack. First, let's look at how much land they were able to take. According to a few findings on various parts of online searches, commentaries, and (direct conversation with men smarter than me) research, the approximate amount of land that was taken and divided was about thirty thousand square miles. Associate that with the potentially promised land of three hundred thousand square miles, and you get about a tenth of the land possessed. The Israelites still had a lot of work to do to receive their full promise."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will find, however, that they don't do much more work, and the sad part is, it will take some time before taking anything else over. We'll find in scripture that the land fluctuates depending on their circumstances and leading of the Lord, but for the most part, they stay consistently small throughout history. It's a viable lesson for us today."},{"kind":"para","text":"We sometimes are promised by the Lord some great and powerful things, only to be content with the little that we were able to accomplish at the beginning. We never fully delve into the power and prosperity of the Lord because we don't want to put any more effort into it. We have at least 10%, why fight for any more? We miss out on the prominent promises of God because we limit His ability through us with the contention of what little we have. Don't get me wrong, we should be content with what God has given us, but we should also be expectant that God will do even more if we are willing."},{"kind":"para","text":"Even in Joshua's old age, God will do plenty more with him than anyone thought possible. If we go back through the first five books of the Bible, we see that God is very generous with His promise to the Israelites about the land promised to them."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"In the same day the LORD made a covenant with Abram, saying, Unto thy seed have I given this land, from the river of Egypt unto the great river, the river Euphrates: The Kenites, and the Kenizzites, and the Kadmonites, And the Hittites, and the Perizzites, and the Rephaims, And the Amorites, and the Canaanites, and the Girgashites, and the Jebusites.","ref":"Genesis 15:18-21"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And I will set thy bounds from the Red sea even unto the sea of the Philistines, and from the desert unto the river: for I will deliver the inhabitants of the land into your hand; and thou shalt drive them out before thee.","ref":"Exodus 23:31"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then your south quarter shall be from the wilderness of Zin along by the coast of Edom, and your south border shall be the outmost coast of the salt sea eastward: And your border shall turn from the south to the ascent of Akrabbim, and pass on to Zin: and the going forth thereof shall be from the south to Kadeshbarnea, and shall go on to Hazaraddar, and pass on to Azmon: And the border shall fetch a compass from Azmon unto the river of Egypt, and the goings out of it shall be at the sea.","ref":"Numbers 34:3-5"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Every place whereon the soles of your feet shall tread shall be yours: from the wilderness and Lebanon, from the river, the river Euphrates, even unto the uttermost sea shall your coast be.","ref":"Deuteronomy 11:24"},{"kind":"para","text":"So on… God doesn't just tell them to fight for the land (though they had to do something about the intruders in the land). He tells them that the land is freely GIVEN to them, and they choose not to take it. Now they have a small portion, and they must utilize the little they have."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So we have nine and a half tribes who are given land. These tribes are: Judah, Simeon, Benjamin, Dan, Ephraim, Zebulun, Issachar, Asher, Naphtali, and the half tribe of Manasseh. Two and a half tribes receive the land on the east side of Jordan (that is Reuben, Gad, and the other half of the tribe of Manasseh (which is land that was not on the proper side of the river (we have spoken on this several times already)), and the rest of the tribes splitting up the land on the west side of the river (the appropriate side). The only tribe remaining (other than the other half of Manasseh) is the tribe of Levi. We should well know (at this point) that the Levites were explicitly told that they would not inherit land. We will be reminded of that in a moment."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":23,"title":"God Gifts Accordingly","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They messed some things up, and we saw them do that on several occasions right off the get-go of coming into the land. This could be the reason why a good portion of them thought it was sufficient to just accept the land they already had and not have to worry about taking more. The Geshurites and the Maachathites might have been quite persuasive if they had had the inclination to continue further. They might have whispered in their ears, \"I don't know, they are a pretty powerful land, your leader is kind of old, you might not be able to take them like you could before.” The Israelites seemed to fail to realize that it was not the leadership of Joshua that they were dependent upon. They failed to understand that same thing with the leadership of Moses as well. It was not human authority they needed. It was God's authority, and it's something they will reject over and over again until Christ comes to make it clear (though there are plenty who still reject the authority and relationship with the Lord today)."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Levites were a ministerial tribe. They were a tribe that was to be the focal point of people following the leading of God rather than the leading of God's human instruments. They don't seem to understand that though, and I feel that over time they get a bit bitter at their tribal association, assuming that God is not giving them a goodly place. The truth of the matter is, the Levites had a far better inheritance than the rest of the tribes. Whether they saw it or not, God was their inheritance, and if God be our inheritance today, we don't have to worry about what we get here on Earth. If we have the Lord as our inheritance, then whatever we get (or don't get) while we are here, living on this planet, is a comfort to us because we understand that it's up to the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"I, perhaps, will step on toes with this statement, but I assure you that I don't mean any ill-will behind it. I've heard people say in the past, \"God will not lavish you with gifts from above. You actually have to put forth effort if you want God to gift you.\" I don't believe this is true. I don't want it to be misconstrued. God loves His children that put forth the effort to serve, but that doesn't negate His free gifts to us. In the same way we receive salvation, I believe is the same way in which the Lord provides any other gift to His children. By simple faith, God can do all things. That is the picture I've gotten from reading scripture. The disciples continuously worried about food, yet Christ continued to feed them without them working to get it. The Israelites continuously worried about leadership, yet God continued to send leaders to them to give them direction. I believe if the Israelites were to stop moving forward in their efforts (even in this book of Joshua) and just went to the Lord in faith, God would provide the victory to them without them lifting a finger. We will see God do this for them over and over again in scripture, and yet we have people who preach that God will not work unless you work. Work is not our moving factor (like the Levites grew to believe in their religion). Faith is what moves the Lord."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But without faith it is impossible to please him: for he that cometh to God must believe that he is, and that he is a rewarder of them that diligently seek him.","ref":"Hebrews 11:6"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"(For we walk by faith, not by sight:)","ref":"2 Corinthians 5:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"Does this mean we should not do any sort of service for the Lord? Of course not. If we say we love the Lord and we don't have an unction to work in service for Him, we certainly do not love the Lord like we claim. Faith moves us into action, rather than action moving us into faith. Faith without action is abusive of God's gifts to you. Action without faith is abusive of your gifts to God. James labels it out the best way possible."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Yea, a man may say, Thou hast faith, and I have works: shew me thy faith without thy works, and I will shew thee my faith by my works.","ref":"James 2:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"Action through faith is a good representation that God is working through your life. Faith without action is a good representation that you don't love the Lord like you should. With that said, I believe there are times when we don't have to lift a finger in order to see God's working. All we must have is faith. When we have faith, God will determine if we should be involved with His working or not. We, then, must be sensitive and ready for what He wants to do with us next."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Reuben got a lot of land. This was all promised to them because of their desire for that land while they were in their wilderness journey. Why they would want that land rather than the land that Moses was gearing them for, I don't know. Perhaps the grass was green and the sky was blue there. I think it's a lot like the depiction of Abraham and Lot’s choice between the two lands when they decided to split up. Lot chooses the land that has the most green in it, and gives the desert to Abraham."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":28,"title":"The Land of Gad","entries":[{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Gad is living closely to the Reubenites. Yet again, it was their own personal decision to take these parts. This was their portion."}]}]},{"start":29,"end":31,"title":"The Land of Manasseh","entries":[{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, I can't say for sure how much land this is for them to split up. I tried to find some sort of answer to this in multiple settings and commentaries, but there is no answer actually found. All I could come up with was \"several thousand miles\" worth of land. Perhaps it was more land than the Israelites took on the other side of the Jordan? Either way, the land was taken first with Moses, and then these three tribes seemed to think that this land was worth giving up the promise over. This doesn't mean they have left the promise (that is a study for a different time, no doubt), but it means they are disconnected from the unity that God intended in the beginning here (that is something we will see towards the end of this study)."},{"kind":"para","text":"They maybe were uncertain about whether or not they would actually get to have that land when the time comes that they cross the Jordan. So they staked their claim right then and there and took the land that they already knew was vacant. I'll tell you, faith is a gamble. The older I get, the more hesitant I like to gamble with my faith. You'd think it would be the opposite, but my flesh turns everything backwards. When I was first saved, I gambled everything for my faith. I didn't give two thoughts about it. In so doing, I have run into a lot of trials and burdens because I did not account for the repercussions of gambling like this. If you are going to do anything by faith, you will always have pushback and conflict. Someone once said, “Nothing moves forward without friction.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"Though there is conflict, and though there is pushback, the greatest times of my life were found when I just moved forward in faith without knowing where it would lead. I might be a little more contemplative in my faith (and perhaps we all can be to some level), but I won't let the burdens keep me from moving me forward in trust to the Lord. These tribes experienced a lot of hurt and trials in the wilderness, and so they didn't want to gamble too much further with their faith. The land was there, and it was already a sure thing. Maybe they had more land on that side of the Jordan than they were able to take in the conquest after crossing. Even if we were provided with more, we miss out on the provisions of the Lord, and the unity that His promises can lead if we are able to just gamble a little more in our trust with Him."},{"kind":"para","text":"I am not a gambling man (and I don't advise anyone to partake in gambling when we are commissioned to be good stewards of God's gifts to us), but when we gamble on the Lord's leading, we are betting on a sure thing that will pay off no matter what. It might not be instant, but it will be worth it, even if you lose it all up front."}]}]},{"start":32,"end":33,"title":"The Levites Role","entries":[{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I find it interesting that Joshua ends this chapter with this thought here. Why did Joshua mention this again? We get the picture, Levi doesn't get land! I think it's important to note this here because these two and a half tribes were not in the land that they were promised. The Levites don't have a \"land\" promised to them either. The Levites (because of their promise in the Lord) were set as representations of how the rest of the tribes were meant to be. The Levites (without a land promised to them) could continue to unite the tribes, even though two and a half of them are on the wrong side of the river. The Levites had that power! That power never really reaches its full potential (however), and we see even the Levites fall into the same trap as the rest of the world in those days. That is the essence of this chapter. It illustrates that the children of God never dig deep enough into the power of God so that they can prosper by the Will of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don't want to forget where we started in this chapter. I mentioned that we will dig deeper into the age and stretch of time that this all took place. This is a hugely debated topic, and I certainly don't want to get this wrong (or pretend like I know what I’m talking about). Joshua will eventually die at one hundred and ten years old (spoiler alert). He's at an old age now, so I assume these last parts of this book are written with a large gap pretty close to that time and age. The first half of this book is likely a little earlier on. Most scholars argue that the conquest took about seven years in total. From the assumption I get in this story (and that is really all it can be because we have no final authority on the matter), I believe it took a little longer than that. I couldn't see Joshua coming in, taking the land, and then just sitting for a certain amount of years before he decided to split up the land. Either that, or he was already pretty old when he started to take the land, and those seven years were all it took for God to say, \"Hey, I think you're just getting too old to do this now.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"According to some historical findings (as well as biblical date raking), it is thought that Joshua was about 69-79 years old when they first entered the promised land. This is actually based on the next chapter of Joshua."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Forty years old was I when Moses the servant of the LORD sent me from Kadeshbarnea to espy out the land; and I brought him word again as it was in mine heart.","ref":"Joshua 14:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"Caleb and Joshua were likely around the same age when this happened, and it would have taken them about forty years and some change to get to this point. So let's assume that Joshua was seventy-nine (that's about as high as I would go in age without any affirmative promise). He would now be around eighty-six if it took them seven years of conquest. While we have a good portion of Joshua left, I don't believe Joshua sat around for the next twenty-four years just dictating land. Obviously, there is some time spent in-between there that is not accounted for, and I believe that their conquest had to have taken a little more time than seven years. If I had to guess, I would assume it took closer to seventeen years rather than seven, but this is just an assumption."},{"kind":"para","text":"Either way, there was plenty more for the Israelites to take, and now it's up to Joshua (in his older age) to get them into a position where they will continue after his death. The dividing of the land is not here to make them comfortable. It's here to establish that they are rightful owners of it. From here, it is still up to the Israelites to walk by faith and accept the gift that the Lord has provided for them. We find in Judges (however) that they forget about Joshua, and in the end, forget about the faith of their leaders as well…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":33}]},
    "14": {"chapter":14,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 14"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we talked about how Joshua decided to divide the land up for the children of Isreal to reside in. He made it known that God will gift the people according to His promises, and He will keep his promises. The last chapter gave specifics about each of the inherited land, and the people that would inherit those areas."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see God continue to keep promises by giving to Caleb what Caleb deserved. At this point, they are all pretty old, and they are trying to establish some things before any of them die from old age."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":5,"title":"The Land Divided","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We've spoken about this quite a bit since the book of Deuteronomy. The land was made to be divided up after they had inherited it, and they still haven't finished taking what they were promised. They seemed so excited to take the land and split it up that they are just doing it now. Perhaps it was done specifically because Joshua was up there in age, and he was worried they wouldn't do it if he were gone. Up to this point, I don't think Joshua has thought about the leadership when he's gone. I think he's confident that they will just stick to trusting the Lord as their leader, so he hasn't really focused on anyone else to take his place."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They stuck to what they promised the Lord to do. They didn't waver or deviate from the plans that God set forth for them, and they were doing quite successfully (for now). The Levites don't own any land, and they were satisfied with that, but they still needed places to stay and so they were given cities to dwell in and cattle to hold them over. The rest of their provision would have to come from the tribes around them or from the Lord directly."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":12,"title":"Caleb's Promise","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Caleb was a servant from the get-go. This is why he (and Joshua) got a part in this land, and it's why they didn't die with the rest of their ancestors in the wilderness. They got to be a part of the promise because they trusted in the Lord for the victory. Everyone else seemed to reject the Lord's leading in nearly every matter except for Caleb and Joshua. The promise to Caleb was that he and his descendants would possess the land (Numbers 13-14)."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So for those keeping score (or wondering what the math is here to \"fourscore and five years old“), Caleb is saying, \"I am eighty-five, and I'm still in peak condition.” The Lord has led this man to have a forty-year-old mindset in an eighty-five-year-old body. Now, Joshua was known to be old at this point in time because the last chapter insinuated it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now Joshua was old and stricken in years; and the LORD said unto him, Thou art old and stricken in years, and there remaineth yet very much land to be possessed.","ref":"Joshua 13:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"So if Joshua was old, Caleb is certainly old. Yet Caleb feels like he could take more land at his ripe young age of eighty-five. I'd like to preface that Caleb never relied on his own strength for battle. It is more likely meant that Caleb was ready for battle because his faith was just as strong as it was before. God was just as good, and God could just as easily give the victory in an eighty-five-year-old man as He can with a forty-year-old man. That is still the case today. It is God that should be depended on for the battle, and it will be God that gets His children through to victory."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The place spoken about here is \"Hebron\" which means \"fellowship\" or “Association.\" It is a place that is mentioned quite a bit in scripture, starting in Genesis with Abraham."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then Abram removed his tent, and came and dwelt in the plain of Mamre, which is in Hebron, and built there an altar unto the LORD.","ref":"Genesis 13:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is the place where Caleb wants to rest. He found fellowship with the Lord here, and so it suits him as his place of refuge (specifically because it was the giants in the land that kept the Israelites from crossing to begin with). Caleb knows (and is assured) that God will provide this land for him. It was part of the promise!"}]}]},{"start":13,"end":15,"title":"A Promise Kept","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The land is given to Caleb, and it was given to him (perhaps) because he was getting up there in age as well. He needed a place to settle down, and he wanted to rest a little easier. We know that he still had the energy to continue on, and I believe if he didn't receive the promise of this land, Caleb would have lived forever until he did. God always keeps His promises, and the promise of a land for Caleb (though made by Moses, who was a mouthpiece for the Lord) was going to be kept. We can bet any money that God will keep His promise to us in our life."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know about you, but God has given me some functions in this life that have made me undone. He has given me promises and desires, and has pushed me in positions that (often) confuse me. He has put me in ministry, taken me out of ministry, given me a desire for ministry, closed off ministry, put one foot into ministry, rejected me from ministry, opened up ministry... I have had a whirlwind of emotions from the Lord. Yet, through it all, I know He will keep His promise for me. I have cried to the Lord in prayer for direction, and for openings, and even for desires to be abated, and God has answered one way or another. He has shown me direction nearly every time, but (more often than not) waiting for the Lord's direction takes some time. His promises will be kept, but it's often agonizing waiting for them. Caleb didn't receive his promise from the Lord until he was quite old. Sometimes we might not receive our promise until we are up there in age as well…"}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Caleb got his land and remained in fellowship with the Lord. After he received the land, it seems they experienced rest. This (if you don't remember) was the same place in which Sarah passed away."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Sarah died in Kirjatharba; the same is Hebron in the land of Canaan: and Abraham came to mourn for Sarah, and to weep for her.","ref":"Genesis 23:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"It's a heartbreaking account (to be sure), but it's one that will always be preserved in the oracles of God's history. I've noticed (lately) that history seems to be changing. People are making up wacky new tales of the early life of humans that don't make any sense and don't add up to anything. I feel like most people are doing it to be trolls. Their end goal is to prove that they can just make up whatever they want, and people will believe it. Those that know the truth (however) are not so easily given over to lies."},{"kind":"para","text":"The truth is, God's Word will never change. People can try and change it all they want, but they won't ever be able to do it with accuracy because there is only ONE truth. They can make up a Bible version like the \"Queen James\" Bible and call it \"truth\" by altering the context, but at the end of the day, they will never be accurate. The truth MUST be accurate in order for it to be true."},{"kind":"para","text":"Let me read you the description of the \"Queen James\" Bible really quick: \"The Queen James Bible is based on the King James Bible, edited to resolve interpretive ambiguity and prevent homophobic misinterpretation.\" So their goal (in this version) is to edit the \"King James\" Bible (which is odd to me why they wouldn't edit a more modern English version, but I digress), so that it can fit their agenda, rather than changing THEIR agenda to what the Bible has already said. If that doesn't describe the world’s history..."},{"kind":"para","text":"The world’s history can change from right to left at the drop of a dime, but God's history cannot. In fact, that is what history is. It's HIS story. God's Word will be true, no matter how many times our current culture tries to change it…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":15}]},
    "15": {"chapter":15,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 15"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we see that Caleb and Joshua are up there in age at this point, and they want to establish how to split up the land for the people before it is too late and the people fight over who gets what. If Joshua takes the charge here, then no one will have to question what they think they are owed, they will simply know that it belongs to them. Of course, every tribe is to work together, but there will be some issues in the future if the land isn't properly divided at this point and everyone is left to fend for themselves. We also got a little more information about Caleb, and what he desired for the land that he was promised."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter we see the land that Caleb and the children of Judah get to take. This is a list of the names of all the cities that belonged to them, and it's a chapter that I think a lot of people skim through because they don't want to take the time to address some of these difficult names. I want to do my best to make the most out of reading these names, and so I hope to paint a picture with these cities that might be beneficial when we are making our decisions in this life."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":12,"title":"The Land Given","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It might seem like a strange thing that we are only getting a look at the land provided to the children of Judah at this particular point in time. The land is split up in this way (for now) specifically because our last chapter focused on Caleb (who came from this tribe). We will find (later on) that Israel will be split up in a terrible fashion, and the children of Judah will be the ones that take a little more time in their downfall. The rest of the tribes will largely fall away from the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"Of course, at the time of this writing, they didn't know that was going to take place. It is also the tribe in which our Lord will come from (we are not left in doubt of this (both Matthew and Luke give the lineage accounts)). I think it's sufficient enough to start with this tribe anyway, because Caleb is an interesting man whom we don't know all that much about."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now in these situations I would usually provide definitions of each place which would give you a bit of a picture of what the places might have looked like (or at least why they might have been called these names). I won't be doing this in this portion of the chapter for a few reasons. Firstly, because we have already defined some of these locations, and it would not do us all that good to keep defining the same places over and over. Secondly, there are a lot of places mentioned here for these borders, and I don't want to just give another list of definitions and names for something we can have a physical picture of (It's beneficial in some settings, and in this one I feel it's better to depict it physically rather than by word). Thirdly, I know we will see these places make mention again further along in scripture, and those are the better times to dive a little deeper into these areas."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think it's sufficient to provide some sort of picture for you so you can see what the land looks like, and make your assessment from there (plus we will get into the definition of the cities that come out of this land, towards the end of this chapter anyway)."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":19,"title":"Caleb Takes Land","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Caleb was promised a specific land, and that is the land which is provided to him. The promise is kept because he kept his side of the promise. I believe if Caleb had given up just a few months shy of when he was supposed to, there would be a different account given in this section of scripture. Caleb is getting up there in age, and he is still as fit as a fiddle."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"As yet I am as strong this day as I was in the day that Moses sent me: as my strength was then, even so is my strength now, for war, both to go out, and to come in.","ref":"Joshua 14:11"},{"kind":"para","text":"I can only assume that Caleb was in such good condition at his age simply because the Lord continued to sustain him. The Lord gives His children the strength and spirit they need to do what He has called them into."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He giveth power to the faint; and to them that have no might he increaseth strength. Even the youths shall faint and be weary, and the young men shall utterly fall: But they that wait upon the LORD shall renew their strength; they shall mount up with wings as eagles; they shall run, and not be weary; and they shall walk, and not faint.","ref":"Isaiah 40:29-31"}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So even serving the Israelites for as long as he had been, and at the age he says he is at this time, he is still provided with victory as he goes to set up and establish his homeland. No doubt, these men were powerful, but the Lord was faithful and Caleb had no issue doing what needed to be done (and what everyone knew they couldn't fight against). It is quite humorous to me (or at least the humor is not lost on me) that there were any cities willing to try and put up a fight against the Israelites at this point. They knew they didn't stand a chance, yet there was a little voice in their minds that said \"Maybe just try and stop them.” It could be that they knew about those small cases where the other cities had won, but they were few and far between. They should have realized at this point that they stood no chance of defeating these people and keeping the land."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Kirjathsepher\" means \"City of the book.” \"Debir\" means “Sanctuary.” I think \"City of the book\" is a very interesting name. City of what book? Doing a little deeper of a dive, I believe the city is named this in a \"plural\" indication. Either there were multiple books here, or there was ONE book which formed a sort of \"record\" account either of the people living there, or some sort of historical journal. That didn't seem to matter when Caleb takes it, however, and it's renamed to “sanctuary,\" which is all well and fine. It is a Sanctuary for the people of Judah at this particular juncture."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Apparently, this was a man that was worthy of his daughter in the eyes of Caleb. I suppose this was one way of getting people to get to task. If there was no one willing to go forward and take the land, there would be no growth for that land. We will see this act take place quite a bit in scripture (including with King David, but that's a study for later)."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Caleb was willing to give his daughter what she (and her new husband) needed to get started in this new land and in their new life. These places would be quite difficult to find on a map today, so we must take God's Word on the matter regarding the land divided."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":47,"title":"The Valley","entries":[{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here we have another account. It is perhaps a better account to read through this and give some definitions here. The names of the places are better split up."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Kabzeel\" means \"God gathers.” \"Eder\" means “a flock.” \"Jagur\" means \"he sojourns.” So God gathers His flock and keeps consistent fellowship with them on their travels in life. That is the great picture represented in these lands (something they will seem to forget)."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Kinah\" means “lamentation.” \"Dimonah\" means \"river bed\" or “silence.” \"Adadah\" means \"Festival\" or \"boundary.\""}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Kedesh\" means \"holy place.” \"Hazor\" means “Castle.” \"Ithnan\" means \"Hire.\""}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Ziph\" means “Battlement.” \"Telem\" means “Oppression.” \"Bealoth\" means \"Mistresses.\""}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Hadattah\" means \"New Hazor.” Like \"New England\" or \"New Mexico.” \"Kerioth\" means “Cities.\" They were running out of names at this point (perhaps). Or it was just a cluster of cities and they just lumped them altogether. \"Hezron\" means \"Surrounded by a wall.” When we have two names, we could assume that it was likely changed by the Israelites after taking the land. So it's possible that Hazor (before Judah takes it) was called Hezron. We will see this happen with a couple of cities."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Amam\" means \"their mother.” \"Shema\" means “hear.” \"Moladah\" means \"birth\" or \"race.\""}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Hazargaddah\" means \"village of good fortune.” \"Heshmon\" means \"Rich soil.” \"Bethpalet\" means \"house of escape,\" or \"place of escape.\""}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Hazarshual\" means \"Jackal village.” \"Beersheba\" means \"well of the sevenfold oath.” This place should be familiar to us at this point (we read about it in Genesis as the place that an oath is made with Abraham)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wherefore he called that place Beersheba; because there they sware both of them.","ref":"Genesis 21:31"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Bizjothjah\" means \"Contempt of Jehovah.\""}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Baalah\" means “Mistress.” \"Iim\" means “Ruins.” \"Azem\" means \"Bone.\""}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Eltolad\" means \"God's generations.” \"Chesil\" means “Foolish.” \"Hormah\" means \"Devotion.\""}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Ziklag\" means “Winding.” This place will be pretty recognizable to us. ”Madmannah\" means “Dunghill.” \"Sansannah\" means \"Palm branch.”"}]},{"start":32,"end":32,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Lebaoth\" means “Witness.” \"Shilhim\" means “Fountains.” \"Ain\" means “Spring.” \"Rimmon\" means “Pomegranate.\" These are all the cities that have come out of the land in which Judah inherits. Some of these names might look familiar to you when you have studied scripture in the past. They will be prominent places again in the future, and others may never be mentioned again as much more than a passing name. Next, we have the valleys."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Eshtaol\" means “Entreaty.” \"Zoreah\" means “Hornet.” \"Ashnah\" means \"I will cause change.” Interesting name. I assume most of these places were named before the Israelites came in, and they should have (likely) changed most of these names. I don't know if they just didn't get the chance to change them, or if they experienced something that allowed them to keep the names. Either way, the best way to dedicate places like this would have been to rename them to give glory to what the Lord provided for them."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Zanoah\" means \"Cast off.” \"Engannim\" means \"Fountain of the garden.” \"Tappuah\" means \"The apple city.” \"Enam\" means \"Double spring.\""}]},{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Jarmuth\" means “Heights.” \"Adullam\" means \"Justice of the people.” \"Socoh\" means “Bushy.” \"Azekah\" means \"Dug over.\""}]},{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Sharaim\" means \"Double gate.” \"Adithaim\" means \"Double ornament.” \"Gederah\" means “Wall.\" \"Gederothaim\" means \"Two walls.” They really wanted to double up on everything here. I wonder if they said everything twice, too? I wonder if they said everything twice, too? This is the first portion in the valley. Next, we have the last half of the valley portion."}]},{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Zenan\" means “Pointed.” \"Hadashah\" means “New.” \"Migdalgad\" means \"Tower of God.\""}]},{"start":38,"end":38,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Dilean\" means “Gourd.” \"Mizpeh\" means “Watchtower.” \"Joktheel\" means \"The blessedness of God.\""}]},{"start":39,"end":39,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Lachish\" means “Invincible.” \"Bozkath\" means \"Rocky height.” \"Eglon\" means \"Calf-like.\""}]},{"start":40,"end":40,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Cabbon\" means \"The builder.” \"Lahmam\" means “Provisions.” \"Kithlish\" means \"Man's wall.\""}]},{"start":41,"end":41,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Gederoth\" means “Walls.” \"Bethdagon\" means \"House of Dagon.” A great representation of why they should change the names of these places. \"Naamah\" means “loveliness.” \"Makkedah\" means \"Place of Shepherds.” It’s a great wonder what they could have possibly been striving for in this area."}]},{"start":42,"end":42,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Libnah\" means “Pavement.” \"Ether\" means “Abundant.” \"Ashan\" means \"Smoke.\""}]},{"start":43,"end":43,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Jiphtah\" means \"He opens.” \"Ashnah\" means \"I will cause change.” Interesting that this name comes up again. \"Nezib\" means \"Garrison,\" or \"pillar.\""}]},{"start":44,"end":44,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Keliah\" means “Fortress.” \"Achzib\" means “Deceit.” \"Mareshah\" means \"Crest on a hill.\""}]},{"start":45,"end":45,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Ekron\" means \"Emigration\" or \"torn up by the roots.”"}]},{"start":47,"end":47,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Ashdod\" means “Powerful.” \"Gaza\" means \"The Strong.” I wonder if they thought of themselves as capable people. They must have put a lot of reliance on this city as being something mighty."}]}]},{"start":48,"end":62,"title":"The Mountains","entries":[{"start":48,"end":48,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we are given to the mountain cities. \"Shamir\" means \"A point\" or “thorn.” \"Jattir\" means “Plenty.” \"Socoh\" means “Bushy.\" This shows up twice as well."}]},{"start":49,"end":49,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Dannah\" means \"Thou hast judged,\" or “judgement.” \"Kirjathsannah\" means \"City of the book.” We've already gone over this one thankfully. The name is changed to Debir."}]},{"start":50,"end":50,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Anab\" means “Fruit.” \"Eshtemoh\" means \"I shall cause my own ruin.” I don't know if this was a great place to live. It sounds to me that they were leading themselves into a terrible place by living here. \"Anim\" means “Fountains.\" It seems they have two different words for “Fountains.\" The other place was called Shilhim, and it was found in the valley."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think it's important to note that water and wells signify importance in scripture. Water is God's Word. Wells are God's provision. Fountains are God's abundance. No matter which area you might be in today in your life (mountain tops or valley depths), God's fountain can be found if we are looking hard enough."}]},{"start":51,"end":51,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Goshen\" means \"Drawing near.” \"Holon\" means “Sandy.” \"Giloh\" means “Exile.\" It seems there are a lot of emotions taking place in this section of land. I'm glad the writer (likely Joshua) is giving a bit of alleviation from name reading as we go along by splitting it up into sections. Psalm's has its \"Selah\" breaks to stop and think about what you have read, and Joshua has its city breaks to stop and think about what you just read. I assume this is done because the cities were lumped together by territory (so eleven cities in this territory, nine in the next). It's either that or Joshua thought, \"It might be a bit easier to read this list if I give myself a limit of about twenty before continuing on to name more. Either way, we press onward."}]},{"start":52,"end":52,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Arab\" means “Ambush.” \"Dumah\" means “Silence.\" Dimonah is the silence you experience in the valley; Dumah is the silence you experience on the mountains. Silence can equate to \"peace\" when it's seen in Scripture. This is something else that God is good at providing."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"These things I have spoken unto you, that in me ye might have peace. In the world ye shall have tribulation: but be of good cheer; I have overcome the world.","ref":"John 16:33"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Eshean\" means \"Support.\""}]},{"start":53,"end":53,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Janum\" means “Asleep.” \"Bethtappuah\" means \"House of the apple.” \"Aphekah\" means \"Enclosure.\""}]},{"start":54,"end":54,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"”Humtah\" means \"Place of lizards.” I wonder what kind of reptiles you might find in this city? \"Kirjatharba\" means \"City of Arba.” We have spoken of this place before. \"Hebron\" means “Association.\" Obviously, when Kirjatharba was taken over, there was no reason to call that place \"Arba\" anymore. The people of Arba were no longer there, and Arba was not the owner of that land anymore. \"Zior\" means \"Smallness.\""}]},{"start":55,"end":55,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Maon\" means “Habitation.” \"Carmel\" means “Garden-land.” (We will talk about Carmel more later on) \"Ziph\" means “Battlement.” \"Juttah\" means \"Stretched out.\""}]},{"start":56,"end":56,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Jezreel\" means \"God sows.” That should be the name of every place in the land. It would have been wise for God to sow whatever He desired in all of the land, but obviously not everyone is going to allow Him the honor or privilege of doing this. \"Jokdeam\" means \"Burning of a people.” Sounds like a fun place to live. \"Zanoah\" means \"Cast off.” This city seems to be for the outcasts. Those that didn't belong in any other city. They still had a place to go. That is the great theme of scripture, reiterated over and over again. If you feel like an outcast today, you likely truly are. However, the Lord has a place for you, and He will make sure you are taken care of."}]},{"start":57,"end":57,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Cain\" means “Possession.” \"Gibeah\" means “Hill.” \"Timnah\" means \"Portion.\""}]},{"start":58,"end":58,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Halhul\" means “Trembling.” \"Bethzur\" means \"House of the rock.” \"Gedor\" means “Wall.\" Plenty of wall places for these cities. The Lord is their wall and fortress however."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The LORD is my rock, and my fortress, and my deliverer; my God, my strength, in whom I will trust; my buckler, and the horn of my salvation, and my high tower.","ref":"Psalm 18:2"}]},{"start":59,"end":59,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Maarath\" means “Bareness.” \"Bethanoth\" means \"House of responses (or afflictions).” Another fun place, no doubt. \"Eltekon\" means \"God is straight.” This group of names really paints a picture. I imagine Maarath is in the center of these two other cities. Today, when we are born again, we have the choice of direction we desire to go in. There are many churches who bring you into afflictions. They lead you into this house, and your only response is to be afflicted. There are a lot of people who might need to be in that house today. There are prideful people who don't know what affliction is like at all. There are plenty more, however, that don't deserve to be in that house. They get stuck in the house of affliction and they don't know how to escape."},{"kind":"para","text":"The best city to go to is Eltekon. It's where we realize that the house of affliction can be confusing and painful if we are there too long. It's where we come to understand that scripture is so much deeper than pain. A house of God that consistently pains its people is a house that stunts their growth. Find you a house where God is told to you straight. A house where scripture is explained straight. A house where the truth is proclaimed straight. Where you get the straight life, rather than constant affliction."}]},{"start":60,"end":60,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Kirjathbaal\" means \"City of Baal.” This place's name certainly needed to be changed. It's changed to “Kirjathjearim.” \"Kirjathjearim\" means \"city of forests.” Still not a great name, but better than \"City of Baal.” \"Rabbah\" means “Great.\""}]},{"start":61,"end":61,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we have the wilderness cities. \"Betharabah\" means \"House of the desert valley,\" or \"place of the depression.” There are many houses like this today as well. I suppose the house of affliction and the house of depression could likely lead to each other. They have the same entrance, no doubt. You might want to stay out of these houses. \"Middin\" means “Measures.\" We can, perhaps, stay out of the house of depression and affliction by measuring ourselves often. See where we stand with God and where we need to tune ourselves accordingly. Because if you don't, you might be caught in a thicket…"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Secacah\" means “Thicket.\" Sorry for the guitar illustration here, but I think it's a perfect example. The other day I was playing my guitar, and I went to tune one of the strings. I tuned it down, and the string broke and popped. I was so disappointed. They were old strings, so I suppose I should have expected it. I went and bought new strings and restrung all of the strings. When you are restringing a guitar, you have to place the new strings on and then tighten them until they are in the proper tuning. Because they are new, they have to be tuned up over and over again until the strings are stretched enough to not untune themselves. It's a proper illustration of us today."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are stretched quite tightly in our Christian walks, and many of us want to tune down. We attempt a tuning and only break. We then have to renew our strings (our faith). We cannot let our strings remain broken; otherwise, we will never be used for what we were made for. The Lord stretches our faith to keep us in proper tuning for His music, and we should not be resisting it. We should be accepting it. Otherwise, we will be off-beat, and everyone will recognize there's something wrong."}]},{"start":62,"end":62,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Nibshan\" means \"Soft soil.” \"Salt\" would likely be named in the Hebrew “Irhammelech.” \"Engedi\" means \"Fount of the kid.” I assume they found a lot of wild goats there."}]}]},{"start":63,"end":63,"title":"Couldn't Drive Them Out","entries":[{"start":63,"end":63,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This will be a theme in Joshua. They didn't take all the people out of the land like they were told to. Now, it says they \"could not drive them out…\" That either means that this place was so powerful that the children of Judah just couldn't do anything no matter how hard they tried, or it means that they DIDN'T do anything because something else came up and they put it off. It's likely they just put it off, honestly. We will find that the taking of this land ends with the life and legacy of Joshua and Caleb, and they don't go much further after this. Every land will have inhabitants that don't belong because the children of Israel \"couldn't\" drive them out. I believe they just don’t. They become content with what was already given to them, and the generations to follow don't put in any effort or work to keep God's promises. It is the same with our generation today."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are so many in our past who have paved the way for us to sit comfortably today and go through our Bible studies. The buck seems to have stopped with us. We aren't progressing through with what they started. Granted, most of it is finished. Our forefathers finished what they started, but we aren't sticking up for what they accomplished simply out of fear of this world, or out of laziness to fight. That is likely what the Israelites are dealing with here. They are afraid, or they are lazy. Keeping these people in these lands will lead them into trouble when we get into the book of Judges."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":47},{"start":48,"end":62},{"start":63,"end":63}]},
    "16": {"chapter":16,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 16"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we open up about the land being split up towards the 12 tribes of Israel and the inheritance that was promised to each of them. We started off by looking at the land in which Caleb and his people were promised and given, and the strength and support that was given to Caleb (in his old age) in order to take."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we take a look at the land in which the tribes are taking, and it seems rather small in the grand scheme of things (and it will (sadly) remain small because they don't do much more about it after Joshua dies (spoiler alert)), but they were seeing the rising of the promises that God had made to them coming to fruition because they were willing to trust and follow. That is, no doubt, how we will see God's promises come true in our lives as well."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":4,"title":"The Land for Joseph","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If it's not remembered (or not well known), Bethel is the place that Jacob saw his vision from the Lord, and it's the place he eventually settles with his family. It's the place that God promises to his line, and it's the place that the line of Joseph has at this point in the word of God. It will be a place that is mentioned over and over again throughout scripture as a place of significance for searching out the Lord (whether by protection or healing)."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Archi\" means “lengthy” \"Ataroth\" means “crowns.\" We won't speak nearly at all about these particular places."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Japhleti\" means \"Let him escape.” \"Bethhoron\" was that \"house of hollowness\" we spoke about before. It seems like a very dreadful place to possess. \"Nether\" insinuates that they owned the lowest part of it. As do some of us… \"Gezer\" means “Portion.\""}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"As long as they had a place for themselves, that was what really mattered at this point. This was a good chunk of land for these people to take and expand on. Or at least you would think it was…"}]}]},{"start":5,"end":10,"title":"The Land for Ephraim","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Atarothaddar\" means \"crowns of glory.” So Joseph's tribes get crowns, but Ephraim's tribe gets crowns \"of glory.” Then we move to \"Bethhoron\" again. Instead of them taking the whole land here, it is split in half. Joseph's tribe gets the deep pits of this hollow house, where Ephraim gets the higher level of it. If I were them, I would have spent a good lot of time changing this name into something more meaningful if I owned it. Any good renovation project has the purpose of making the place right for what you are doing."},{"kind":"para","text":"When my mom first moved into her new home, she had a lot of cleaning up to do. There was so much old and worn things in the house that had to be fixed or replaced. It could all do with a fresh coat of paint if I am honest. The first thing that needed to take place was a bug bombing. We did the bombing ourselves, but man was it rough. On the first day we went to clean up the spiderwebs and whatnot, we killed no less than twenty black widows. I didn't even know that black widows lived with each other like that. I saw more black widow spiders on that one single day than I have in my entire lifetime on Earth. But she purchased the place, and she wanted to make it her own (and she wanted to do it as quickly as possible as well)."},{"kind":"para","text":"May I say, that is how it should be in our walks with the Lord. We don't have the promised land like these Israelites do, but we have a promise of eternity. Until then (because the Lord has redeemed you), make your walk your own. Do what needs to be done to live righteously before the Lord and make a name for His house. (If I may) you are an old and dilapidated building that needs to be fixed up and renewed. Allow that renewal from the Lord."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For every house is builded by some man; but he that built all things is God.","ref":"Hebrews 3:4"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For we are labourers together with God: ye are God's husbandry, ye are God's building.","ref":"1 Corinthians 3:9"}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Michmethah\" means \"hiding place.” \"Taanathshiloh\" means \"approach to Shiloh.” \"Janohah\" means \"He rests.” Most of these places won't be mentioned again either. I don't really know what happens to these places. Maybe they go to nothing and so they are forgotten about, or maybe they are taken up by a bigger-named place. Kind of like Lancaster or Rosamond. When I lived in Rosamond, and people would ask where I lived (especially when I worked down below), if I told them “Oh, I am from Rosamond!\" Nearly everyone would say, \"Oh! Where is that?\" But when I say, “Oh, I'm from Mojave.” They all knew exactly what I was talking about. I could up the ante and say, \"I'm from the Antelope Valley.\" and it was quite likely that they knew exactly where that was."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Naarath\" means “maiden.\" It is highly likely that these places didn't keep their names either. Perhaps these places do become something more? We won't know too much about this until scripture reveals it, or until we are in eternity. It is evidenced (though) that the promises of the Lord were being fulfilled, and the land was being possessed, and there was no doubt in anyone's mind that the Lord was not with the Israelites through this campaign."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In the last chapter, it says that the Israelites \"could not drive out\" the people of that land. In this chapter here, it says they simply just \"didn't drive out\" the Canaanites that lived here. They either put it off thinking they would get to it eventually, or they didn't see a problem with them staying there and serving them. They were willing to live with the Canaanites as long as they were cordial and able to work. The Lord has made it clear, however, that the purpose for getting these people out was to prevent their culture from influencing the Israelites. The Israelites, obviously, weren't thinking about that though. They don't seem to think it's a problem... but it will become one soon enough."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":10}]},
    "17": {"chapter":17,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 17"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In the last few chapters (and the chapters to come) we have been getting a word picture of how the land was set up, and how it was organized and inherited by each tribe. We get a little bit of an idea of what the beginnings of Israel looked like, and how much more work needed to be put in."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter (in fact) there is some issues that arise that weren't really talked about by Moses, and Joshua simply has to use common sense to address the problems and get them situated so the people can be ready and prepared to grow their land and survive on their own faith and leadership (when Joshua passes away)."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":6,"title":"The Land For Manasseh","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we are introduced to the land that the tribe of Manasseh inherits. We are getting a slight outline of how this land looks at this point, and it seems to be somewhat of a narrowing strip. Actually, if we were to look at a map of this region by the time it is done, it would look a lot like a dagger or an arrow pointing north. That is a fascinating picture to me."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Land was split up to the male children of the family. I've heard a lot of people give the Bible flak because it doesn't speak very much about the women in scripture. This is simply not true. There are a lot of instances where the Bible focuses on the women (Genesis is a good image of that), but the man is the one (in those days) to lead the family. So the land was given to the males, specifically because they are the leaders both physically and spiritually. The women were protected, and they were to be served by the man, not the other way around."},{"kind":"para","text":"Our society sees this very differently now, and the institution of gender has been blurred, allowing very serious problems to enter into the family dynamic. This has not made stronger and more inclusive families. This has only caused confusion and seclusive families. It has made people turn to hate and bitterness, rather than love and acceptance. They taunt a message of love, but it's superficial and cheap. Either way, this particular verse is written out in this matter specifically because the next verse illustrates further about the women..."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Mahlah\" means “disease.\" \"Noah\" means “motion.\" \"Hoglah\" means “partridge.\" \"Milcah\" means “Queen.\" \"Tirzah\" means \"favourable.\" I have some questions about the names of his daughters. In those days (especially), names had very important context and meaning. They usually represented the personality of that person who was named such. It's why Naomi wants her name changed in the book of Ruth."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And she said unto them, Call me not Naomi, call me Mara: for the Almighty hath dealt very bitterly with me.","ref":"Ruth 1:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"Mahlah either wasn't a very loved child or she had some sort of disease. I believe she probably had a disease of some sort, rather than her being a disease to her family only because of the names of the rest of the children. The one that makes me scratch my head though is \"Tirzah.\" Zelophehad had a favorite, and it was very evident…"}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So the women are inheriting land here, and it was because they were in the lineage. The Bible doesn't leave the women out. Some have said, \"The Bible is harsh towards women... they don't even have a chance to speak up for themselves...\" Yet, here, we have women coming boldly before Joshua and asking him about the land that they will get to inherit. They are still part of the family lineage after all, and their gender shouldn't be a problem for their legacy. And guess what? Their gender isn't a problem. They are given land."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":13,"title":"The Shape of the Land","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are getting a word picture of how the land looked for the children of Manasseh, and I thank the Lord we have this. Of course, it doesn't do us all that well to try and paint this picture in our mind, so having a reference to bounce off of would be a big help. We have an idea of how the land looks, and the formation of the tribes taking place. Most people find sections of scripture like this extremely boring, and I don't really blame them. I will give a few definitions that I find interesting in these land names. \"Asher\" means “Happy.\" \"Michmethah\" means \"Hiding place.” \"Shechem\" means \"Back (as in the part of the body)”. \"Entappuah\" means \"Fountain of the apple-city.\" These places seem like pretty great and rich places to be. Who wouldn't want to dwell in a city called \"happy?\" But some people shy away from defining some of these terms."},{"kind":"para","text":"They forsake the original language either because they are lazy, or because they don't find interest in sections of scripture like this. All scripture should be interesting. I get that some of these chapters are boring. Names, and locations, and difficult pronunciations. These are all Hebrew words that really mean nothing to us when we are reading. Yet, we should be approaching God's Word (all of it) as a means of refreshing nourishment for our bodies. If we approach this chapter with a closed mind, ready to skip over it, we are doing a disservice to our souls. God has placed this great information here, not just as a means of explaining the land growth. He puts it here as a means of remembering His promise to His children. God keeps His promises. This is proof of that."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God keeps His promise, but we fail in living in it. You might think, \"So what? They didn't kill all the people. Isn't that a good thing? I mean they were being graceful to the people there and not killing.\" Sure, but it's also a bad thing because of the next verse…"}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Tribute\" means “forced labor.\" The Canaanites become slaves. This is not the method that God gives them. They were to have slaves, only because people owed them and couldn't pay. They were then to treat their slaves fairly. Ultimately, what this will do is cause the people of Israel to rebel against the Lord. Every time they forsake to live out the promises of God and do what they were told to do (driving the people out of the land), they grow a little more disobedient to the Lord. They slide back into the culture, and eventually (as Judges illustrates well), they forsake the Lord altogether and just become like everyone else around them."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":18,"title":"Ephraim's Land Too Small","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The children of Ephraim didn't like the land they had gotten. It wasn't enough for the amount of people they had. They were trying to figure out if there was a way to get better land, or possibly more of it to support the volume of people. They came to Joshua with this issue, knowing that Joshua is fully capable of providing a means, and this is what Joshua did…"}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua tells them to take care of the situation themselves. If the land was not good enough for them, they would have to put the work in and take the land around them to gain more for their tribe. There still was plenty of land for the Israelites to take, and there was ample reason for the Israelites to take it, but I think (because Joshua comes from this tribe) they were under the assumption that Joshua would just give them more land without having to put in the work."},{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua is old, remember. He is at a point where he has to set up the people to start doing the work themselves and lead themselves because he won't be around forever to continue to lead them. They would have to rely on the Lord and in following God's commands in the matter, not follow Joshua. Joshua wouldn't be around forever, so doing the work FOR them would stunt their growth in their faith walk. It is the same with us today."},{"kind":"para","text":"We can be at a great soulwinning church who goes out every Saturday and hands out gospel tracts and prays, and preaches, and helps whomever they can to be a part of the church, yet if YOU are personally not involved, you are stunting your growth. The crazier thing is, those people who don't get personally involved with the church’s ministries, are usually the ones that complain the most about the ministries (or about life in general) and its leading. It's a very difficult terrain to walk on, and it's a narrow path with not much room to expand. Hence the issue with this tribe. They want a place to expand, and they want it to be handed to them. Joshua doesn't want to just fight their battles for them, he wants them to take the initiative."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They make a good point. These people around them had far better weapons and tools, and they were worried about being defeated. But they seem to forget the immense victories that God has already provided for the entire land since they have entered into it…"}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua doesn't just have confidence that these men will have the power to take what they need. He knows that the Lord will deliver them if they work by faith. They just have to be willing to trust the Lord in the process of doing. Honestly, I don't think they would even have to lift a sword or spear in battle to succeed in the land; they would just have to have come to the Lord directly for provision and direction, and God would have granted it."},{"kind":"para","text":"Instead, they came to Joshua, and Joshua turns them around and tells them to seek this matter out themselves. I think it's a bold strategy that could have been expressed a bit deeper, but that is what we have here, and we assume they don't take very much more into consideration after that. It seems they ignore Joshua's advice and just accept what they have already been given…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":18}]},
    "18": {"chapter":18,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 18"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: We have been getting a lot of information about the lands that are being possesed by the children of Israel. Joshua was quite detailed about everything everyone was getting, and he wrote it all down to assure that nothing could be argued about later stating that one tribe or another deserved someone elses land."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see that there are still several tribes struggling to take the promise, and they confront Joshua at the Lord's dwelling about this problem, requesting an answer and direction about how to proceed. Joshua doesn't just leave them hanging, but he also makes it clear that they have already been instructed, and it is suffice for them to take the matters on themselves if they are desiring to living in the promise of God. See, God has made a promise to the entire world. Just because we have a promise made, doesn't mean we are living in it. It's a decision WE (personally) have to make."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"Land's Still Missing Inheritance","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We will find, later, that the tabernacle will be moved to a proper place. Right now, they needed to find a spot that was sufficient for all the tribes to meet for the time being. It was not stationed centrally, and that is really what God wants. He wants His house to be right in the middle of the land, so that every one of His children would be able to locate and travel to it. Jerusalem will eventually be that central area."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We've already read about the tribes that have gotten their land and are prepared to move in, but these other tribes were still missing direction of where they were supposed to dwell and grow. The thing is, they didn't have to wait for instruction of where they were supposed to go. They knew their places, and they knew that there was still a lot of land to take over. They are relying on Joshua to give them direction, rather than relying on the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"Up until we are introduced to Moses, God's children were consistently seeking the Lord for direction. They are now used to seeking after a man, and that would be quite a big problem to them for the remainder of this book (because man fails them over and over again). Eventually, however, there will be one man that does not fail them, and He will save them (and the world) from all of their errors and sins."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua is not saying that they don't deserve land (obviously), he is telling them that they still had a mission. The soulwinners mission (today) is still needed. In this time, it was taking land. Today, it is telling people the gospel, and moving them out of the desire to live in the world, and live in eternity's mind."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua is giving them a task. He says, \"There is land already prepared for you. You just have to go take it. The Lord has promised that it is yours. So take three men from each of your tribes, and have them go look at the land and divide it into seven parts, and I'll bring it before the Lord so that you can agree with how to proceed.\" Joshua knows they could take this information straight to God themselves, but they are relying solely on a mediator here and (at this time) they depended on Joshua. They would have depended on Moses, but (obviously) he's not there for them to depend on."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sadly, many of us are like these tribes here. We've been given salvation and freedom, and we just stand there and wonder when God is going to move us. We wait for our leaders to tell us what to do rather than just simply obey the Lord and move forward. The bigger problem is, our leaders command us to wait for them to receive instruction. We, sometimes, hold back on things that are good, knowing they are good, simply because we know that the leader wants us to wait for him to make sure it is actually good. When we know what is good, and we know how the Lord is leading, (I risk demerits for saying this) ignore them and do what the Lord would have you to do."},{"kind":"para","text":"God is pretty pointedly clear that He wants a relationship with all of His children, not just the leaders He has placed over them. The leaders are there to help those that need direction, receive direction. Those that know their calling, don't really need to depend on the leaders for it. HOWEVER, that does not mean we should not work together, and that is why Joshua says this next…"}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Levites don't receive land, so the rest of the tribes were to make sure to still help them and support them. Just because we don't have to follow leaders, and just because we can know for sure where God is leading us personally, doesn't mean we don't work together to support one another. That will be better suited, and understood, by the design of the church in the New Testament, but they were to showcase this right now as they set up this new land. They were to be a force to be reckoned with if they supported each other and walked by faith. That is the power of the church today."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":10,"title":"Casting Lots","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We've talked about \"casting lots\" before a few times, and it is something we will make mention of again (in the future as we read), but it was just a simple test with some stones or sticks to see how things would land. God allows this method for a few reasons, but we don't utilize this method today because we have God's complete Word and Holy Spirit to direct us on what we should be doing. If I'm not mistaken (and perhaps I am here), but casting lots to replace Judas Iscariot in the book of Acts is the very last time they do it (they are then given the Holy Ghost and never had need to do it again)."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this situation, they desired God's instruction as quickly as possible, and the need (here) is not necessarily dire. Now, if they were casting lots to determine a person’s life, that might be a different story (that happens in the book of Jonah). But just to split up the land (knowing that the land would grow), Joshua thought it was best to determine this with lots and get it out of the way so they can't complain about how Joshua determines the lands. They agreed that the lots would be sufficient to determine it, and so they will have to be happy with as big (or as little) of the land they have been given by the lots that are cast."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So the lots were cast, and this was the final verdict for the remainder of the land they had taken control over. Again, there was still plenty of land for them to expand upon, but we won't see that happen for quite some time, and even when it does, they still don't take it all…"}]}]},{"start":11,"end":20,"title":"Benjamin's Inheritance","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So now we have the tribe of Benjamin receiving land. Benjamin is the youngest tribe in the land, and they are (likely) the smallest tribe as well. We will see the first king of Israel come from this tribe, and he will set a whole course of change for the land of Israel."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jericho is where the people started, and we are introduced to this book through the taking of this land first. \"Bethaven\" means \"House of vanity.\" This will be a place that's mentioned quite often as we read through different wars and accounts of the Israelites in the Old Testament."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have talked about Bethel before, and it will be a place brought up again in the future. \"Atarothadar\" means \"Crowns of glory.\" We won't hear too much of this place again. \"Bethhoron\" means (if you don't remember) \"House of hollowness.\" These places don't seem too thrilling or exciting. Living in vanity and victories, and hollowness seems kind of drab and simple. It's the idea, honestly, of today’s high life. Our Hollywood elites live in these types of houses."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Kirjathbaal\" means \"city of Baal.\" Baal (as we should know at this point) was a false prophet and a very wicked man. People worshipped him, though, because they thought he had power and truth. People are easily tricked. The name of the city seems to change once the Israelites take over. \"Kirjathjearim\" means \"city of forests.\" It doesn't leave much to the imagination, but we can safely assume that this city likely had a lot of trees growing in it."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"”Nephtoah\" means \"Opening.\" Again, this was a place we had already mentioned before. \"Hinnom\" means \"Lamentation.\" Another sad name for a valley. \"Jebusi\" means \"Descendants of Jebus.\" We assume the Jebus people were giants simply because they lived around the valley of giants, and so anyone in this valley were quite large people. \"Enrogel\" means \"Fount of the Fuller.\" That's a promising name! A little spark of God's care is found in all of this dread in the land."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Enshemesh\" means \"Fountain of the sun.” \"Geliloth\" means “Circuits.\" \"Adummim\" means \"Ruddy One.\" We have a few plays on words here as we read through scripture. We will find Samuel (the last of the judges) going on circuits throughout the land. It will be mentioned that David is a \"ruddy one\" (and some others as well), and God's sun will be referenced over and over again through scripture as being an evident provision from Him every single day. \"to the stone of Bohan the son of Reuben\" indicates that the Reubenites border butts up against Benjamin’s here. \"Arabah\" means \"Desert plain.\" Perhaps that is the Antelope Valley? I'm joking of course… \"Bethhoglah\" means \"House of the partridge.\" That's where they have their pear trees! (Again, another joke)."},{"kind":"para","text":"These places seem to be great pictures of the diverse lands they have to utilize. How they actually utilize it is a wonder to me. We don't get much information about the depths of their culture, and how these lands eventually turn out to be. We only see a few names pop back up to give an idea and credence that the lands were being used, or that they were changed in some way."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So the children of Benjamin have their land, and they were ready to utilize it and (hopefully) prosper."}]}]},{"start":21,"end":28,"title":"The Cities Inherited","entries":[{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a bit of a quick recap (adding some cities that weren't mentioned before). Just to give the definitions (and to hopefully utilize the options and ways in which these people can go), I want to take a little bit of time in studying these names. \"Keziz\" means \"cut off.\" There is a story there… \"Zemaraim\" means \"Double fleece of wool.” \"Avim\" means “ruins.\" \"Parah\" means “Heifer-town.\" \"Ophrah\" means “fawn.\" \"Chepharhaammonai\" means “Village of the Ammonites.” \"Ophni\" means \"mouldy.\" I, perhaps, would never mention to anyone that I am from here… \"Gaba\" means “hill.\" \"Gibeon\" means \"hill city.” \"Ramah\" means \"hill.\" Interesting how there are two different words for hill here. I am sure these are two different kinds of hill, but I am not sure what the differences represent. If I knew someone that spoke Hebrew, they might be able to explain."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Beeroth\" means “wells.\" \"Mizpeh\" means “watchtower.\" \"Chephirah\" means “lioness.\" \"Mozah\" means “fountain.\" \"Rekem\" means “variegation.\" \"Variegation\" means \"alter in appearance.” \"Irpeel\" means \"God will heal.\" It seems this land was going through some massive changes here. Fountains, changing appearances, times of healing. Interesting picture being painted right in the center of ruins and moldy."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Taralah\" means “reeling.\" \"Zelah\" means \"a rib.” \"Eleph\" means \"thousand.\" And you might look at all these and say, “Well, wonderful, this information is utterly useless to me. Sitting and talking about the definitions of these Hebrew names has not made me any wiser, and I will likely forget the meaning of nearly all of these.\" You are right. However, it was important for scripture to mention it here, and when people are spoken about (later on), they will be mentioned about where they are from. Instead of just giving their name, they give the name and the city with it. We can determine a lot about a person based on the city they are from."},{"kind":"para","text":"We can see Sam from Irpeel and think, \"Being from a place like that, he must be being worked on.\" Or we can see Sam from Ophni and think, \"Boy, I don't know if I want to be around that guy...\" If I were to tell people that I am from Lancaster, CA, they would think of me as a desert dweller. They are right. Now, if I told them I was from Beverly Hills, they would think of me as rich. That would be wrong. If I told them I was from Detroit, or Compton... I'll let you determine what people might think of me."},{"kind":"para","text":"When I moved out to Texas, everyone around me knew I was from California. They treated me like a Californian. It was earned to be considered a Texan, and there was no means (at least to me) to work towards that earning. They hear \"Californian\" and they think \"Liberal politics, sunshine and beaches, desk jobs and retail, Red Hot Chili Peppers and In-N-Out Burger.\" I enjoy beaches and In-N-Out, but the rest I cannot say I am particularly interested in. When I think of Texas I think, \"Conservative politics, desert and farmland, rodeo and large everything, Country music and What-A-Burger.\" So if I said, \"Hello I'm Jonathan from California.\" You would have your mind made up about me regarding the former. Or if I said, \"Howdy, I'm Jonathan from Texas.\" You would think the latter of me. You see where I'm getting at. These names are not just fill-ins, they become useful. Ruth of Moab will not have a ring to it when we get to that point. Jesus of Nazareth will sound quite uncultured. I think you understand where I'm coming from."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":28}]},
    "19": {"chapter":19,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 19"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In the last few chapters, we have been diving deep into the land that is being divided between all the children of Israel. It's a bit of a tedious task (if I'm being honest) to look into all these meanings and names, but I think it has been quite refreshing in some instances to get a little bit of a background of the area, and to use the names as a story to link to the rest of scripture (that's what I am hoping to try and do anyway)."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we continue on that same vain. However, this will be the last of the dividing of land. It has been a trek, and a workout to go through this, but I think it has been fascinating and (hopefully) fun to get as much information as possible. One last chapter to go, and then we can move on to some final stipulations and encouragement."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":9,"title":"The Land For Simeon","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Continuing through the dividing of land (though they haven't taken all the land they knew they were supposed to), Joshua makes it an important venture to give every single tribe at least one section of it. This land of Simeon is actually land that was already possessed by another tribe (but we will read on that in a second). So there are some places that have already been defined before, and I won't take too much time to define them again if I don't have to. I think it's sufficient (and perhaps I should have said this at the beginning of this division, but you can do it however you see fit) if you are using this as a means to teach a Bible study, you could likely lump all of these land division chapters together to go through it a little quicker. I personally like going through the names and defining the meanings, especially because some of these places we are going to see again. Like, for instance…"}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Beersheba\" means \"Well of the sevenfold oath.” This is actually a place we have already heard of. It's a place that was named by Abraham."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wherefore he called that place Beersheba; because there they sware both of them.","ref":"Genesis 21:31"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Sheba\" means “Seven.\" Seven is a number that is brought up over and over again throughout scripture ascribing to God's perfection. \"Moladah\" means “Birth.\" Perhaps Israel changed the name of this place (or it didn't have a name), or the people who were living here prior were a part of a new race (though highly unlikely)."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Hazarshual\" means \"Jackal village.” We have already spoken about this place in chapter 15. It's anyone's guess as to what lives there. \"Balah\" means \"waxed old.” Maybe this was a good retirement spot. This could be a good place for someone to just rest. Maybe that's an option for Joshua at the end of this chapter. Maybe it's just a really old place that's dry and dead. Maybe it's not a place you'd want to live at all… There are some retirement homes that are like that. They are not there to help you live out the rest of your life with joy and purpose. There are some retirement homes (believe it or not) that are just there to babysit the older folk until they pass away. They are there to simply give news to the family, and nothing more. That's so sad to me. “Azem\" is another place we spoke about in chapter 15. It means \"Bone\" if you don't remember. Again, it sounds dry and weary here."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Eltolad\" comes from chapter 15. \"Bethul\" means \"God destroys.” It can also mean \"dweller in God.” I think this land has a choice. \"Eltolad\" means \"God's generations.” So if they are stubborn and negligent in God's leading in this land, the Lord has a right to go either way with their name. \"Hormah\" means “Devotion.\" That's what they will need to keep from becoming \"God destroys.”"}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Ziklag\" has already been mentioned in chapter 15 as well. \"Bethmarcaboth\" means \"house of the chariots.” Perhaps there was some great power in this place. Armies could come from this place."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And at Bethmarcaboth, and Hazarsusim, and at Bethbirei, and at Shaaraim. These were their cities unto the reign of David.","ref":"1 Chronicles 4:31"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Hazarsusah\" means \"Horse village.” That's fortuitous. They need horses for their chariots, and they need chariots for their horses, so this is a great land to accomplish this task."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Bethlebaoth\" means \"House of lionesses.” Seems like a safe place to sleep. “Sharuhen” means \"Refuge of grace.” Seems like a safer place to sleep. What a great name for a city. Everyone should escape to the \"refuge of grace\" as often as possible. So this was one portion, and its cities/villages."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Ain, Remmon, Ether, and Ashan\" are all described in chapter 15. Another section is recorded here. Anytime we see the verses split up like this (ending with \"and their villages“), we can rightfully assume that this is the end of a section. If we see it on a map, this was (perhaps) a part of the area that was taken at one time, and then the other areas were taken at other times. Or they are sections from border to border."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Baalathbeer\" means \"mistress of the well.” \"Ramath\" means “hill.\" Imagine someone asking you where you are from and you tell them, “Oh, I'm from that hill over there.” Better yet, you say, “Oh, I live over at the mistress of the well.” What a strange name."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Judah had a large inheritance already, and so Joshua is splitting up some of that land to give to the children of Simeon. The children of Judah had a large portion because they were the capital tribe. The authoritative tribe. They were the (some would call) \"kingly\" tribe. At this time, they were not planning on a king, but it will eventually come to be that this is the tribe in which Israel's ultimate King will come through."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":16,"title":"The Land of Zebulun","entries":[{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These names seem to bring about a story of sorts. I think they resemble the story of Revelation if I'm being honest. Track this with me (humor me for a moment). \"Sarid\" means “survivor.\" \"Maralah\" means “trembling.\" \"Dabbasheth\" means \"a hill-place.” This is funnier than just saying you are from “hill.\" You tell people, \"Oh, I'm from a hill-place.” You could probably not guess what this place looks like. \"Jokneam\" is spoken about in chapter 12. It means \"the people lament.” The world aches without the Lord, and so they find reason to try and pain people who are living prosperous IN the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Chislothtabor\" means \"flanks of Tabor.” The enemy flanks those with something valuable. The most valuable thing a Christian has is His faith. That is where the enemy will attack you. \"Daberath\" means “word.\" God's Word is the only thing this world needs. We will get little glimpses of some of these places later on (like this one) but just for reference more than for any important information."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And out of the tribe of Issachar; Kedesh with her suburbs, Daberath with her suburbs,","ref":"1 Chronicles 6:72"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Japhia\" is actually mentioned back in chapter 10! \"Japhia\" means “Shining.\" That is the Lord. We reflect His light in this extremely dark world. And (not to be pessimistic or anything), this world is getting darker."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Gittahhepher\" means \"The winepress of digging.” Winepresses are usually useful and joyous things, so I assume that digging was a passion in this area. Honestly, I don't know what this really means; it could mean digging in a lot of different things. Digging is what we should be doing in God's Word. Perhaps that is what they were doing here. This place was the \"winepress of digging into God's Word!” There was plenty of fruit to pull from it. This is actually the place in which Jonah is from (either born in or residing in)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He restored the coast of Israel from the entering of Hamath unto the sea of the plain, according to the word of the LORD God of Israel, which he spake by the hand of his servant Jonah, the son of Amittai, the prophet, which was of Gathhepher.","ref":"2 Kings 14:25"},{"kind":"para","text":"I think Jonah loved to dig into God's Word. I think he struggled giving it to Nineveh, though. He was more of a hoarder of God's Word than a giver. That's a conversation for a different time. It seems at the end of time, we will see people trying to dig anything up that they can to help them proceed and prosper. They will find nothing to supply them, though."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Ittahkazin\" means \"Time of the judge.” That's a call to the future, isn't it? There will be a time when God judges."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Remmonmethoar\" means “pomegranate.\" It has the same spelling in Hebrew as Rimmon. I assume these are two different places (I could be wrong), but this particular spelling of it doesn't appear in Scripture again."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Neah\" means “Shaking.\" Okay, so we got judgment, pomegranates, and shaking. There is quite a bit of symbolism here. We are looked at like pomegranates:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"As a piece of a pomegranate are thy temples within thy locks.","ref":"Song of Solomon 6:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"Pomegranates have many seeds, and the church has many people (we can study that in Song of Solomon later). But when God's judgement comes, it will certainly shake His people. We have a protective shell in the Lord (however), and He will sustain us when the shaking happens. That is what Hannathon proves."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Hannathon\" means “gracious.\" That is what saves us. It's God's grace. \"Jiphthahel\" means \"God opens.” That's a very important note to make. Some have said, \"You can force open doors that the Lord has closed, and it will cause you some harm.” I don't think that is true. I think the Lord opens what He wants to and closes what He doesn't want open. We cannot force His hand. God opens. We are to simply follow."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And to the angel of the church in Philadelphia write; These things saith he that is holy, he that is true, he that hath the key of David, he that openeth, and no man shutteth; and shutteth, and no man openeth;","ref":"Revelation 3:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"He holds the keys. He controls the times. He makes the decisions. We cannot do anything about it, except trust in Him, or suffer the consequences of being His enemy."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Kattath\" means “Small.\" That is, honestly, what the end times will look like. There will be a minority of people saved in those days. It will be a \"small\" remnant."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Nahallal\" means “Pasture.\" Interestingly enough (and I'm seriously not trying to tie-in all these names with the end times, I just see that is what the Holy Spirit is reminding of here), the saved (in those days) will be living in greener pastures over the rest of the world. While the rest of the world falls into chaos and turmoil, those that have Christ will be protected from God's wrath. That will upset those without Christ, which is why they will go to attempt and attack the Christians in those days, and they will succeed at killing a good portion of God's children. But God's judgement and wrath will only shake His children. It will not destroy them. They will have pasture."},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Shimron\" means “Watch-height.\" That is where the Christians stand. We stand at “watch-height.\" We can see what God is doing and how God is moving, and we have a front-row seat to God's biggest victories! Praise the Lord!"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Idalah\" means \"memorial of God.” That is what the world needs. They need to remember God. Even in the days when God judges this world and sends down His wrath, people will choose to reject and forget the Lord. God's proof will literally be all around them, and they will find no reason to turn to the Lord. \"Bethlehem\" means \"House of bread.” This is how the Christian will survive in those days."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Jesus said unto them, I am the bread of life: he that cometh to me shall never hunger; and he that believeth on me shall never thirst.","ref":"John 6:35"}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So there are only twelve tribes, but it feels like (at this point, with so many chapters on this subject now) that we have mentioned fifty tribes. We are almost through them all, though, so bear with me."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":23,"title":"The Land of Issachar","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jezreel is found in chapter 15. \"Chesulloth\" means “flanks.\" \"Shunem\" means \"double resting place.” One resting place is great, but a DOUBLE resting place is even better! You'd think this place would be a wonderful spot to stop and stay. It is, however, only associated with somewhat negative scenes in Scripture. They are rollercoaster scenes because bad happens, and good happens in both cases."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the Philistines gathered themselves together, and came and pitched in Shunem: and Saul gathered all Israel together, and they pitched in Gilboa.","ref":"1 Samuel 28:4"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And it fell on a day, that Elisha passed to Shunem, where was a great woman; and she constrained him to eat bread. And so it was, that as oft as he passed by, he turned in thither to eat bread.","ref":"2 Kings 4:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps there is such thing as too much rest, and that is why Solomon finds it necessary to point out the \"Sluggard\" quite a bit in Proverbs."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Haphraim\" means \"two pits.” If the enemy doesn't fall into one pit, he certainly will fall into the other? \"Shion\" means “ruin.\" This is where the prosperity gospel was born (I'm kidding, of course). \"Anaharath\" means \"The groaning of fear.” These are all very interesting names. I imagine that these spots would be used for the enemies more than for the people dwelling there. I don't know, though, because Scripture doesn't talk about them again. That's shocking, because they seem like such great places to live."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Rabbith\" means “multitude.\" \"Kishion\" means “hard.\" \"Abez\" means \"I will make white.” The multitude of Israel were certainly a hard-hearted people, and God had to cleanse and purify them. These small towns were a good picture of Israel's (and the world’s) future!"}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Remeth\" means “Height.\" \"Engannim\" means \"Fountain of the garden.” \"Enhaddah\" means \"swift fountain.” \"Bethpazzez\" means \"house of dispersion.” This little story here (if I may) seems to escalate quickly."},{"kind":"para","text":"They are found in the height. It might be a beautiful place up there. The fountain there starts to water the garden. Suddenly this fountain gets faster, and it's doing too much to it, to where the house and everything in this fountain’s path must disperse. This is also Israel's future. God has planted them, and sends them prosperity, and they will let it go to their heads. They will fill their gardens far too full, and they will ruin it for themselves and everyone else around them."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Tabor\" means “Mound.\" \"Shahazimah\" means \"Toward the heights.” \"Bethshemesh\" is also spoken about in chapter 15. \"Bethshemesh\" means \"house of the sun.” That's where we should always be looking. Look to the house of the sun. Look up! That is where the Lord is."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Home stretch, four more to go. This might not be quite as entertaining to you as it is to me, but I think it builds the story of the tribes quite well."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":31,"title":"The Land of Asher","entries":[{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Helkath\" means “Smooth.\" \"Hali\" means “necklace.\" \"Beten\" means “womb.\" \"Achshaph\" means \"I shall be bewitched.” If I were to believe this is creating a story for us, I would think this story takes a hard left turn. That is how this world (and the Israelites) get tricked so easily, though. The world entices us in with smooth words and speeches, wraps a necklace around you, takes care of you, and gives birth to desires and lusts… Then it's too late…"}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Alammelech\" means \"Oak of the king.” \"Amad\" means “Enduring.\" “Mishael\" means “entreaty.\" \"Carmel\" is a place we have spoken about quite a few times already in this book. It's a place that we will visit over and over again in Scripture. All the way up to Nahum."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He rebuketh the sea, and maketh it dry, and drieth up all the rivers: Bashan languisheth, and Carmel, and the flower of Lebanon languisheth.","ref":"Nahum 1:4"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Shihorlibnath\" means \"Blackness of whiteness.” What does that even mean? What a strange picture being drawn here. If it's black, there is no white. If it's white, there is no black. That's like saying that my living room will still be dark if I put a light in it. Perhaps that's the story it's telling. There are superficial people in this world. They say they are in the light, but they are not. They present a false light."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"If we say that we have fellowship with him, and walk in darkness, we lie, and do not the truth: But if we walk in the light, as he is in the light, we have fellowship one with another, and the blood of Jesus Christ his Son cleanseth us from all sin.","ref":"1 John 1:6-7"},{"kind":"para","text":"You can see how dark they are, though they consider themselves light."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Bethdagon\" means \"House of Dagon.” Dagon is a false god. They would do well to change this name as quickly as possible (perhaps they do, because I don't think it's mentioned again after this). \"Bethemek\" means \"house of the valley.” \"Neiel\" means \"moved by God.” As we all should be. \"Cabul\" means “binding.\" As we all seem to be…"}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Hebron\" means “alliance.\" \"Rehob\" means \"broad place.” \"Hammon\" means \"warm springs.” \"Kanah\" means “reed.\" \"Zidon\" means “hunting.\""}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Tyre\" means “rock.\" This will be a prominent place throughout the Old Testament as well."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Tyrus did build herself a strong hold, and heaped up silver as the dust, and fine gold as the mire of the streets.","ref":"Zechariah 9:3"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Hosah\" means “Refuge.\" \"Achzib\" means “deceit.\" Sounds like a trusted lot of people."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Therefore shalt thou give presents to Moreshethgath: the houses of Achzib shall be a lie to the kings of Israel.","ref":"Micah 1:14"}]},{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Ummah\" means “Union\" \"Aphek\" means \"enclosure.\""}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A lot of information dumping in this chapter. I think it's quite incredible that there was this much detail remembered and maintained in this portion of scripture here. Joshua knew exactly what land belonged to whom, but he also memorized (or at least by the leading of the Holy Spirit's prompting) all of the names of the cities that each tribe was taking. You certainly cannot accuse the Bible of not being diligent and specific."}]}]},{"start":32,"end":39,"title":"The Land of Naphtali","entries":[{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Heleph\" means “exchange.\""},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For what shall it profit a man, if he shall gain the whole world, and lose his own soul? Or what shall a man give in exchange for his soul?","ref":"Mark 8:36-37"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Allon\" means \"great tree.” \"Zaanannim\" means “removings.\" \"Adami\" means \"man of Adar.” \"Nekeb\" means “cavern.\" \"Jabneel\" means \"God causes to build.” This place would do well to remember that. I suppose that is why it doesn't build much further. They were trying to build it themselves, and they didn't allow the Lord to build."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Except the LORD build the house, they labour in vain that build it: except the LORD keep the city, the watchman waketh but in vain.","ref":"Psalm 127:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Lakum\" means “fortification.\" \"Jordan\" means “descender.\""}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Aznothtabor\" means \"Peaks of Tabor.” \"Hukkok\" means “Appointed.\" Zebulun, Asher, and Judah were the surrounding tribes, it seems."}]},{"start":35,"end":35,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Ziddim\" means \"the declivities.” “Declivities\" is a downward slope. “Acclivity\" is an upward slope. The acclivities would have been a better name for it, but obviously the people that named it were downward thinkers… \"Zer\" means “flint.\" \"Hammath\" means \"hot springs.” \"Rakkath\" means “shore.\" \"Chinnereth\" means “harps.\" These places sound like paradise. That is (perhaps) their greatest downfall…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"So Benhadad hearkened unto king Asa, and sent the captains of the hosts which he had against the cities of Israel, and smote Ijon, and Dan, and Abelbethmaachah, and all Cinneroth, with all the land of Naphtali.","ref":"1 Kings 15:20"}]},{"start":36,"end":36,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Adamah\" means \"The earth.” \"Hazor\" means “castle.\" This is a place we have picked up on quite a bit throughout Joshua. It will be referenced continually as well."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Hazor shall be a dwelling for dragons, and a desolation for ever: there shall no man abide there, nor any son of man dwell in it.","ref":"Jeremiah 49:33"}]},{"start":37,"end":37,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Kedesh\" is the \"Holy place\" mentioned in Joshua a few times. It continues to be brought up through the Old Testament. \"Edrei\" means \"goodly pasture.” \"Enhazor\" means \"fount of hazor.”"}]},{"start":38,"end":38,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Iron\" means “fearing.\" \"Migdalel\" means \"tower of God.” \"Horem\" means “sacred.\" \"Bethanath\" means \"house of response.” That is where we should visit when God gives us a sacred trust. We should respond with glory to Him."}]},{"start":39,"end":39,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Inching our way along, we have two more tribes to study through now."}]}]},{"start":40,"end":48,"title":"The Land of Dan","entries":[{"start":41,"end":41,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Zorah and Eshtaol are found in chapter 15. \"Irshemesh\" means \"city of the sun-god.” Another place whose name needs to change quite quickly."}]},{"start":42,"end":42,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Shaalabbin\" means \"Place of foxes.” Try growing anything here, and eventually you will be having fox meat for dinner. It probably won't taste good, but it will get these pesky critters out of the way… \"Ajalon\" means \"field of deer.” That's a good hunting place! \"Jethlah\" means \"he hangs.” Who hangs? I don't know. This place isn't mentioned before, so whoever named it, likely named it before the tribe of Dan gets there. It's a good reminder of the one who hung on a tree for us though…"}]},{"start":43,"end":43,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Elon\" means “mighty.\" \"Thimnathah\" means “portion.\" \"Ekron\" means \"torn up by the roots.” This will be a place to visit again in the future."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he said unto him, Thus saith the LORD, Forasmuch as thou hast sent messengers to enquire of Baalzebub the god of Ekron, is it not because there is no God in Israel to enquire of his word? therefore thou shalt not come down off that bed on which thou art gone up, but shalt surely die.","ref":"2 Kings 1:16"}]},{"start":44,"end":44,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Eltekeh\" means \"let God spue thee out.”"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"So then because thou art lukewarm, and neither cold nor hot, I will spue thee out of my mouth.","ref":"Revelation 3:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Gibbethon\" means “mound.\" \"Baalath\" means “mistress.\""}]},{"start":45,"end":45,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Jehud\" means “beauty.\" \"Beneberak\" means \"sons of lightning.” Interesting. I linked this to:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And James the son of Zebedee, and John the brother of James; and he surnamed them Boanerges, which is, The sons of thunder:","ref":"Mark 3:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"One is the son of a very loud noise (usually followed by loud action). The other is the sight of loud action. \"Gathrimmon\" means \"winepress of the pomegranate.” I don't think I've ever had pomegranate wine. It sounds interesting though. But again, this fruit is a picture of Christ's bride. We are going to be crushed by this world, but we will never be forsaken by the Lord. It's interesting that we are given a female pronoun to this city later on."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Aijalon with her suburbs, Gathrimmon with her suburbs; four cities.","ref":"Joshua 21:24"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Aijalon with her suburbs, and Gathrimmon with her suburbs:","ref":"1 Chronicles 6:69"},{"kind":"para","text":"I don't find that coincidence, but I'm not going to exaggerate that fact either."}]},{"start":46,"end":46,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Mejarkon\" means \"waters of yellowness.” I don't know about you, but if my water is yellow, I am probably not going to be using it for much of anything. The saying goes, \"Don't eat yellow snow.” Well, I think it's sufficient to say, \"Don't drink yellow water.” \"Rakkon\" means \"the temple.” \"Japho\" means “beautiful.\" This is supposedly the same place as \"Joppa\" later on."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But Jonah rose up to flee unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD, and went down to Joppa; and he found a ship going to Tarshish: so he paid the fare thereof, and went down into it, to go with them unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD.","ref":"Jonah 1:3"}]},{"start":47,"end":47,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Dan needed more space (it seems), so they went to take more land. Honestly, I think ALL the tribes needed more space. God wanted them to flourish. He wanted them to be a people in a GREATER number than the rest of the lands around them. But they limited themselves."},{"kind":"para","text":"I had several goldfish when I was growing up. Perhaps some of you reading can relate to me through this. Those goldfish that I purchased were always so small and never seemed to get very big. They would die so quickly, and I always thought they were dying as baby fish. I didn't think they ever really grew up. I came to find out later in life that I was honestly just limiting my goldfish. Apparently (as I did some research later), the bigger the bowl you give the fish, the bigger and stronger those fish become. My mother-in-law has two goldfish in her house, and she's had them for about 5 years now (probably longer). They are quite large because of the size tank she had given them. They are both about the size of a 16oz can of Red Bull."},{"kind":"para","text":"Dan did the smart thing and expanded their land so they could suffice their needs."}]},{"start":48,"end":48,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Even still, it won't be enough for them, and they should have all taken more. I think (honestly) if they had taken more, we wouldn't be seeing nearly the same amount of wars and battles for that land today, and there would be a lot less hassle and trouble for that land. A lot of things would be different had they just listened to God on the matter."}]}]},{"start":49,"end":51,"title":"The Land of Joshua","entries":[{"start":49,"end":49,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua belonged to the tribe of Ephraim. That land was given to them in chapter 16 of this book. But the children of Israel wanted to give him the choice and wanted him to choose which place he would like to live. They felt he deserved a choice. The problem was, they chose the places from which he could choose."},{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua could choose to live with a different tribe. Perhaps some of the choices were a little more lush than others. The tribe of Judah had plenty of land to go around (maybe they gave him a few options there). It was the central city after all (possibly considered the wealthiest of the tribes). But Joshua chose to stay loyal and humble."}]},{"start":50,"end":50,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Timnathserah\" means \"abundant portion.” Joshua stayed with his people in Ephraim. He belonged there, and he knew it. He chose a desert land to bring in the close of his life. There wasn't much there. That seems to be where God does the most work for some reason. He chose the land that seemed lifeless and caused it to flourish. He was just happy to have been able to lead these people this far. He led them far enough to get started with their growth and wealth. It was now up to them about how they were going to proceed from here on out."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know why Israel even gave him the option of this land. If I were Israel, I would have just said, \"You have the entire map before you, take your pick, and we will plant you there.” Rather, they gave him the option of the desert, and I don't know if he was just humble enough to take the leftover land, or if that was the best choice of the mix. Either way, it says a lot about the people if they don't even try and coerce him to stay somewhere better and closer to them."}]},{"start":51,"end":51,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It is time for them to retire. They have run their race, and they can rest easy until their deaths. We will see (in the next few chapters) that there was some cleaning up to do, and some stipulations that have to be remembered (in order for them to continue to prosper), but the dividing of the land was officially completed. And hey! Congratulations if you read all of this and made it this far. Good for you! You deserve a break with Joshua and the priest as well."},{"kind":"para","text":"While you might have found it odd that I had taken the time to label off all of these places and dive deeper into the definitions of these areas (though I tried to explain myself as thoroughly as possible that all scripture is important (not just for information but for our transformation)), I think this is a useful tool to us later on. We can keep an eye out for some of these names later, and reference what they mean, but I think you will find it interesting that a lot of these are not mentioned again. We don't know what comes of some of these places. They just seem to disappear and get swallowed up by the more prominent places."},{"kind":"para","text":"Granted, the more prominent sections of the map should become more (I'd rather live in the house of bread, than watch-height), but they are written here for us to illustrate that they were places with real people who were creating lives and lineages and legacies. We should not just skimp over any portion of scripture simply because we don't want to take the time to dig into it. Digging into scripture is fun. It's a little messy, and sometimes we don't find much in the end, but other times we are able to find little pearls, or nuggets of gold that were well worth the effort and time it takes to dig in there."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":39},{"start":40,"end":48},{"start":49,"end":51}]},
    "20": {"chapter":20,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 20"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: We have had a marathon with these last five chapters or so, talking about different parts of the land, and how it would be split up. I did my best not to just give \"information\" for the sake of information, but to give something tangible and useful to us as we continue to read through scripture. These chapters with names and locations are sometimes tedious and boring, and I am hopeful to find some use for them other than to just give us historical information, or factual information. We must remember that ALL scripture is profitable for our souls matter!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"All scripture is given by inspiration of God, and is profitable for doctrine, for reproof, for correction, for instruction in righteousness:","ref":"2 Timothy 3:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"Those chapters that seem to be name heavy, are useful chapters! They can be viewed as well as the rest of scripture as far as leading to the Savior, and applicable to our spiritual health needs."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we get a really important aspect of this land that has been somewhat forgotten since we entered into this book of scripture. There is plenty of spiritual application here that I think is important for us to focus on, because it shows the heart and mind of the Lord towards His people so that we don't forget about a very important (and key) characteristic for unity. That is, mercy."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":6,"title":"The Cities of Refuge","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We spoke about this quite extensively in the book of Deuteronomy."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"When the LORD thy God hath cut off the nations, whose land the LORD thy God giveth thee, and thou succeedest them, and dwellest in their cities, and in their houses; Thou shalt separate three cities for thee in the midst of thy land, which the LORD thy God giveth thee to possess it. Thou shalt prepare thee a way, and divide the coasts of thy land, which the LORD thy God giveth thee to inherit, into three parts, that every slayer may flee thither. And this is the case of the slayer, which shall flee thither, that he may live: Whoso killeth his neighbour ignorantly, whom he hated not in time past; As when a man goeth into the wood with his neighbour to hew wood, and his hand fetcheth a stroke with the axe to cut down the tree, and the head slippeth from the helve, and lighteth upon his neighbour, that he die; he shall flee unto one of those cities, and live: Lest the avenger of the blood pursue the slayer, while his heart is hot, and overtake him, because the way is long, and slay him; whereas he was not worthy of death, inasmuch as he hated him not in time past.","ref":"Deuteronomy 19:1-6"},{"kind":"para","text":"Is this to say that God wants a way of escape for a murderer? Obviously not (we spoke about how this was an accidental incident, and not intentional). People say that God is not just, or that He is too mean or cruel, and yet we see God giving a form of escape to a man who has caused a quite serious accident. Now people will read this and say, \"God is too forgiving, He doesn't know how to enact proper justice.” Which is it? He is either too cruel, or too forgiving for these people. May we just admit to the fact that God is just? He is our even balance, and the scale of law is weighed by Him. But even in this instance we see a version of Christ here."},{"kind":"para","text":"We ALL (reading this today) have a city of refuge in Jesus. We do not deserve to be forgiven for our actions, and we all (in some fashion) INTENTIONALLY chose to break God's law, knowing FULL WELL it is God's law. We have chosen to go against our conscience and lie, and steal, and cheat, and God has granted us forgiveness for that. We cannot view this and say, \"God is too forgiving, He is letting a murderer go,\" and then look at ourselves and say, \"well, at least I'm not like this murderer.” No. You are worse. Because you do it with intentionality, rather than by accident. These cities gave a fighting chance to someone that was innocent and needed the help. God is showing mercy by providing these cities as places of refuge."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think it is important to note that God has to remind Joshua about this. Imagine if God had stayed silent on the case. This has been something that God has reminded the Israelites (and Joshua specifically) about over and over again. It was written about in Numbers, it was readdressed in Deuteronomy, it was well understood and well known by the Israelites living in this land at this point, and yet no one said anything about it. God (I personally believe) had a right not to bring it up again. But God is Just and Holy, and so He finds it important to remind them about mercy."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think if God didn't mention this to them again, we would see a very dark Israel in the coming books. They would be merciless people, and the innocent people that they were supposed to stand up for, and plead with would be thrown under the bus, just like they were doing in all the other nations. The elders of the city would be judging unjustly, and they would all be back to square one again. They would say like the elders in Matthew;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"...What is that to us? see thou to that.","ref":"Matthew 27:4b"},{"kind":"para","text":"It's evident that in the New Testament they were not judging correctly. If God ignored this here, they would have started off like this…"}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It would not be \"every man for himself.” These people were to take care of each other. The church should be run in this same manner. I know there are a lot of people who do stupid things in the church house intentionally. They try and make it out to be some sort of accident or happenstance, but we all know that they just didn't think through the situation, and people get hurt by it, and the leadership (I'd say 90% of the time) just ignores it and protects that individual. Then we have someone who is speaking the truth, and is completely innocent in the matter who is being ignored and shunned by the leaders in the church. Like with all things, there needs to be balance in our judgement during times of strain and problems. We should be able to take care of issues that were accidents with wisdom, and problems that are obvious intentional mistakes with nobility. This doesn't mean we should cut off someone’s head, or stone them to death because they made a mistake. Rather, we should just be wise about how we take care of the problem, otherwise nobody is going to trust you with their next one. The innocent people are going to be mistreated, and the people who are just not thinking will be put on a pedestal."},{"kind":"para","text":"You might think to yourself, \"My church doesn't do that. I think I've sat in a pretty good standing as far as I've seen at my church.” You are absolutely wrong. It wouldn't take much digging to find out that your church leadership falls for the same traps…"}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If someone was innocent, they were protected by the city of refuge until the high priest died. That could take some time. If someone was upset even after the death of the high priest, it is likely that this person needs some therapy and help from his neighbor. After that much time has gone by, there should be no thought on it, or ill-will. Sure, it's still sad. But it should have been largely forgiven, if the leadership is able to work things out correctly."},{"kind":"para","text":"Now we have the question, \"Well, what if you are found innocent, and the high priest (at that time) is like one hundred years old, and dies the next day? Would I be let go from the protection of the elders?” There are stipulations that the Israelites had to be wise about following. I think it would be sufficient that people who are running there for safety, be given the extension of the next high priest as well. I'm sure that is how they worked that out. Thankfully (for those of us today), we don't have to worry about our forgiven protection from the length of life from our high priest because He still lives! As long as He lives, we are protected."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":9,"title":"Cities Appointed","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These places should be quite familiar to us at this point. They were places that have been set up by their ancestors. They were places of refuge for them as well. \"Kadesh\" was a place during the time of Abraham known as “Enmishpat.\""},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And they returned, and came to Enmishpat, which is Kadesh, and smote all the country of the Amalekites, and also the Amorites, that dwelt in Hazezontamar.","ref":"Genesis 14:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"It literally means \"holy place.” Abraham knows the place well. Actually, there was a well STATIONED on the edge of Kadesh."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wherefore the well was called Beerlahairoi; behold, it is between Kadesh and Bered.","ref":"Genesis 16:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Shechem\" is another familiar location. Jacob lived here for a little time."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Jacob came to Shalem, a city of Shechem, which is in the land of Canaan, when he came from Padanaram; and pitched his tent before the city.","ref":"Genesis 33:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"\"Kirjatharba\" is a recurring location as well. It is where Sarah (Abraham's wife) died."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Sarah died in Kirjatharba; the same is Hebron in the land of Canaan: and Abraham came to mourn for Sarah, and to weep for her.","ref":"Genesis 23:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"The name seems to change to Hebron at some point (probably during this time of Joshua's reign). \"Kirjatharba\" means \"City of Arba.” Obviously, now that the Israelites own the city, Arba is no longer a part of that leadership. So they change it to Hebron, which means “association.\" From here on out, that will be the name of this city. These cities were well-known locations that hold some significance to the life and lineage of the Israelites, and they seem to be in a decent triangle to their central location. It seems that it is mostly centered around Judah. That is, of course, where the kingly tribe comes from. They are the ones that have been given some special accommodations for their land because they will be the ones to bring forth salvation. The Lord comes from this tribe. That is also (of course) the tribe in which David comes from."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Every tribe was to have three cities of refuge. Reuben, Gad, and Manasseh were on the other side of the Jordan, and so they would not be able to cross the Jordan to get to the Israelites’ cities of refuge. So they established one place for each of them, and I think it was presumed that they would continue to build from there. I don't know if they ever planted more cities of refuge. There aren’t too many stories in Scripture involving refuge for the innocent."},{"kind":"para","text":"That's the sad reality of the sin of the land. We are looking far more into how we can get even with someone who has harmed us, rather than how we can be forgiving to them. Even when we come to the story of David, he finds no refuge in his homeland. He finds better refuge with the enemy than he does with his own people, and ends up living with them for a time, and in caves wandering around in the wilderness. Even these places are mentioned before, though. These are places that these tribes should be somewhat familiar with. God had already separated these places for them when we looked at Deuteronomy."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"That the slayer might flee thither, which should kill his neighbour unawares, and hated him not in times past; and that fleeing unto one of these cities he might live: Namely, Bezer in the wilderness, in the plain country, of the Reubenites; and Ramoth in Gilead, of the Gadites; and Golan in Bashan, of the Manassites.","ref":"Deuteronomy 4:42-43"},{"kind":"para","text":"Before they took the land, they had examples of what cities of refuge would look like for them. They had places to go even before they established themselves in the Promised Land. God takes care of His children, including when they are not where they are supposed to be."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have a city of refuge today, who is in the person of Jesus Christ. He was an innocent man who was killed by intentional murderers. Every person that chanted \"away with him, away with him, crucify him\" killed the Lord intentionally. Yet, they (including Judas) were all given Christ as refuge for their despicable acts of blasphemy. That city is still open for all and any that would come to Him. It's available to the most heinous of us if we would just turn and run to Him. He's given us a place of escape."},{"kind":"para","text":"The high priest is HIM, and we don't have to worry about whether or not we are protected because He has already died and has proven that He has dominion over it! He can protect your eternity if you would trust Him with it. If we are not willing to run to that refuge, we are equally as guilty as the chanters of His day. We will not go unpunished for our voluntary slaughter of an innocent man. He has placed the burden of salvation on the shoulders of every individual in this world."},{"kind":"para","text":"We all walk around aching and lulling about our needs and turmoil, but we can just turn and trust in Him with our burdens. He will relieve us, and He will comfort us, and He will change us, renew us, and set us free. That's the power of Christ's salvation. That's the power of His sacrifice for us. I've felt it, and countless others have felt it, and we live in new life, with new power, and new strategies and plans. God has a specific direction for every individual if we would just go to Him and follow. He leads us to safety in His city of refuge."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"That by two immutable things, in which it was impossible for God to lie, we might have a strong consolation, who have fled for refuge to lay hold upon the hope set before us: Which hope we have as an anchor of the soul, both sure and stedfast, and which entereth into that within the veil; Whither the forerunner is for us entered, even Jesus, made an high priest for ever after the order of Melchisedec.","ref":"Hebrews 6:18-20"},{"kind":"para","text":"He is our forerunner. He knows where the refuge is, and He wants to lead us there. We must trust and follow Him. Otherwise, we will be in the wilderness as a guilty man with no room to plead in our impending judgement."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":9}]},
    "21": {"chapter":21,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 21"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Joshua spoke about the cities of refuge that each tribe was supposed to have for when mistakes and problems arose. They would always be able to find safety no matter their situation. He takes special care to make sure the cities are places that are far enough away for murderers to get to, but close enough for people who are innocent to run to if there was a need."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we have one last tribe to speak about. We have done a lot of land dividing already, and it has been quite the trek as we've journeyed across each place, and I certainly don't want to bog down readers with a plethora of more information (this book has been quite information heavy already), but I believe there is a need to stop and consider this last tribe, because they are extremely important to not forget about."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"The Levites Inheritance","entries":[{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have read over and over again throughout Deuteronomy that the Levites were not going to inherit any land. Their inheritance was of the Lord, and so their main focus (and job) was to minister to the other tribes. I truly believe this was the Lord's way of doing two things."},{"kind":"para","text":"Firstly, it was the Lord’s way of requiring the other tribes to think and remember that all of the tribes were to take care of each other. It would be easy for them to forget one or the other (or eventually become dull and calloused to their service to the point where they are just doing it out of necessity rather than out of love for each other)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Secondly, I believe it was to give credence to the fact that ministering was (and still is) one of the most important institutions that God has ordained. No other country was like this. They didn't have a people set apart to love one another and point everyone back to their God. The other countries had priests and the sorts, but those priests were more of a good luck token, or a person that they could take their burdens to, to be forgiven. It's a lot like our priests today."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are church denominations that think that going to the priest and asking for forgiveness, or asking for a sacrifice will suffice for their needs. The Levites were not to be like these priests. They were to be people who would give sacrifice, certainly, but it was only so they reveal and remember the ultimate sacrifice that will be given to them in the future (Jesus Christ of course). The priest will forget this, in due time, and they will fall into the same trap as all the other countries and people around them. They will just be doing their religious duty, and not sacrificing out of a desire to see God's salvation. They will be ministering out of duty, and not doing it because the Lord wants them to continue in unity with one another."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Levitical tribe will be hugely neglected and forsaken. Now, of course, not all the Old Testament prophets are Levites, but some of the best and most well-known are, and they are all forsaken. Moses, Aaron, Miriam, Samuel, Jeremiah, Ezekiel, Ezra, and Malachi will all have their share of leading the people through prophecy, and you will see that the Israelites don't take them seriously. I mean, Jeremiah is known as \"the weeping prophet.\" I wonder why?"}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So we will get a look at the cities the Levites are going to get in a moment, but it should be noted that they will be spread out across the twelve different tribes. They were to be ministers to all of the land of Israel, and Israel would do very well to remember them and consider them for every task they are faced with. The Levites were to turn the others into priests with them. That is, of course, not what will happen."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Levites will just be separated, and people will only go to them when they need something serious and dire, not when they are searching out the Lord. People are dying from moving the ark? Call the priests! The people are being overthrown in battle and don't know what to do next? Call the priests! The people are so gone from their sin and they can't figure out how to sacrifice properly because it's been too long since the last time they have? Call the priest! They were like the Ghostbusters of the tribes."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":7,"title":"The Cities Dispersed Accordingly","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Kohathites were the ones taking care of the sanctuary at this time. This is where Aaron and his family came from (if you don't remember). So when it references \"the Kohathites\" it is just talking about the priests through the people of Aaron. We will see in a moment that there are other names mentioned here who had different responsibilities in the sanctuary. But this portion took care of the sanctuary and the main objects used for their sacrifice."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Gershon were placed to take care of the coverings and curtain in the wilderness."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the charge of the sons of Gershon in the tabernacle of the congregation shall be the tabernacle, and the tent, the covering thereof, and the hanging for the door of the tabernacle of the congregation, And the hangings of the court, and the curtain for the door of the court, which is by the tabernacle, and by the altar round about, and the cords of it for all the service thereof.","ref":"Numbers 3:25-26"},{"kind":"para","text":"(You can find all the information of what each lineage is in charge of in Numbers 3). We must remember that they didn't have a set place for their sacrifice during their time in the wilderness. They will have a (somewhat) set place now because they have land to develop, but before they had to constantly pick up and move this sanctuary and tent to the areas that they were moving to next."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don't know if you've ever been camping before, but when I was younger we would go camping in a trailer or fifth wheel. This had a kitchen, a bathroom, power, two bedrooms, and it could carry around ATVs and dirt bikes, and everything your heart desires. It was a house on wheels. When I got older (and had my own family) we decided to go camping on our own. We bought a tent, some firewood, food for the evening (breakfast for the morning), a little propane heater, sleeping bags (the whole deal). There is a stark difference between camping with tents and camping with an RV. Setting up the tent, building the fire (with little to no supplies), cooking over the fire (rather than on a stovetop), having no walls for protection... It was an interesting experience to say the least. Imagine we had to do this AND pack up our sanctuary that we serve the Lord in every single day. That seems hectic. They, obviously, had a lot of people to help them move things when they needed to be moved, and that is the reason that there are certain parts of the priestly tribe that are responsible for certain parts of the tabernacles moving. But even with all the help, it couldn't have been an easy task to set everything up, and clean everything up all the time."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The children of Merari were the sanctuary guards. They kept the wrong people from trying to enter the tabernacle and chance themselves getting killed. They had learned a lesson pretty quickly through Aaron’s sons why it's important that the right people go in with the right abilities, and right service before anything is touched or done. These children were also responsible for carrying the pillars and boards of the tabernacle."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":12,"title":"The Levites Job","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These cities were all scattered throughout all the land, and if you were to look at it on a map, you would see that they were all pretty evenly placed. There was not supposed to be a \"central city\" for the Levites. It should have been easy for the Israelites to turn every way and see the Levites ministering. It should have been easy for anyone in the land to know that God was using His people here. Hebron is a place that will be talked about quite a bit throughout Scripture, and so it's a key place to note as we read along. It seems that it might have kind of been the central place for the Levitical tribe, but it was never meant to be this way."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will also see (later on) that there were a lot of tasks that the Levites were to be a part of. Moses had mentioned (already) that the Levites were not just there to give sacrifices for sins. Yes, this was part of their job, but that was not their primary job. Their job was to point EVERYONE (no matter who they were in the cities) back to the Lord. The people will continuously forget the Lord, and the Levites were to be the people that reminded them of who it is that they are serving. They were to do this in a few ways, but the real emphasis was to be on teaching God's Word to people."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And that ye may teach the children of Israel all the statutes which the LORD hath spoken unto them by the hand of Moses.","ref":"Leviticus 10:11"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"They shall teach Jacob thy judgments, and Israel thy law: they shall put incense before thee, and whole burnt sacrifice upon thine altar.","ref":"Deuteronomy 33:10"},{"kind":"para","text":"There is nothing more satisfying than teaching God's Word to others who need a reminder. I have a Bible study every day of the week, and it's not just to keep myself occupied. The purpose of the Bible studies is to keep others in reminder to the Lord, but also to teach myself as I go through scripture. I want to be refreshed with those that I am refreshing in God's Word. The Levites could learn this (and they do for a season) and utilize this as a means of help to their own spirits as well as to the spirits of everyone around them. They don't do this much, and so it dwindles out until Christ comes again. During that time, the priests are mostly teaching people for profit, not for unity. Christ would be the one to flip that around, and the disciples would carry that banner to their death."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Caleb already inherited the villages and fields, so the Levites couldn't possess them. Even if he didn't, the Levites still couldn't take any land specifically. They were to be taken care of, and that is a very important reminder to the Israelites and to the Levites. If the Israelites wanted the Levites to minister successfully, they would do well to be sure that the Levites are well taken care of according to the Law of God."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":19,"title":"Aaron's Cities","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have a more extensive list here that illustrates what the Levites are inheriting. I won't spend time defining these specific cities because we have already done that to pretty much all of these in the previous chapters. But it is important to note that the list is detailed. They took special care to write down the names and provinces of every single city so that there was no argument about who got what during this time. I don't believe the Levites had any room to argue about their dwelling, honestly. They knew that this was all they were going to get, and it was going to be a benefit for them that the other tribes would make sure they are taken care of. Them, the poor, and the widows."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":26,"title":"Kohath's Cities","entries":[{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If we did decide to break down this list of names (which again, I won't do because we have already established these names), we would see that these cities were not just made up for the occasion. All of these cities are matched to the cities we have mentioned before, and none of them are randomized. They were specific for these people. They kept their kind quite close together, and that was on purpose."},{"kind":"para","text":"The different tribes would have different skills for ministering, and they should all use their skills to help each other balance the ministry. That is how it should be in the church today. Everyone has different skills, and it is a shame that people would argue over whose skills are better, or whose ministry is more needful compared to each other."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he gave some, apostles; and some, prophets; and some, evangelists; and some, pastors and teachers; For the perfecting of the saints, for the work of the ministry, for the edifying of the body of Christ: Till we all come in the unity of the faith, and of the knowledge of the Son of God, unto a perfect man, unto the measure of the stature of the fulness of Christ: That we henceforth be no more children, tossed to and fro, and carried about with every wind of doctrine, by the sleight of men, and cunning craftiness, whereby they lie in wait to deceive; But speaking the truth in love, may grow up into him in all things, which is the head, even Christ: From whom the whole body fitly joined together and compacted by that which every joint supplieth, according to the effectual working in the measure of every part, maketh increase of the body unto the edifying of itself in love.","ref":"Ephesians 4:11-16"},{"kind":"para","text":"Everyone should be working together for the cause of helping everyone else work together. The Levites had one primary office (that was their sacrifice), but not all of them ministered in the same way. We could look to Eli and Samuel as an appropriate representation of this. Eli was a great priest, but Samuel did things better. Not because he was a better priest, but because he was willing to take what he was taught and expound upon what Eli had done. Eli was skilled in his duties, but God prepared Samuel in a different way. It seems (and I could be wrong, but I'm just evaluating scripture here), that Eli stayed put in his duties. If he wanted to minister, people would have to come to him. Samuel, on the other hand, went from city to city and gave the word of God. He couldn't sit still."},{"kind":"para","text":"Was one wrong and the other right? No. I think they were both good at what they did, but they both had different skills. If they used their skills together, they would have been an unstoppable force. Of course, by the time that Samuel is old enough to take the reins, Eli was too old to work with Samuel, but I imagine that is on purpose. Imagine the kinds of things that Samuel and Eli would have done together! God needed the children of Israel in a specific place during that time, however, and so Samuel takes his skills and uses them alone (and against the children of Israel because they were stubborn at that time)."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Levites all had different skills, and different placements, and they would have been wise to work on each other’s skills! The church should be found doing the same thing today. I may not be the greatest person to speak to about what a teen should do as a Christian and dating, but I should know someone in the church who knows. Others might come to me about a man who is struggling with depression (I know how that feels), because others have never experienced that. We will find, when we are helping and serving others, it actually fills us up with joy and compassion."}]}]},{"start":27,"end":33,"title":"Gershon's Cities","entries":[{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Levitical tribe seems to be huge at this time. Perhaps this is the reason that God chose this tribe out of all of them to minister. They were in the majority, and so they had the manpower to spread out and minister, whereas the other tribes still had a lot of growing (numerically and spiritually) to do. We still have one more portion left!"}]}]},{"start":34,"end":40,"title":"Merari's Cities","entries":[{"start":40,"end":40,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We will notice that through each of these divisions, they point out a city of refuge for the slayer. The priests were not perfect people. They were not sinless people. They never WILL BE sinless saints. The priests today only play the part. They are not the called-out Levites like Scripture enforces. Becoming \"believer priests\" does not make us of the tribe of Levi. Rather, it makes us of the tribe of Judah. We have seen, and we will continue to notice that Judah was anything BUT a priestly tribe. They were dysfunctional to say the least. But that is the redeeming tribe. That is the kingly tribe. That is the tribe in which Jesus Christ comes through and adopts us into. We become a part of His people, and what a match we are to them."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are as dysfunctional as this tribe, but the Lord can save even the most dysfunctional of the bunch of us. The cities of refuge are a testament to the fact that the holiest of us are sinners in need of salvation. You wouldn't think that the tribe that would be responsible for ministering would need a city to run to if they accidentally kill someone. How could they accidentally kill someone? I'm not exactly sure, but the city was there for them just in case. They had a place to go for sanctuary if it ever came down to it. We will see that holy people will do very unholy things as we progress through Scripture, and they don't do it because it was an accident either. They do it intentionally, and they won't get away with it for too long before the Lord deals with it."},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe that if the holy people in the Old Testament stayed their course, much of Israel would have stayed their course as well. But the priestly tribe will become calloused to their ministry (just like many ministers today), and it will cause others to grow calloused to their work and living as well. The spirit we give off (especially as leaders in the faith) is the same spirit that everyone around us will follow. You won't even have to say anything, and people will have a bad spirit if you have a bad spirit as well."}]}]},{"start":41,"end":42,"title":"The Division Summation","entries":[{"start":42,"end":42,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The summation is, the Levites had plenty of cities to dwell in. Forty-eight cities is a lot to take for themselves and be sufficient with. The Levites should be consistently moving from house to house in their cities to keep up with the people and continuously care for the people, but the people were also to come to the Levites for care. The Levites’ homes would be their ministering dwellings. There would be ONE place of worship, of course, but several churches (the Levites’ houses). Obviously, the church is not a picture yet (there is still time for that to come in the New Testament), but there is a strong example of it here."},{"kind":"para","text":"Imagine if California looked like this today. If we took forty-eight cities of California (which isn't very many), and we scattered men of God through every one of them, and they all planted small churches, we would see a revival in a heartbeat (at least for a time). It would have expanded across the land quite rapidly and lives would be forever changed."}]}]},{"start":43,"end":45,"title":"A Restful Ending","entries":[{"start":43,"end":43,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The LORD gave them the land. They could not acclaim that they did it by their own strength. They would have to give glory to the Lord. The only reason they had any success was because the Lord granted them that. We have learned that since the beginning of this book: God gives the means to success."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you are not successful today in your life or in your ventures, it is likely that the Lord is the reason you are not. Perhaps it's your sin, but far more than that, God knows what He is doing, and what you need and don't need. What may look like failure to you is actually what God wants to use for your future. He will work on you, of course, but that doesn't mean that your failure is not useful."}]},{"start":44,"end":44,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The LORD gives them rest as well. No one could attack them. They were untouchable for the time being. God was dwelling with His people, and His people were dwelling in His promise, and everything is copacetic and amazing!"}]},{"start":45,"end":45,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If only it stays this way. We can close the book up right here and be content with the fairytale ending that is being told. We can finish this chapter and say, \"Boy, what a great story! I believe that the Israelites have come out on top, and they shall remain there now. Everything looks good for them, and I'm glad they can finally settle in.\" Of course, that would be lying to ourselves. We will find that things don't stay this way, and it's a rollercoaster with the Israelites. Again, I've mentioned this several times now, but Israel means \"wrestles with God.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"In a wrestling match, you are never sure who is going to win. You always think one side has it, and then suddenly the tables turn, and the other side seems to dominate. You are rooting for one side because you know they are strong and willing, but the other side seems to be putting up a good fight. That is the relationship dynamic of Israel and their Lord. They will battle against His Will every step of the way, and God will give them what they want, letting them think that they know what’s best, and then they find out it was never really worth it. They ALWAYS lose the wrestling match and eventually have to submit to the Will of the Lord. As should we…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":33},{"start":34,"end":40},{"start":41,"end":42},{"start":43,"end":45}]},
    "22": {"chapter":22,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 22"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, we were reminded about what the Levites were going to inherit. They were not promised land, but they were promised the presence of the Lord. They, however, needed a place to sleep and live in order to do their work for the Lord, so they were given dwellings among the other tribes, dispersed in a way so that they could easily minister to the individual needs of the people. They were reminded about their jobs, and given an apt description about how each of the Levitical lineages were to be split up in the cities."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we get a little story about the two and a half tribes that have land on the other side of the Jordan. They are sent away with a blessing, but they do something that gets the children of Israel worried and ready to go to war with them."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":6,"title":"The Land on the Other Side","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have talked about it quite a bit at this point, but these two and a half tribes didn't want the land on this side of the river. They wanted to take land on the other side (the side where they spent their time in the wilderness). Why they rejected the promised land is beyond me, but I don't think it was a smart move on their part. Sadly, that is the move that most people in this world make."},{"kind":"para","text":"They have the opportunity to live in a better land, and they are given every reason possible to take that land, and to claim God's promises, they may even help the Christians get there, but they don't want it for themselves. They have found reward in the wilderness. They have found purpose in their trials and wandering. They think they are better suited to live in the confusion than to live in God's freedom. That is not a good place to live. These tribes still had to put in the effort to help their brothers in the Lord, but they were already promised a land. So Joshua is keeping that promise here, and providing the reality of it."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The truth is, there will be no rest for these people until the Lord comes. They were certainly entering into rest, but they won't stay there. They abuse their rest in the Lord, and that eventually turns against them, and they will suffer the consequences for not just seeking God and resting in His promises. At this moment (as Joshua is illustrating), these tribes had freedom (for a time), and so these two and a half tribes could hold the promise they were given by Moses to take that land they wanted so badly on the other side of the Jordan."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Just because they were separated from the rest of the tribes and living in their own land did not permit them to reject their calling from the Lord. We will see that all the tribes eventually reject their calling and fall away from the Lord (over and over again), and they will find it difficult to survive because of it. Really (essentially), that is why God is giving them the structure they needed. If they didn't follow that structure, they were just causing great harm to themselves more than to anyone else. They could either choose to follow God's structure or the world around them. Joshua will speak about that in the coming chapters."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua gave them a blessing to go. They go home, and they are tasked with prospering and living holy lives for the Lord. They will have a difficult time where they are stationed being separate from their brothers in the other tribes. They are to all work together, worship together, and provide for each other, and when you have to travel such a distance to do so, it makes it very difficult to unite."},{"kind":"para","text":"We used to go to a church in Palmdale, yet we live right down the street from a massive church (with the same doctrine) that we could go to. We chose to drive twenty minutes out of our way to go to this church and unite with these believers because of the growth (spiritually) and feeding we received from this church. It would be far easier to go to the church down the street than to go to the church in Palmdale, but the unity with the church was worth it. It's worth making the trip to unite with these believers. It's worth staying in one of the most expensive states in America to unite with these believers. It's worth the hassle and turmoil that we face on a daily course, because we can unite with these believers. It's not always easy, but it's always worth it."},{"kind":"para","text":"These two and a half tribes are going to have to go out of their way to unite with the rest of the people. Do they do it? I don't know how often, but I'm sure there are plenty of them that do. Will it be for the Lord? Not always (if hardly ever)..."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":9,"title":"A Blessing","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua gives them provisions to help them start. He could have just said, \"Well, you have your land promised to you already. You have fought a good fight with us. Go ahead and take the land you wanted.\" But he provides a starter pack for them. He gives them some materials to start them off, some cattle, and clothes. He then says, \"I pray it grows from here, and the Lord blesses you with more so that you become rich.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"These tribes are essentially already rich in the eyes of the world at this time. They were being taken care of by the Lord, and they had more than they knew they deserved. But the only way to truly and genuinely enjoy those riches and continue to receive them is if they stayed consistent in faith towards the Lord. I've said it before and I will say it a million more times. If you have the Lord, you are rich already."}]}]},{"start":10,"end":12,"title":"A Massive Altar","entries":[{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I'm assuming this altar was quite large. They must have taken a bit of time to build this thing. They were serious about what this altar was going to represent. So they come to the river and think it's a good time to build an altar. This could be a whole slew of implications. What was the altar for? Why couldn't they wait? Was this a pagan practice?"},{"kind":"para","text":"We see Abraham throughout Genesis continuously build altars to the Lord. He built altars as testimonies of what God had done for him. Anyone that saw the altars may not know what they represent, but they would certainly know something happened there. Isaac seemed to be a well builder for his testimonies. These two and a half tribes thought it was sufficient to build an altar. Not just any altar, but apparently a very prominent and large altar."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They thought of this altar as a pagan practice, and they were extremely upset. That escalated so fast. They didn't even establish themselves in their own land yet, and they are already going to war with one another. Again, they are to provide and permit unity with the tribes (at all costs), not division. There was no discourse, just straight to action. Understandably so, because they didn't want to fall away from the Lord, or have the Lord against them suddenly and quickly. They've worked too hard to lose it all so quickly. I don't know why they jumped the gun so quickly and assumed these tribes were building a pagan altar."},{"kind":"para","text":"It seems strange that they would think that these tribes had fallen away even before they established themselves. I think the other tribes were already a bit bitter towards these two and a half tribes because they already had assured land. But all the other tribes had the bigger blessing in the promised land."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":20,"title":"The Accusation","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They had their richest representatives go forward to converse with these two and a half tribes. They wanted to show strength and power in these men's eyes so they knew they meant business. They were all from prominent houses, and they were in charge of \"thousands\" in each tribe."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The accusations come flying quite quickly. Notice (once again) that they don't go before the Lord first to ask about this problem. They just assume what is taking place and take the problem into their own hands. That is a common theme with these children of Israel (at least until we come to David and the Psalms)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus gives us the best representation, because He is constantly seen in prayer towards the Lord every opportunity He gets. Even Paul makes it clear that we should be in consistent and constant prayer."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Pray without ceasing.","ref":"1 Thessalonians 5:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"I'll give them the benefit of the doubt, however, and say that they were too worried about there being a rebellion and falling away from the Lord to ask any questions. They wanted to fix this quickly and take care of these tribes. They weren't going to live in their land anyway, and this (to them) was an act of rebellion that should not be taken casually."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"To the Israelites, they were going to experience something they had already experienced before. They didn't want to be on the Lord's bad side. If we read this in context, they have a bit of a warped view of the Lord. They are not living in fear of God, they are living AFRAID of God. They don't understand that their experiences (like the one in Peor) were for a reminder and example. If you don't know what happened in Peor (or perhaps you forget), it's written about in Numbers 25."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Moses said unto the judges of Israel, Slay ye every one his men that were joined unto Baalpeor.","ref":"Numbers 25:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"You will notice, however, that the Lord dealt with the individual people who worshipped Peor, rather than just destroying the entire nation because of it. They mistakenly and must have forgotten that part. We will also notice that God forgives them (who turn back to Him) because He is merciful!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Phinehas, the son of Eleazar, the son of Aaron the priest, hath turned my wrath away from the children of Israel, while he was zealous for my sake among them, that I consumed not the children of Israel in my jealousy. Wherefore say, Behold, I give unto him my covenant of peace: And he shall have it, and his seed after him, even the covenant of an everlasting priesthood; because he was zealous for his God, and made an atonement for the children of Israel.","ref":"Numbers 25:11-13"},{"kind":"para","text":"You might also notice that it's the same priest that is dealing with this problem again. He is living in the confidence that God has made a covenant of peace with him due to addressing this issue once before, so he is taking the steps necessary to make sure that these other tribes are not ruining it for everyone else. But we will soon see that the altar was not for Baal. The altar had pure intentions."},{"kind":"para","text":"You know what's really sad? People, today, cannot tell the difference between someone who is serving the Lord, or someone who is serving a fake god. Satan has done a good job at making disciples and replicas that look and act the part. There will be plenty of illustrations of this throughout scripture, but there is so much worldly influence in Christianity today that it is difficult to tell if Christians are genuinely doing what they do for the Lord or if they are doing it to get something else out of them. That is why many people have their guard up against Christians. We can be genuine with them, and their first thought is, \"Okay, so what kind of Christian are you? Are you a Mormon 'Christian,' a Catholic 'Christian,' a Presbyterian 'Christian,' a Baptist 'Christian?'\" There are a lot of types of \"Christians\" these days, and they all have their flaws and downfalls."},{"kind":"para","text":"I am very cautious about introducing my Christianity to others with a denominational connection. I try my best to not even talk about church if I am sharing the gospel with someone. I don't care about what church they go to as much as I care about the Jesus they know. There is only one correct faith, and that is in the Christ of the Bible alone. Your denomination doesn't make you a Christian, only Jesus does that. Your altars don't make you a child of God, only your faith does that. These men were building an altar based on their faith in the Lord, but it won't be viewed in that light..."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is, actually, a really kind gesture. It's the grand definition of peace. That is how they should have started the conversation, honestly. They came out with accusations first, when they should have come out with questions and searched for peace. They said, \"Hey guys, don't worship false gods. Don't turn away from the Lord, and compel us to do likewise. If it's too difficult for you to live on that side of the Jordan, you can come back over here and live in our land. We won't hold anything against you.\" The door of acceptance was open. That is a door we can all afford to leave open."},{"kind":"para","text":"Many of us close that door very early on in our Christian walks. We have been hurt by someone (most likely) that we had tried to accept, and that made us guard that door and lock it. May we not be found trapping that door, because it's the only one that provides peace to people who don't know peace. They are like children, who don't know better, and when we shut that door, we are keeping them out of receiving understanding."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, we have (yet) another example of God dealing with the individual people who struggled with their sin. If you are under the impression that his whole family died with him, I will not hold that against you. We don't have to agree on all things, but I do believe there are many more than his family that died that day. I personally do not believe his family died with him, but rather this is specifically speaking about the \"congregation of Israel\" suffering from Achan's sin. They went to battle that day and lost because of what he did. They all suffered because they didn't take care of the problem in their own camp. People died with him because of it. The congregants dealt with it, and they had victory the next day."},{"kind":"para","text":"These are all fair examples to bring up, and so they would be wise to try and plead with these two and a half tribes if they are falling astray (especially so quickly after they had finally received their land). They are trying to convince them to stay the course. May we be seen doing the same. Take some notes here. Most of us go out to talk to people with swords and arrows thinking we are going to have to attack. We assume the worst in people, and so we stand in offense ready to strike if the opportunity calls. We plead with people behind a shield."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Put on the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil. For we wrestle not against flesh and blood, but against principalities, against powers, against the rulers of the darkness of this world, against spiritual wickedness in high places. Wherefore take unto you the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to withstand in the evil day, and having done all, to stand. Stand therefore, having your loins girt about with truth, and having on the breastplate of righteousness; And your feet shod with the preparation of the gospel of peace; Above all, taking the shield of faith, wherewith ye shall be able to quench all the fiery darts of the wicked. And take the helmet of salvation, and the sword of the Spirit, which is the word of God:","ref":"Ephesians 6:11-17"},{"kind":"para","text":"That is not a wise way to plead with people. If we are going to plead with people, we should have our shield of faith on, but that should not be what we stand behind intentionally stabbing people with the word of God. Now, I will say, we should certainly go towards those that are without."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Walk in wisdom toward them that are without, redeeming the time. Let your speech be alway with grace, seasoned with salt, that ye may know how ye ought to answer every man.","ref":"Colossians 4:5-6"},{"kind":"para","text":"But as this verse says, we should be answering with grace and seasoned with salt. We are more prone to answering with opinions and seasoned with dirt. We should be quite weary of constantly being on guard against people who don't know any better. Again, we have been so battle-hardened by past circumstances that we just assume the worst, and so we have our shields up constantly, but we should be pleading with the lost at all costs with our guards down unless they are not willing to listen."},{"kind":"para","text":"There will be use for your shields, but it should not be what causes you to attack someone. Shields are for defending. Swords are for defending too (mind you). There is no part of the armor of God that is for attacking (a study for a different time, no doubt)."}]}]},{"start":21,"end":29,"title":"The Answer","entries":[{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They are making clear that this altar is not meant for worshipping false gods. This altar was meant as a testimony of God's promises. His promises were large, and He has kept every one of them. If they were going to make burnt offerings or meat offerings, it would only be by the commands of God's mouth personally. These two and a half tribes were stating their sensitivity to God's responses towards them. They were willing to seek out the Lord in the matter. The other ten tribes were not at the moment..."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we have some other accusations being thrown towards the ten tribes of Israel. These tribes thought, “Well, if we are cut off from the land, then the other tribes will completely reject us and make it so that our children are turned away from the Lord.\" The tribes having their land on the other side of the Jordan were facing the real problem here, honestly. They were in fear of being disconnected and cut off from their brethren in the Lord. They were afraid that if any of them fall away, it would be likely that they would lead the rest of the tribes astray and they would see their downfall. This is a genuine concern, but it should have been one far before they desired to receive land on the wrong side of the Jordan..."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That was not supposed to be the case. That will certainly become an issue, however. During the time of Christ (especially), the tribes were completely scattered and disconnected. This is why (I personally believe) Jesus chose twelve very distinct and specific people to be His disciples. He was uniting the tribes because that was how it was meant to be since the beginning. This altar was a testament that there was a God in Heaven who loved them and desired to unite them. This would help all the tribes (and all the strangers passing by) to know and remember that they serve a real God who is working with them."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They had one place to worship, and this altar was not that place. This altar was just a reminder. These tribes didn't want to be forgotten about. I don't know if this altar is ever really mentioned again in scripture, but it would serve its purpose quite some time later. There have been people looking for this altar for a while, and they had finally found it on the west side of the Jordan River. They had been looking on the East side, supposing it was not in the Promised Land, but they found remnants of it on the west side. It was a reminder (even still, though it was broken down) that God unites His people."}]}]},{"start":30,"end":34,"title":"Is This Good?","entries":[{"start":30,"end":30,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They were relieved, and they were (probably) excited about this idea. I assume that if this event didn't take place, these other tribes would have surely forgotten about the two and a half tribes on the other side of the Jordan. It was likely that these two and a half tribes would fall victim to the culture around them, and they would be completely neglected. But this single act will set as a reminder to the people, and Joshua thought it was worthy and sufficient to write it down because of how important it was (for him and Israel) to remember each other. That was a continual reminder to the Israelites from Moses before even entering the land. Deuteronomy is filled with \"remember\" others verses (specifically the Levites, the widows, and the strangers)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Take heed to thyself that thou forsake not the Levite as long as thou livest upon the earth.","ref":"Deuteronomy 12:19"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the Levite that is within thy gates; thou shalt not forsake him; for he hath no part nor inheritance with thee.","ref":"Deuteronomy 14:27"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The priests the Levites, and all the tribe of Levi, shall have no part nor inheritance with Israel: they shall eat the offerings of the LORD made by fire, and his inheritance.","ref":"Deuteronomy 18:1"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And thou shalt rejoice in thy feast, thou, and thy son, and thy daughter, and thy manservant, and thy maidservant, and the Levite, the stranger, and the fatherless, and the widow, that are within thy gates.","ref":"Deuteronomy 16:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"Whatever they could do to help each other remember to support one another was a blessing in the land and in the eyes of the Lord."}]},{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They would do well (in the future) to remember the Lord in this way. They would do well to go straight to Him for direction in conflict and in prosperity. They would do well to rely on His power and providence. They will lose that in the very next book, however..."}]},{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They breathed a sigh of relief. They didn't have to fight against their own brothers in the Lord. That would probably give them ample reason to pick up some stones and help them complete this altar (perhaps it was already done?). Either way, they can view this altar and remember those tribes. It would be good for them to take that remembrance with them as they go to the Jordan or pass by and see it. They tried to make it big enough to see from a distance. Perhaps that would be something sufficient for them."}]},{"start":34,"end":34,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"Ed\" means \"witness.\" It seems like a funny name for the altar. It makes it human, honestly. But that was what it was doing. It was a witness to God's unity. It was an evident witness to God's love and support for the tribes. It was a witness that God keeps His promises."},{"kind":"para","text":"Here's the problem though (because there always has to be a problem with sinful human nature), this altar will be more than just a witness. The people will (eventually) use it for the wrong reasons, and they will likely worship fake gods on it. That is what the Israelites were prone to do. That is what all human beings are prone to do. We (so easily, might I add) fall away from the Lord and start abusing good gifts from God. That is what they did with the brazen serpent in the wilderness. It's what they will do with the Ark of God too. It's what they will do with all sorts of tabernacles and trees. Jesus even finds people selling goods in God's temple rather than using it to worship God."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are a fallen people, and it would (no doubt) be quite quick for the Israelites to abuse this altar and start using it for the wrong reasons. Again, they will forget everything in this book of Joshua and completely fall away from the Lord in the very next book. It's almost as if they did not learn anything from Joshua, or they completely rejected Moses and Joshua's teachings and leadership. They didn't pass any information down to the next generation, just like these tribes thought the altar would be for. They neglect their duty to explain the altar to their children, and so their children will utilize this altar for the wrong reasons, and they will turn a good thing into something terrible…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":29},{"start":30,"end":34}]},
    "23": {"chapter":23,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 23"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, the two and a half tribes (the Reubenites, the Gadites, and the half tribe of Manasseh) are going back to the land that was promised to them. In the process they decided to make an altart as a memorial to the Lord providing for them and (hopefully) as a reminder to the Israelites that they serve the true God of creation. They wanted that understanding to be passed down, and all the tribes to be in agreeance to that. The other ten tribes didn't know what they were doing, and were ready to go to war until they explained themselves. They were happy with the prospect because it was in worship to the living God."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see that some time has past, and Joshua is establishing some final thoughts before his death. He isn't sure how much longer he has, but he knows it won't be too long before he is absent from the Israelites. So he wants to give them some direction to (hopefully) help them continue on without his leadership, and to constantly pursue after the Lord with everything they have."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":13,"title":"Love God","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua seems to get some time to relax in God's provision and goodness. He has some time to get old and enjoy the Lord's life for him. That is a blessing for anyone reading this today. That is what a good portion of this world longs for. They want to simply grow old and enjoy life. However, most people that desire this do not know the Lord. They are looking forward to retirement so they can cash out on their retirement income and live a life without the Lord. Those that seek the Lord have a good reason to retire and enjoy life. Those that don't squander their old age. They don't live in wisdom, and instead fall victim to worldly treasures and meaningless pleasures."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I read that Joshua called \"for their heads...\" and chuckled because I'm a child. I know he's speaking about their leaders of their lands, but it just sounds like he is calling for a hit on his own people. I apologize for the brief intermission to explain that, seeing as it's not beneficial to your learning growth. I think it's sometimes funny to read some portions of scripture with the way language has changed today, and this is one of those sections."},{"kind":"para","text":"He was gathering the leaders of the people because he has something important to say to them in his final moments. He doesn't know how much more time he has left on this Earth and (like Moses) he wants to prepare them for after he has passed away, so they can continue forward without him. I don't know how much of him these people saw at this point, but I assume he still had his hands tied in much of the growth that was taking place in the land."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Why did God do this for them? He did it from their faith. He knew these people, not just in the future promises (which is something He established His promise in), but in the moment. He knew them better than they knew themselves. He knew exactly how to get their attention, and how to turn them, and how to move them forward. He knew how long it needed to take, how much energy they needed to exert, and what direction they would go in before they were even born. He had it all planned for them so they could walk in this new land with strides!"},{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua reminds them (as a good leader should always do) that the LORD provided it for them (not their own doing). They were instruments in battle, but it was the Lord that was really doing the fighting. It was His protection, His calling, His enabling, His directing, His moving, His energy, His strength that they were using to get them to this moment."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have already read the great, detailed list of the land being divided for them. If they ever mistakenly wonder what belonged to whom, Joshua has an account for them so they can never argue over what they deserve and what was given. Receipts should be an important file for the Christian. God has His receipts for you."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"That we should be to the praise of his glory, who first trusted in Christ. In whom ye also trusted, after that ye heard the word of truth, the gospel of your salvation: in whom also after that ye believed, ye were sealed with that holy Spirit of promise, Which is the earnest of our inheritance until the redemption of the purchased possession, unto the praise of his glory.","ref":"Ephesians 1:12-14"},{"kind":"para","text":"There are receipts written for the purchases of lands even during the time of Abraham;"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Abraham hearkened unto Ephron; and Abraham weighed to Ephron the silver, which he had named in the audience of the sons of Heth, four hundred shekels of silver, current money with the merchant.","ref":"Genesis 23:16"},{"kind":"para","text":"Receipts were important to purchasing land all throughout history (including to the land purchased here in the United States (you can still see those receipts today hung in history museums throughout the US)). We should take receipts very seriously in our Christian walk. Keeping an account of things is part of our duty of being good stewards for the Lord's inheritance. That truly is what they are doing in this land. They are stewarding it for the Lord. He gave it to them as a possession, but it truly belonged to God, and they would only do well if they left the land under the ownership of God. That is why God still has the receipt for His children. That is why we have Christ as the proof of our redemption from the Lord. He has proof that He purchased you."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For ye are bought with a price: therefore glorify God in your body, and in your spirit, which are God's.","ref":"1 Corinthians 6:20"}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They have been called by God. Moses reminded them of their calling, and that is why Joshua was able to lead them so successfully into this new land. Joshua is now reminding them of their calling, and it would be up to them to lead correctly if they wanted to continue to grow and \"possess their land\" just as the \"Lord your God hath promised unto you.” The promise is not going to be kept if they are not willing to stick to their end of following that promise. Salvation is looked at in the same way."},{"kind":"para","text":"Once you are saved (of course), you are always saved. But I look at salvation like a present that was wrapped at your front door. It sits there, and the doorbell rings, and when you open the door, you see a gift. It says on the outside that it was purchased for you by the Savior. Sadly, many people know that they have this gift, but they haven't opened it. If they don't open it, they don't receive the gift. It sits dormant and useless even though they might have it in their homes. It is only those that actually believe in the value of the gift and open it who truly receive that gift. The Israelites have been gifted this land, and it's up to them to receive and steward it. If they are not willing to do that, they will not possess more, and they will not grow properly. Actually, they don't grow much at all until David takes the throne (spoiler alert)."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Don't even use these fake gods’ names. It's better to avoid them and refrain from being around them as much as humanly possible. Imagine you are in this situation for a second. You get to know someone in the land, and they are quite nice people. They seem like they are loving, caring, and they have a good head on their shoulders. They are compassionate and sympathetic, and they are willing to literally give their shirt right off their back for you if you are in need. They provide food and shelter for those in need, and they are constantly supporting the weak and ministering to the hurting. That sounds like a dream come true. Then they take their idol out and set it in the garden in front of their homes. You are visiting them and you say, “My, what a pretty garden! What is that in the middle?” They say, \"Oh, that's my god. He is the god of goodness. He is the reason I am able to give such good gifts.\""},{"kind":"para","text":"Now, most of us today would probably look at this person weirdly and think, \"You don't know the true God!” Some of us might try to explain the true God to them, others might just let it go and wait for an opportunity to introduce them to the God of the Bible. Joshua says, \"If they take their idols out, avoid them like the plague. Just walk away and stay away.” In the Old Testament, this was a smart strategy. We will see that if they don't take this command seriously, they are easily prone to fall for the fake gods of everyone else around them."},{"kind":"para","text":"In the New Testament, being so staunch isn't necessary. If you are not secure enough in your faith to be around sinners, then you do not have a faith in the Lord of the Bible. If you know Christ, there is nothing that can sway you away from Him even if you are in the midst of idols. That is literally what every single letter of Paul emphasizes in the New Testament. All those churches were in the middle of idolatry, and Paul gives them advice about how to be in the middle of it, and yet refrain from being a part of it. The Israelites, however, couldn't even be in the middle of it. They wouldn't have a strong enough faith in the Lord. I argue that our faith is stronger than the saints of the Old Testament. They looked forward to a coming promise, and waited for quite a long time. We look back to the promise kept, and anticipate the next one! The only reason we may not have strong faith today is only because we don't have genuine belief in what God's Word says. So, they (and ourselves) must do what Joshua says next!"}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Moses told them to do this already."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"That thou mayest love the LORD thy God, and that thou mayest obey his voice, and that thou mayest cleave unto him: for he is thy life, and the length of thy days: that thou mayest dwell in the land which the LORD sware unto thy fathers, to Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob, to give them.","ref":"Deuteronomy 30:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"Cleave to God like your life depends on it. Because it does! Your life literally depends upon your relationship with the Lord. Joshua says, \"Cleave to Him, 'as ye have done unto this day.’\" In other words, they are currently cleaving to Him, but there could be a time when they don’t. They have to continually be conscious of their closeness to the Lord. So do we..."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These other nations were much stronger than the Israelites. By human scientific logic, there was no reason that the Israelites should have ever been victorious in any of their ventures. In fact, they should have been killed and dead in the wilderness during those forty years. They (at this time) would probably have agreed to that. It was evident that they were alive and standing in this land today only because the Lord made it happen. Even if Joshua doesn't remind them about it here, they still could not say, \"Look how amazingly mighty and numerous we are! We are stronger and greater in number than the rest of the world!” Even during this time (which is after their wilderness journey, and they have dwelt for some time in the land having children and growing in number), Egypt was a powerhouse. They were still greater in number and stronger than them. That is why they left Egypt if you don't remember. Pharaoh didn't want the Israelites to outnumber the Egyptians. Either way, God makes it clear and evident that they are only provided and protected for by the Lord personally, and nothing in themselves."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That was true even when they were taking the land. They didn't have to put much effort in. They just needed one faithful man to make a grand impact. I've said it before, but I believe that Joshua could have gone in by himself and succeeded in taking everything. God wanted to build all of their faiths, however, and so He requires them all to go in and put in the effort. Something we have already spoken about in this book."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It's not just about believing God. It's not just about holding onto God's power. It's about having a relationship and love for the Lord. That is still necessary advice today, and it's largely missing in the Christian faith. They know the power of God, and they know the law of God. They know what God has done, and what He can do. They know the judgement and wrath of God, and many people hang on to those things. But Christians should also know the love of God. They should know the mercy and sacrifice of God. They should have a deeper relationship with God than we do with our own fathers. Hebrews gives a great illustration of this kind of relationship (a study for a different time). Know God enough that we love Him. That should be the driving factor of everything in our lives."},{"kind":"para","text":"Should we go to these places? Will it show my love for God? Should I watch these things? Will it show my love for God? Should I say these words? Will it show my love for God? That is what keeps us in constant humility and correction. We must love God enough that we don't want to break His heart. Those that are not His children have no problem breaking the heart of the Lord. Those that ARE His children should not be looking to do that."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That is what it looks like to not love and cleave to the Lord. When we don't have a proper love for the Lord, we will have a sinful love for the world. It will reject God's desires for us, and we will have no problem breaking God's heart. We won't care about how God feels. We will only care about how WE feel. That is what we will see come to the Israelites. That is what we see come to the world today. You can know that people are evil, not just based on their acts (some evil people are good at hiding their acts). Rather, you can know they are evil by their lack of love for the Lord. Do they love Him enough to grow closer to Him? Or are they willing to disappoint and break the Lord’s heart?"}]}]},{"start":14,"end":16,"title":"Turn to Him","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God keeps every promise. He promises to return, and you can bet any money that He will keep that promise. That is what Joshua's final speech focuses on. He wants the people to remember that God has not forgotten them, and He has done what He said He would do, and He will continue to keep His promises even until the Savior comes. The History of the Savior is not ending with Joshua (Joshua knows this), but it sounds almost as if this is the last reminder that he will ever give to the Israelites and there won't be any knowledge of their future. He knows there's a Savior coming for them. He doesn't know what that will look like, or how that might happen, but he knows that God keeps His promises, and through Abraham those promises were evident at this present moment as Joshua is saying it. He is preparing them for the next steps of their life and walks in this new land."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They have a choice to make. That was the purpose of reading the blessings and curses on those mountaintops at the beginning of this book. They could choose the curses, or they could choose the blessings. They were each individually responsible to choose one side or the other. They, however, would do this all together because they leaned on each other rather than on the Lord. That is one of the major modern problems with the church today."},{"kind":"para","text":"We choose to lean on our pastor and leaders, or on this friend or that brother more than we lean on the Word of God. If someone fails us in the church, we all fall apart with it. Everyone seems to hurt because we lean on each other. This isn't necessarily a bad thing, but it becomes bad when we start to hurt and we don't try to mend or heal the pain. Rather, we point fingers, place blame, and try and cause pain somewhere else so we don't feel pain in ourselves."},{"kind":"para","text":"When I was younger (probably around middle school), I lived out in the desert on ten acres of property. I would go outside and chase lizards and catch them (that was the boy thing to do, and I didn't have a brother or friends close by). One lizard I was chasing ran under a Joshua tree. If you don't know what a Joshua tree is, they are like really spiky palm trees (actually that is supposedly why they called Palmdale this, because they thought the Joshua trees were palm trees). They aren't really that scary, and they can be easily snapped (especially the dry ones). When the lizard ran under there, I tried to scare it by kicking around the bottom of the tree. Well, I guess I kicked too hard, because one of those spikes went straight into my shin. After a small complaint of pain, I limped into the house and sat down. My mom rolled up my pant leg and saw that the spike was still buried in my shin. She had to get tweezers and rip it out. As a small boy (probably about eleven or twelve) I was not thrilled about the prospect. I was crying and complaining the entire time she was trying to get me to sit still so she can yank the spike out. Eventually she caused pain on my arm (with a pinch I'm sure), and yanked out the spike at the same time. While I was focused on the pain in my arm, I didn't feel the spike come out. (The spike was way about 3/4 inches long, but who’s counting.)"},{"kind":"para","text":"That is what it's like in the church today. They hurt another spot so that they don't have to feel the pain already occurring from the damage that's been done. If Satan can hurt only ONE area of the church, he is able to get the rest of the body hurting. It is up to us how to heal and mend that area, however. We have to choose to either lean on the rest of the body, and help that part heal, or we will inflict pain everywhere else so we don't have to address the hurt in that member… The Israelites were tasked with this same choice. They would have to make a conscious decision."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Yes, this is a continuous reminder to them (and rightfully so, because it's an evident problem). I want us to look at one specific work in this verse, though. He says, \"All this stuff will come upon you and you will perish (that is \"go astray\" or \"be destroyed\") quickly (that is \"with speed”)! It will happen fast, and there will be no way to reverse it in the middle of the process. We will find that to be true throughout the story of the Israelites. They fail to follow the Lord for long periods of time, and they think they are fine without Him, and suddenly the Lord will do something to get their attention, and they will panic and do things that they have enjoyed doing (thinking it will somehow relieve the pain) and everything just falls apart quickly. That is a lesson for all of us to learn."},{"kind":"para","text":"God is not wanting you to perish \"quickly\" so He gives you chances to turn and follow Him. However, if we don't take those chances, He will eventually give you over to your own devices, and things start to fall apart quite quickly. He still gives us chances to turn to Him, though! We will just have to go through some turbulence to get there…"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":16}]},
    "24": {"chapter":24,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 24"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, there are a few reminders that Joshua wants to leave with his people. He is very close to death, and he knows that his end is on the horizon. He wants to be sure that these people have a proper head on their shoulders. He reminds them about how important it is to love God, and constantly seek Him out and turn to Him if there is every a falling away. It's a lovely chapter to this book, and this chapter is just as lovely."},{"kind":"para","text":"This chapter is actually a bit bitter-sweet. It certainly has plenty of sweetness to make you feel confident in God's people after the passing of Joshua, but we also know that in the very next book (the very next part of Israels history) things don't go well. It's almost as if this chapter doesn't even exist. Thankfully it's written down and recorded, and it gives us clarity and assurance that all these people were not just left with any final goodbyes."},{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua pulls the people back together, one last time, to give them some instructions, and remind them of their history, and the importance of where they are standing today. They were liable to forget God's goodness, and Joshua understood that they needed constant reminders. That's why all of this information is written down for them. It seems that these people did not like to read, however, and that is why there is a falling away so quickly."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is a great connection to our lives today is it not? I think those that study scripture frequently have a much better head upon their shoulders than those that don't. Some people just simply don't like to read, and I understand that. These people didn't like to either. There's dangers in not diving into God's Word, and that danger is found throughout the rest of the Old Testament. We will see that Jesus uses God's Word as a means of combating false ideologies and a way to show closeness and commitment to the Lord. We should follow that same logic if we want to remain close to God, and remember His Word and power."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":13,"title":"A Providential Recap","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is the same thing that Moses did before his death. He gathered all the people before entering the land and gave a recount of the events that brought them to this point. Moses gave them a recap from their exiting of Egypt to the point of standing outside the land. Joshua will go a little further back than that. I think it's sufficient that he starts further back because it will give them a much clearer picture as to why the land was promised to them, how they got to this point, and the importance of tracing God's presence through this entire chapter of their lives. The purposes are the same in both cases, and Joshua wants to prepare these people for their future."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua brings them back to before the flood. He says that they had \"fathers\" who dwelt on that side and served false gods. That is a very big problem in the Godly lineage. That is still a very big problem today. We may not serve false gods like these (at least not all of us, unless you are a cultist or a religionist), but we certainly serve false gods in our possession. A false god is anything we might put value in over everything else. It could be money, a job, a situation, a family member, a church leader, or a political leader. People trust failing things all the time. Joshua reminds them that their ancestors worshipped false gods. That includes Abraham."},{"kind":"para","text":"He says that Abraham's father (Terah) served the wrong god. Somehow, the Lord got ahold of Abraham and brought him out of that idolatry (though it was certainly a work to completely drive out that mindset throughout his life). God calls Abraham away from his family, probably because they were not worshipping the true God. There was a lot of confusion in that time, but no one could deny the existence of the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"Noah was a very prominent spearhead for the lineage of faith, and it seems his children would follow those footsteps (obviously a bit loosely). When it gets to Abraham, all the languages are in disarray, and they are forced to comply with those they can actually talk to. Some of these people fall away and start serving other gods, and Abraham’s family is one of them. Of course, Abraham comes through the line Shem, who has the promise from Noah about the land covenant. So it's Abraham that the Lord chooses, and He chooses him because He knows what Abraham's faith leads to."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"So we know this lineage account pretty accurately. They are heavily listed and emphasized throughout Genesis. I won't spend a great deal of time speaking about these accounts because it's better suited for reading through Genesis. God was working in the lives of both the chosen lineage and the departed lineage. We see this with Isaac and Ishmael, and we see it with Jacob and Esau. There were promises given to these lineages only because of Abraham. God chose the man Abraham and made promises to him, and God kept those promises. The Biblical lineage account (however) will be through the line of Jacob (who is Israel)."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We pick up with the events from Exodus to Deuteronomy now. These accounts all reveal God's protection. They may not have seen His protection (or sometimes even cared about it), but God was always there to lead them, guide them, and use them. He even intervenes with Balaam’s trickery and provides blessing through a wicked man who tried to end them before they even got started."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we are picking up with the events that we have studied through this whole book of Joshua. God gave them this land. He says, \"I sent the hornet before you, which drove them out from before you…\" You might read that and say, \"What hornet? What is God talking about here?\""},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And I will send hornets before thee, which shall drive out the Hivite, the Canaanite, and the Hittite, from before thee.","ref":"Exodus 23:28"},{"kind":"para","text":"God says He is sending hornets before them. Whether that was literal hornets, or a symbol of fear, I will not argue. It's obvious that God did something, and it made these people weak. What really makes me laugh is the \"...I have given you a land for which ye did not labour…\" We just read about the labor that they had to go through to take this land. God means this in a twofold way."},{"kind":"para","text":"One, they did not grow this land or plant this land. There was no labor they had to build this land up. Two, they did not have to put too much effort into their battle. The Lord fought for them and with them. We see one specific time through this book where God does a very serious miracle (extending the day, and dropping huge hailstones to kill their enemies), but that is the only time we see the Lord work in that capacity. I believe it's because that was the only day that they actually followed the Lord in genuine faith. They could have done that every time (I believe) if they leaned on God wholeheartedly. The Lord would have literally taken care of their battle had they just sought Him. They didn't have the strongest faith, and I believe they relied heavily on their own strength and personal venture. We know this to be true because in the very next book they turn from the Lord over and over again. They have a hard time leaning on the unseen. As do we..."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":15,"title":"Choose Your God","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now, I don't think they had their old gods with them. I believe they left that at Mount Sinai with their father's corpses. They learned the hard way what following idols led them to. Joshua wants to make sure that those idols stay there. They don't have to physically have the idols to worship them either, might I add. They can worship them in their hearts and minds just as easily. They can give glory to the wrong things."},{"kind":"para","text":"When I was growing up, I used to love watching Sabrina the Teenage Witch. It was funny, and a little awkward, yet somehow relatable. I didn't watch it religiously, but if it was on, I wouldn't change the channel. In recent years, there has come out a new Sabrina story on Netflix. I was kind of excited to see this character come back to life. Boy was it hard to watch. As a Christian, we want to give all the glory to God, and so when something good happens, we say \"Praise the Lord!\" That is who praise and glory belong to. In this new show, when something good happened, they would say, \"Praise Satan.\" It is extremely eerie. It makes you grind your teeth. There was no Satan statue that they worshipped; they could just give the praise to Satan with their mouths, minds, and hearts."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Israelites were fully capable of falling for the same fake gods that their fathers fell for. Even going back to the gods from before the flood. God has proven Himself faithful to these people, and now these people have more than enough reason to prove themselves faithful to Him. Except we all know they won't..."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The sad reality is, we all have the God-given freedom to reject the Lord and serve fake gods. I wish it were not so, but it's true. God has allowed us the ability to reject Him. And more sad than that, people intentionally do! They want to serve fake gods, even though they have seen the Lord work and do things that protected them and got them through some terrible turmoil. Let's put this into a more relatable venture today."},{"kind":"para","text":"We could say something like \"If the God of the Bible seems evil for you to serve, then make it clear who it is that you are going to serve. You will either serve the gods that your family and traditions have passed down, or the new gods that this world is trying to tie into our culture…\" I have a lot of family that serve their parents’ gods. Their faith is not their own. Only those with Christ can say, \"as for me and my house, we will serve the Lord.” Joshua knows what he would be giving up if he doesn't sell out for the Lord. He has seen the victory, and has been the forefront of God's calling and moving since before they even got into the land. Joshua could not deny God's providence and loving kindness to him and his people. There was nothing in Joshua that wanted to serve any other gods. Simply because he had Christ as His guide."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":18,"title":"Don't Forsake God","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"\"God forbid that we should forsake the Lord, to serve other gods…\" Yes, and God does forbid it. Yet they (and many today) reject the forbidding of the Lord to fill our lives with meaningless fool’s gold. We think that these other things have more value, and we end up feeling so empty because we do not have the Lord. Show me a man that has everything in this world without the Lord, and I will show you a man that is more full because of the Lord."}]}]},{"start":19,"end":28,"title":"A Second Promise","entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a controversial topic. He says, \"You can't serve God.” Why can't they serve God? Well, because He is holy, jealous, and serious about His law. You say, “Well, then the Israelites (and all of humanity) are doomed right from the start. God is unreachable. If He doesn't forgive sins, then we are all destined for eternal torment.” Joshua illustrates what he means in the next verse..."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God has done so much good for these people. It would be unwise for them to forsake Him and serve other Gods. If they choose to go down that route and completely reject the Lord altogether, then they will have to face God's judgement for it. God will not allow sin and wickedness to run rampant. I know it might seem that is all He is doing today, but He is also working on quite a few things for that purpose. Firstly, He is working on His remaining children in this world. There is still a chance for the lost to come to salvation today. That door has not closed. Second, He is working on His Son's second coming. Eventually, the world will be bad enough that the Lord will have to return and put a stop to it. Evil will not win in the end. It might look like it will, but the Lord will come before it does. He has the final say, and He will say it finally."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It's written here. This should be something they pick up, highlight, underline, circle, asterisk, repeat, or whatever they need to do so they can remember that they promised this. They are giving clarity that they will not turn to false gods. Except we have the rest of the Bible. We know what happens to them. They do not stick to their word here..."}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Yes, they are their own witnesses. Perhaps they do a well-enough job. I'll say that this generation who is answering Joshua might have meant what they said about serving God alone. The next generation will not know about this promise, however, and so they turn rather quickly away from the Lord. Maybe these families were so concentrated on their own relationship with the Lord that they didn't take much time to pass down that knowledge to the next generation. Or maybe the next generation's prosperity simply makes them forget because they don't know the kind of lengths their parents had to go through to get them here. I don't really know, but I know that they won't hold to this promise for very long..."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Take the false gods out of your heart and mind. They will do you no good to follow them. They will lead you astray and leave you more empty than when you arrived. Don't let them be a thing in your life anymore, because God can give you what is needed. I think there are a lot of people who put Christ on their shelf. He is up there next to their traditions and religion. They say, \"Yes, Christ is important, He is a very valuable asset to my faith.\" Yet, if it came down to it, they would give Christ up in a heartbeat to follow their family’s tradition, or their church’s religion. They would have no problem taking Jesus off the shelf if it interfered with what they grew up believing. That is not how you can have the Lord, however. You must take everything else off the shelf and only put Jesus up there. He is the only thing that has value in this life, and if you put anything else on the shelf, it must be less valuable than He is. If it interferes with Christ, take it off the shelf of your heart and throw it away."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Twice now they have made this promise. Joshua gives them a physical representation of this promise as well. It is written down next to the writings of Moses, and they could easily open it up and see their signatures. They made the promise twice, and it would be well worth their lives and time to keep to that promise."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The stone will remember their promise better than the people will. That's a twisted reality, isn't it? Abraham was a man known for setting up little altars wherever he went. He set up testimonies as a remembrance of the Lord's providence and guidance to him (as well as the Lord's promises in those lands). Those stones remembered a lot better than the lineages that followed Abraham. This stone (before the people of this day) will remember these promises a lot better than the children following them. I don't think there is a single time when a child in Israel brings these stones back up again. They forget all about it..."}]}]},{"start":29,"end":31,"title":"The Death of Joshua","entries":[{"start":31,"end":31,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Joshua had a full and busy life. He served his course with triumph and boldness. He was a great example for the Israelites to lean on. I find it interesting that Moses will be so well remembered, better than Joshua. If you were to ask me (before I was a Christian) if I knew who Moses was, I would be able to answer you. If you asked me if I knew who Joshua was, I'd have no clue except to say, \"Oh, that's my nephew’s name!”"},{"kind":"para","text":"Moses had some major flaws in his faith walk. He had a bit of an anger problem, and he was consistently turning away from God's direction (as was the rest of Israel). Joshua didn't have these same problems. The Israelites will know the story of Exodus very well, but they seem to forget the story of Joshua and the promised land. This is a book that kind of just gets buried and hidden, yet it's what sets the course for the history of Israel. The families that say they will serve the Lord, keep to their end of the deal. They were not the problem. It's the generation following them that becomes a problem."},{"kind":"para","text":"If we learn anything from this book of the Bible (leading into the book of Judges), it should be that we are in need to prepare the next generation to continue the service you leave this world from. The next generation needs trainers. They need teachers and guides. That is the function of the church. The Israelites didn't have that ability in those days, but they were still strongly commanded to TEACH scripture to their children."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And thou shalt teach them ordinances and laws, and shalt shew them the way wherein they must walk, and the work that they must do.","ref":"Exodus 18:20"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And thou shalt teach them diligently unto thy children, and shalt talk of them when thou sittest in thine house, and when thou walkest by the way, and when thou liest down, and when thou risest up. And thou shalt bind them for a sign upon thine hand, and they shall be as frontlets between thine eyes. And thou shalt write them upon the posts of thy house, and on thy gates.","ref":"Deuteronomy 6:7-9"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"This book of the law shall not depart out of thy mouth; but thou shalt meditate therein day and night, that thou mayest observe to do according to all that is written therein: for then thou shalt make thy way prosperous, and then thou shalt have good success.","ref":"Joshua 1:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"They must forget all of this though, because it doesn't seem like the next generation learns much..."}]}]},{"start":32,"end":33,"title":"Keeping Promises","entries":[{"start":33,"end":33,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This chapter (and book) ends off on a very positive note. If we stopped reading right here, we would be sufficient to believe that the Israelites lived prosperous, happy lives following the Lord. This is a picture of our promised land, mind you. We have a promise in eternity that we are going to be in. It will have an ending that suffices, and there will be no death (no need for burying bones), and no pain, and we will not have the choice to serve other gods because the one true God will be in our presence and grasp. Of course, that's saved for eternity. That kind of ending will not come to this Earth. The Earth is littered with sin, and that is the great problem with having to be here. We must prepare people for a better place away from this sin-cursed world, and into a land that flourishes."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":31},{"start":32,"end":33}]},
  },
};
